window.EXAM_QUESTIONS = [{"id": 4, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 45-year-old man with severe epigastric pain after binge drinking has a markedly raised serum lipase. CT shows peripancreatic stranding and scattered chalky white foci within the omental fat. He is afebrile and haemodynamically stable at triage. Which mechanism produces these chalky foci?", "options": {"A": "Metastatic calcification driven by hypercalcaemia from parathyroid hormone excess", "B": "Dystrophic deposition of calcium phosphate within previously caseating granulomatous omental nodules", "C": "Coagulative necrosis of omental fat from ischaemia after mesenteric arterial occlusion", "D": "Lipase release hydrolyses triglycerides to fatty acids that combine with calcium to form soaps"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lipase release hydrolyses triglycerides to fatty acids that combine with calcium to form soaps): Enzymatic fat necrosis follows release of pancreatic lipases into the peritoneal fat, which splits triglycerides into free fatty acids. These bind calcium in a process called saponification, producing the chalky white deposits. Dystrophic calcification of granulomas and hypercalcaemic deposition need different settings, and ischaemia gives coagulative necrosis."}, {"id": 13, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 63-year-old man has nephrotic range proteinuria, macroglossia and periorbital bruising. Bone marrow examination shows clonal plasma cells. A renal biopsy stains with Congo red. Serum immunofixation shows a monoclonal lambda band and he has pitting leg oedema. Which protein forms the deposits in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Beta-2 microglobulin retained because of impaired renal clearance", "B": "Immunoglobulin light chain fragments from a clonal plasma cell proliferation", "C": "Transthyretin produced by the liver in its wild type or mutant form", "D": "Serum amyloid A produced by the liver in response to chronic inflammatory disease"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Immunoglobulin light chain fragments from a clonal plasma cell proliferation): Primary AL amyloidosis is caused by clonal plasma cells producing light chains, often lambda, that deposit in kidney, heart, tongue and soft tissue. The macroglossia and periorbital purpura are characteristic. AA amyloid follows chronic inflammation, beta-2 microglobulin follows long-term dialysis, and transthyretin causes cardiac or neuropathic amyloid."}, {"id": 22, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 74-year-old woman develops watery diarrhoea and abdominal pain eight days into a course of clindamycin. CT shows circumferential colonic wall thickening with an accordion sign. Stool testing detects toxin and the white cell count is raised. What is the characteristic endoscopic and histological lesion?", "options": {"A": "Patchy mucosal infarction with submucosal haemorrhage and ghost crypts sparing the muscle", "B": "Deep linear serpiginous ulcers with cobblestoning and non-caseating granulomas in a transmural infiltrate", "C": "Continuous mucosal friability with crypt abscesses and basal plasmacytosis confined to the mucosa", "D": "Adherent yellow-white plaques of fibrin, mucus and neutrophils erupting from crypts in a volcano pattern"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Adherent yellow-white plaques of fibrin, mucus and neutrophils erupting from crypts in a volcano pattern): Clostridioides difficile toxins injure colonic epithelium and produce a pseudomembrane composed of fibrin, mucus and inflammatory cells that erupts from damaged crypts like a volcano. Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis and ischaemic colitis each have distinct histology, and antibiotic exposure with diarrhoea points to the toxin-mediated lesion."}, {"id": 23, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 40-year-old shepherd has right upper quadrant discomfort. Ultrasound shows a large cystic hepatic lesion containing internal daughter cysts with a calcified wall. Serology is positive and the eosinophil count is mildly raised. Which structural description best fits the wall of this cyst?", "options": {"A": "Single layer of cuboidal epithelium with surrounding ovarian type stroma and mucinous fluid", "B": "Inner germinal layer, middle laminated acellular layer and an outer host fibrous pericyst", "C": "Fibrous capsule lined by granulation tissue and neutrophils containing thick pus", "D": "Thick wall of necrotic hepatocytes and fibrin surrounding old haemorrhage"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Inner germinal layer, middle laminated acellular layer and an outer host fibrous pericyst): Echinococcus granulosus forms a unilocular cyst with a nucleated germinal layer, an acellular laminated membrane and an outer fibrous pericyst contributed by the host. Brood capsules and daughter cysts are typical, and rupture can cause anaphylaxis. A mucinous cystic neoplasm has epithelium and ovarian type stroma, and an abscess contains pus."}, {"id": 26, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 60-year-old diabetic man has a cold, blackened toe with foul smelling discharge and crepitus on palpation. Radiograph shows gas in the soft tissues of the foot. He has a long history of peripheral neuropathy and poorly controlled glucose. Which pathological description best fits the necrosis present?", "options": {"A": "Wet gangrene, coagulative necrosis modified by liquefaction from bacteria and neutrophils", "B": "Fat necrosis from lipase release with calcium soap deposition", "C": "Caseous necrosis with granulomas containing amorphous cheesy debris", "D": "Dry gangrene, ischaemic coagulative necrosis with a sharp line of demarcation and no infection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Wet gangrene, coagulative necrosis modified by liquefaction from bacteria and neutrophils): Gangrene is clinical coagulative necrosis of a limb, usually from ischaemia. When bacterial infection is superimposed, liquefactive change is added, giving wet gangrene with foul discharge and, with gas-forming organisms, soft tissue gas. Dry gangrene stays mummified and uninfected, whereas fat and caseous necrosis arise in other settings."}, {"id": 29, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 35-year-old man with severe sepsis develops refractory hypoxaemia. CT shows bilateral dependent consolidation with ground glass change. Autopsy lung histology at one week shows pink membranes lining the alveolar walls. Blood cultures grew gram-negative bacilli. What are these membranes principally composed of?", "options": {"A": "Surfactant protein and phospholipid accumulation producing granular periodic acid Schiff positive alveolar filling", "B": "Organised granulation tissue plugs within alveolar ducts and bronchioles", "C": "Necrotic epithelial debris mixed with condensed plasma proteins leaked through damaged alveolar walls", "D": "Neutrophils and fibrin plugs within alveolar spaces without damage to the alveolar wall"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Necrotic epithelial debris mixed with condensed plasma proteins leaked through damaged alveolar walls): Diffuse alveolar damage, the lesion of ARDS, begins with endothelial and type I pneumocyte injury. Protein-rich fluid leaks into alveoli and condenses with necrotic epithelial debris to form hyaline membranes. Alveolar proteinosis, lobar pneumonia and organising pneumonia are different patterns, and plugs of granulation tissue occur later in repair."}, {"id": 32, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 29-year-old migrant from rural Latin America has new generalised seizures. CT shows several punctate calcifications in the brain parenchyma and one small cyst with an eccentric dot. Cerebrospinal fluid is acellular. What is the pathological explanation for the punctate calcifications?", "options": {"A": "Viable larval cysts producing mineralised walls to evade host response", "B": "Dead larval cysts that have degenerated and undergone dystrophic calcification", "C": "Healed tuberculomas with caseous centres replaced by fibrosis and calcium", "D": "Metastatic deposition due to chronic hypercalcaemia from granulomatous disease elsewhere"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Dead larval cysts that have degenerated and undergone dystrophic calcification): Taenia solium larvae form cysts in the brain that eventually die and degenerate, provoking a granulomatous reaction and then dystrophic calcification. The eccentric dot is the scolex within a viable or degenerating cyst. Calcification in normal calcium states reflects previous damage, not hypercalcaemia, and a living cyst is not calcified."}, {"id": 33, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 66-year-old man with recent diverticulitis has fever and a 5 cm hepatic lesion on CT, with a thick enhancing rim and a hypodense centre. Blood cultures grew mixed enteric organisms. Which histological description best fits the wall of this lesion at several weeks?", "options": {"A": "Granulation tissue and fibrosis encircling a core of liquefactive necrosis and neutrophils", "B": "Zonal hepatocyte necrosis with ballooned cells, steatosis and Mallory hyaline in perivenular regions", "C": "Cuboidal biliary epithelium lining a unilocular cyst with clear serous fluid", "D": "Epithelioid granulomas with central caseation lined by Langhans giant cells and lymphocytes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Granulation tissue and fibrosis encircling a core of liquefactive necrosis and neutrophils): An abscess is a localised collection of pus consisting of liquefactive necrosis and neutrophils. Over weeks the host walls it off with granulation tissue and fibrous tissue, giving the enhancing rim on CT. Granulomas, simple biliary cysts and alcoholic hepatitis do not show a pyogenic core with a vascular granulation wall."}, {"id": 36, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 58-year-old man collapses and dies three days after a hip replacement. At autopsy a long, firm, dry cast with a corrugated surface lies in the pulmonary trunk. Which microscopic finding best shows this material formed in flowing blood before death rather than after it?", "options": {"A": "Alternating bands of platelet-fibrin and red cells known as lines of Zahn", "B": "Homogeneous eosinophilic fibrin with abundant neutrophils and central liquefactive necrosis in the core", "C": "A soft, uniformly dark red gelatinous mass moulded to the vessel lumen and unattached", "D": "Loosely packed red cells and serum with no fibrin meshwork or platelet component"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Alternating bands of platelet-fibrin and red cells known as lines of Zahn): The laminated bands are produced by repeated deposition of platelet-fibrin layers and trapped red cells under flow, and they mark an antemortem thrombus. A postmortem clot is soft, gelatinous and uniformly dark, so it is the tempting alternative. Septic emboli and simple stasis clot lack the alternating architecture. Calf deep vein thrombus is the usual source after orthopaedic surgery."}, {"id": 37, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 27-year-old woman on the oral contraceptive pill develops an unprovoked left iliofemoral deep vein thrombosis, and her mother had a similar event at 35. Thrombophilia screening is planned. Which inherited abnormality is the most common cause of this pattern in people of European ancestry?", "options": {"A": "A deficiency of antithrombin reducing inhibition of thrombin and factor Xa", "B": "A mutation of the prothrombin gene increasing circulating prothrombin concentration", "C": "A point mutation making activated factor V resistant to cleavage by protein C", "D": "A deficiency of protein S reducing activated protein C cofactor activity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (A point mutation making activated factor V resistant to cleavage by protein C): Factor V Leiden is the commonest inherited thrombophilia, and the altered factor V is not inactivated normally by activated protein C. This leaves the Virchow hypercoagulable limb unchecked, typically causing venous rather than arterial events. Antithrombin, protein S and prothrombin variants are genuine thrombophilias but are less frequent. Hormonal therapy adds a further acquired risk."}, {"id": 38, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 34-year-old woman with a recent calf vein thrombosis presents with sudden left hemiparesis. CT shows a wedge-shaped cortical hypodensity in the right middle cerebral territory, and the chest is clear. Echocardiography with bubble study shows early left heart opacification. Which mechanism best explains the cerebral lesion?", "options": {"A": "Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation causing left atrial appendage thrombus and subsequent cardioembolism", "B": "Thrombus formed on a calcified aortic valve and lodged in the middle cerebral artery", "C": "Atheroembolic cholesterol crystals released from an ulcerated carotid plaque into distal branches", "D": "Venous thrombus crossing a patent foramen ovale into the systemic arterial circulation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Venous thrombus crossing a patent foramen ovale into the systemic arterial circulation): The combination of a venous thrombus, cerebral infarction and early bubbles in the left heart indicates a right to left shunt. A patent foramen ovale lets venous emboli bypass the pulmonary filter and cause arterial infarcts. Valvular, carotid atheroembolic and atrial fibrillation sources are all genuine causes of stroke but do not explain a venous origin or shunt."}, {"id": 42, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 69-year-old man who had a right middle cerebral artery stroke is re-imaged six months later. CT shows a well-defined, cerebrospinal-fluid-density cavity in the territory with ex vacuo enlargement of the adjacent ventricle. Which pathological sequence best explains the end result?", "options": {"A": "Caseation with encapsulation by a rim of fibroblasts and Langerhans giant cells", "B": "Liquefactive necrosis, macrophage clearance and astrocytic gliosis around a cystic space", "C": "Haemorrhagic transformation with permanent haemosiderin tattoo and no cavity formation", "D": "Coagulative necrosis followed by fibrous scar formation replacing the lost neurons"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Liquefactive necrosis, macrophage clearance and astrocytic gliosis around a cystic space): Brain tissue is rich in lipid and lysosomal enzymes and has no collagenous stroma, so infarcts liquefy and are removed by microglia and macrophages. The reactive astrocytes form a glial scar rather than collagen, leaving a fluid-filled cavity. Fibrous scarring is the heart and kidney outcome, and caseation is not a consequence of ischaemia."}, {"id": 44, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "Two patients have bilateral airspace opacities on chest radiograph. Patient one has an enlarged heart, Kerley B lines and pleural effusions. Patient two, after severe pancreatitis, has normal heart size and a normal wedge pressure. Which pathological feature best identifies the process in the second patient?", "options": {"A": "Alveolar spaces filled with pink transudate and haemosiderin-laden macrophages from raised hydrostatic pressure", "B": "Bronchiolar neutrophil exudate with patchy consolidation limited to dependent segments", "C": "Intra-alveolar proteinaceous material with foamy macrophages from surfactant clearance failure", "D": "Hyaline membranes lining alveolar walls from diffuse alveolar damage with increased permeability"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hyaline membranes lining alveolar walls from diffuse alveolar damage with increased permeability): Non-cardiogenic oedema in acute respiratory distress syndrome reflects injury to the alveolar capillary barrier, with protein-rich fluid and fibrin forming hyaline membranes in diffuse alveolar damage. Heart failure oedema is a low protein transudate with haemosiderin macrophages. Alveolar proteinosis and bronchopneumonia give different histology and a patchy distribution."}, {"id": 45, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 45-year-old woman remains hypotensive for several hours after a ruptured ectopic pregnancy and receives intravenous fluids and blood products. Urine output then falls, and ultrasound shows normal-sized, echogenic kidneys without hydronephrosis. Serial serum creatinine results are plotted. Which pathological lesion most likely underlies the renal failure?", "options": {"A": "Patchy tubular epithelial necrosis with sloughed cells and casts within the proximal tubules", "B": "Crescentic proliferation in Bowman space with rupture of the glomerular basement membrane", "C": "Interstitial infiltration by eosinophils and lymphocytes with tubulitis after drug exposure", "D": "Fibrin thrombi throughout glomerular capillaries with necrosis of the entire cortex"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Patchy tubular epithelial necrosis with sloughed cells and casts within the proximal tubules): Prolonged hypoperfusion causes ischaemic acute tubular injury, with patchy necrosis of proximal tubular cells and casts. Kidneys appear normal or enlarged and echogenic without obstruction. Diffuse cortical necrosis requires abrupt, severe ischaemia, usually in obstetric catastrophes with disseminated coagulation. Crescentic and interstitial lesions do not fit this history.", "image": "question_images/q_45.png"}, {"id": 50, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 9-year-old boy from Papua New Guinea has a rapidly enlarging jaw mass. CT shows an expansile lesion with displaced teeth, and biopsy shows sheets of medium-sized cells with scattered macrophages containing apoptotic debris. Which genetic event is most characteristic of this tumour?", "options": {"A": "Translocation of BCL2 into the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus with antiapoptotic effect", "B": "Translocation placing the MYC gene beside an immunoglobulin heavy chain locus", "C": "Translocation fusing BCR with ABL1 to produce a constitutively active tyrosine kinase", "D": "Amplification of MYCN on chromosome 2 in neural crest derived cells"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Translocation placing the MYC gene beside an immunoglobulin heavy chain locus): Burkitt lymphoma, including the endemic jaw presentation, shows t(8;14) in most cases, which juxtaposes MYC with the heavy chain locus and drives proliferation. The starry sky appearance arises from macrophages ingesting apoptotic cells. BCR-ABL1, MYCN amplification and BCL2 translocation define chronic myeloid leukaemia, neuroblastoma and follicular lymphoma."}, {"id": 51, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 45-year-old man presents with fatigue, early satiety and marked splenomegaly on examination. His full blood count and bone marrow cytogenetic result are shown in the table. Which molecular consequence of this chromosomal change is responsible for the disease in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Amplified receptor tyrosine kinase signalling owing to multiple gene copies", "B": "Overexpression of cyclin D1 from placement beneath an immunoglobulin enhancer", "C": "Constitutive tyrosine kinase activity from fusion of two genes", "D": "Loss of function of a tumour suppressor caused by a deletion on one chromosome arm"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Constitutive tyrosine kinase activity from fusion of two genes): The Philadelphia chromosome arises from the reciprocal translocation t(9;22), creating BCR-ABL1, whose protein has unregulated tyrosine kinase activity that drives myeloid proliferation. This is the target of tyrosine kinase inhibitors. Cyclin D1 overexpression follows t(11;14) in mantle cell lymphoma, and gene amplification is a separate oncogenic mechanism.", "image": "question_images/q_51.png"}, {"id": 52, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 2-year-old with leukocoria has bilateral intraocular masses with calcification on CT. Her father had a childhood eye tumour removed. Years later she develops a distal femoral osteosarcoma. Which explanation best accounts for the bilateral disease and the second cancer?", "options": {"A": "Somatic mutation of both RB1 alleles in a single retinal cell during early childhood", "B": "Germline mutation of a mismatch repair gene causing microsatellite instability", "C": "Germline RB1 mutation with loss of the second allele occurring in many somatic cells", "D": "Amplification of MYC in retinal cells under the influence of ultraviolet exposure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Germline RB1 mutation with loss of the second allele occurring in many somatic cells): Hereditary retinoblastoma follows Knudson's two-hit model: one RB1 allele is mutated in the germline and the second is lost somatically, so multiple retinal cells are at risk and tumours are bilateral. Carriers also have raised risk of osteosarcoma. Sporadic retinoblastoma needs two somatic hits in one cell and is usually unilateral."}, {"id": 58, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 66-year-old man has a caecal tumour resected, and the surgical pathology report extract is shown. The oncologist asks which element of this report determines prognosis most strongly and is conveyed by the TNM system rather than by the histological grade. Which element is it?", "options": {"A": "The extent of local invasion and nodal involvement at the time of diagnosis", "B": "The presence of mucin production within the tumour cells", "C": "The degree of nuclear pleomorphism and gland formation within the tumour", "D": "The number of mitotic figures counted per ten high-power fields"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The extent of local invasion and nodal involvement at the time of diagnosis): Grading describes how closely tumour cells resemble normal tissue, using differentiation, pleomorphism and mitotic activity. Staging describes anatomical extent, namely the primary tumour size and depth, nodal involvement and distant metastasis. For most solid tumours, including colon cancer, stage is the stronger predictor of outcome and guides treatment.", "image": "question_images/q_58.png"}, {"id": 63, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 15-year-old girl with short stature and primary amenorrhoea has an antenatal history of nuchal cystic hygroma. MRI shows a bicuspid aortic valve, aortic coarctation, a horseshoe kidney and streak ovaries. Her intelligence is normal. Which karyotype is most likely?", "options": {"A": "47,XXX, with a normal phenotype and mild learning difficulty", "B": "45,X, with absence of one X chromosome in all or most cells", "C": "47,XXY, with an additional X chromosome in a male phenotype", "D": "46,XY with androgen receptor insensitivity and intra-abdominal testes"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (45,X, with absence of one X chromosome in all or most cells): Turner syndrome results from monosomy X, causing gonadal dysgenesis, short stature and left-sided cardiovascular lesions such as coarctation and bicuspid aortic valve. Renal anomalies including horseshoe kidney are common. Klinefelter syndrome affects males, triple X has a mild phenotype, and androgen insensitivity presents with a female phenotype but a 46,XY karyotype."}, {"id": 67, "domain": "general_pathology", "question": "A 33-year-old has bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy and perilymphatic nodules on CT, with a normal chest examination and raised serum ACE. Lymph node biopsy shows compact epithelioid granulomas. He has no fever or night sweats. Which histological feature best favours sarcoidosis over tuberculosis?", "options": {"A": "Granulomas arranged around a central stellate microabscess with neutrophils", "B": "Granulomas with central caseation surrounded by Langhans giant cells and lymphocytes", "C": "Palisading histiocytes around fibrinoid necrosis with mucin in the centre", "D": "Well-formed granulomas without central necrosis, often with Schaumann bodies"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Well-formed granulomas without central necrosis, often with Schaumann bodies): Sarcoid granulomas are tightly formed, non-necrotising and may contain asteroid or Schaumann bodies. Tuberculous granulomas, driven by type IV hypersensitivity to mycobacterial antigen, show central caseous necrosis. Stellate microabscesses suggest cat scratch disease, and palisading granulomas around necrobiosis occur with rheumatoid nodules. Exclusion of infection remains necessary."}, {"id": 71, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 64-year-old man dies suddenly three days after an anterior myocardial infarction. At autopsy the anterior left ventricular wall shows a central yellow-tan area with a rim of hyperaemic tissue, and the pathologist notes the changes are at their peak. Which microscopic finding is most characteristic of this stage?", "options": {"A": "Wavy fibres and marginal contraction bands without a significant inflammatory cell infiltrate", "B": "Granulation tissue with new capillaries and abundant macrophages removing necrotic myocytes", "C": "Dense neutrophilic infiltrate surrounding myocytes with coagulative necrosis and loss of nuclei", "D": "Collagen deposition with a densely fibrotic, grey-white scar and few remaining inflammatory cells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Dense neutrophilic infiltrate surrounding myocytes with coagulative necrosis and loss of nuclei): At about three days the infarct shows maximal acute inflammation: neutrophils infiltrate dead myocytes that show coagulative necrosis, and the macroscopic centre is yellow with a hyperaemic border. Granulation tissue with macrophages appears from about one week, so it is too late. Wavy fibres belong to the first hours, and dense scar needs several weeks."}, {"id": 72, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 71-year-old woman recovering from a large transmural anterior infarct collapses on day five with sudden hypotension and pulseless electrical activity. Bedside echocardiography shows a large pericardial collection with right atrial collapse. Which pathological process best explains the timing of this complication?", "options": {"A": "Autoimmune pericardial inflammation several weeks after myocardial injury leading to haemorrhagic effusion", "B": "Immediate loss of contractility from coagulative necrosis within the first hours of coronary occlusion", "C": "Thinning of old fibrous scar with progressive outward bulging of a mature ventricular aneurysm", "D": "Maximal enzymatic softening of necrotic myocardium before collagen scarring has strengthened the infarcted wall"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Maximal enzymatic softening of necrotic myocardium before collagen scarring has strengthened the infarcted wall): Free wall rupture classically occurs between about three and seven days, when inflammatory cells have digested the necrotic wall and granulation tissue has not yet provided tensile strength. The result is haemopericardium and tamponade. Early hours produce arrhythmia and pump failure rather than rupture. Dressler syndrome arises weeks later, and aneurysm involves a healed fibrous wall."}, {"id": 75, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 67-year-old man develops acute pulmonary oedema and a new loud systolic murmur four days after an inferior myocardial infarction. Echocardiography shows a flail mitral leaflet with severe regurgitation but preserved left ventricular size. Which anatomical fact best explains why this muscle ruptured?", "options": {"A": "The posteromedial papillary muscle receives blood from a single coronary artery, making it vulnerable to infarction", "B": "The chordae tendineae derive their blood supply from the right atrial branches and degenerate early", "C": "The anterolateral papillary muscle has a single end-arterial supply from the left circumflex artery alone", "D": "Both papillary muscles are supplied by the left anterior descending artery and infarct with anterior walls"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (The posteromedial papillary muscle receives blood from a single coronary artery, making it vulnerable to infarction): The posteromedial papillary muscle is supplied usually by the posterior descending artery alone, so inferior infarction readily necroses it. The anterolateral muscle has a dual supply from the left anterior descending and circumflex arteries, making it more resistant. Chordae are avascular. Rupture occurs several days after infarction, when the necrotic muscle is weakest."}, {"id": 77, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 19-year-old athlete collapses and dies during a football match. His heart is heavy, with a markedly thickened asymmetric septum, a small left ventricular cavity and a fibrous plaque on the anterior mitral leaflet. Which microscopic finding would the pathologist expect in the septal myocardium?", "options": {"A": "Lymphocytic infiltrate with focal myocyte necrosis and oedema of the interstitium", "B": "Pink amorphous interstitial deposits staining with Congo red and showing apple-green birefringence", "C": "Replacement of the myocardium by adipose tissue and fibrosis with entrapped residual myocytes", "D": "Haphazard arrangement of hypertrophied myocytes with interstitial fibrosis and enlarged, bizarre nuclei"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Haphazard arrangement of hypertrophied myocytes with interstitial fibrosis and enlarged, bizarre nuclei): Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy shows myocyte disarray, hypertrophy, bizarre nuclei and interstitial fibrosis, with asymmetric septal thickening and a mitral leaflet plaque from contact with the septum in systole. Fibrofatty replacement characterises arrhythmogenic disease. Lymphocytic infiltrate indicates myocarditis, and Congo red positivity indicates amyloid."}, {"id": 78, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 35-year-old woman has progressive heart failure with all four cardiac chambers dilated, thin walls and globally reduced systolic function on echocardiography. Coronary arteries are normal and no toxic cause is found. Her father died of heart failure at 48. Which gene is most commonly mutated in familial forms of this condition?", "options": {"A": "PRKAR1A, encoding a regulatory subunit of protein kinase A, with inactivation", "B": "PKP2, encoding the desmosomal protein plakophilin 2, with loss of function", "C": "TTN, encoding the giant sarcomeric protein titin, with truncating variants", "D": "MYH7, encoding the beta myosin heavy chain, with missense variants"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (TTN, encoding the giant sarcomeric protein titin, with truncating variants): Truncating variants in TTN account for the largest share of familial dilated cardiomyopathy. MYH7 is better known in hypertrophic disease, although it can contribute to dilated forms. PKP2 is the leading gene in arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy. PRKAR1A underlies Carney complex, which includes cardiac myxomas."}, {"id": 79, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "An 80-year-old man has heart failure with preserved ejection fraction, low voltage on electrocardiogram and symmetrically thickened ventricular walls with a small cavity on MRI. The summary of his non-invasive work-up is shown. Which pathological finding is expected on endomyocardial biopsy?", "options": {"A": "Extracellular transthyretin-derived fibrils that stain with Congo red and show apple-green birefringence", "B": "Non-caseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells and scattered patchy interstitial fibrosis", "C": "Intracellular iron deposition in myocytes shown by Perls stain with relatively preserved wall thickness", "D": "Extracellular immunoglobulin light chain deposits that stain with Congo red and show apple-green birefringence"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Extracellular transthyretin-derived fibrils that stain with Congo red and show apple-green birefringence): Wild-type transthyretin amyloid is the typical cause in elderly men with bone-tracer uptake and no paraprotein, and the fibrils are Congo red positive. AL amyloid is also Congo red positive but needs a monoclonal light chain. Haemochromatosis gives iron-laden myocytes, and sarcoidosis gives patchy granulomatous disease.", "image": "question_images/q_79.png"}, {"id": 81, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 24-year-old man has recurrent ventricular tachycardia with left bundle branch block morphology and a family history of sudden death. MRI shows a dilated right ventricle with fatty replacement of the free wall and regional aneurysms. Which genetic abnormality is most likely responsible for this condition?", "options": {"A": "Mutation in beta myosin heavy chain producing abnormal sarcomere contraction and myocyte disarray", "B": "Truncating variant of the giant sarcomere protein producing global dilatation with systolic dysfunction", "C": "Loss of function of desmosomal proteins such as plakophilin 2 impairing cell-to-cell adhesion", "D": "Abnormal accumulation of a misfolded plasma protein producing stiff, thickened ventricular walls"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Loss of function of desmosomal proteins such as plakophilin 2 impairing cell-to-cell adhesion): Arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy is mostly caused by desmosomal gene mutations, notably PKP2, so that stressed myocytes detach and are replaced by fibrofatty tissue, predominantly in the right ventricle. Sarcomere mutations cause hypertrophic disease, titin truncation causes dilatation, and amyloid causes restrictive thickening."}, {"id": 82, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 22-year-old man presents with chest pain, new heart failure and raised troponin ten days after a flu-like illness. Coronary arteries are normal. Endomyocardial biopsy shows interstitial oedema, myocyte necrosis and a mononuclear infiltrate. Which infectious agent is the most frequent cause of this picture in developed countries?", "options": {"A": "Borrelia burgdorferi, with spirochaetal invasion of the myocardium and transient atrioventricular block", "B": "Trypanosoma cruzi, with amastigote pseudocysts packed within myocytes and a chronic dilated course", "C": "Corynebacterium diphtheriae, with exotoxin-mediated myocyte injury and early conduction block", "D": "Enterovirus such as Coxsackie B, with direct myocyte injury and a secondary immune response"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Enterovirus such as Coxsackie B, with direct myocyte injury and a secondary immune response): Lymphocytic myocarditis in developed countries is most often caused by Coxsackie B and other enteroviruses. Chagas disease occurs in Latin America and shows parasites in myocytes. Diphtheria is now rare with immunisation. Lyme carditis characteristically presents with conduction block rather than heart failure."}, {"id": 86, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 28-year-old man who injects drugs presents with high fever, a new regurgitant murmur and multiple pulmonary nodules with cavitation on CT. Echocardiography shows a bulky vegetation on the tricuspid valve. Which organism is the most likely cause of this valve infection?", "options": {"A": "Enterococcus faecalis, which is usually acquired after genitourinary or bowel instrumentation", "B": "Staphylococcus aureus, a virulent organism able to infect previously normal valves", "C": "Viridans group streptococci, which usually colonise valves already damaged by prior disease", "D": "Staphylococcus epidermidis, which typically infects prosthetic material after cardiac surgery"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Staphylococcus aureus, a virulent organism able to infect previously normal valves): Right-sided endocarditis in people who inject drugs is usually due to S. aureus, which causes acute destructive disease of normal valves, and septic emboli lodge in the lungs. Viridans streptococci cause subacute disease on damaged left-sided valves. S. epidermidis suits prosthetic devices, and enterococci follow instrumentation."}, {"id": 87, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 63-year-old man with metastatic mucinous adenocarcinoma of the pancreas has a cerebral infarct. Blood cultures are negative. Autopsy shows multiple small, bland, firmly attached vegetations along the closure lines of the mitral and aortic valves, with no valve destruction. Which mechanism best explains the lesions?", "options": {"A": "Bacterial colonisation of damaged valve tissue producing large friable vegetations with cuspal destruction", "B": "Carcinoid plaque formation from serotonin-driven fibroblast proliferation affecting the right-sided valves", "C": "Circulating immune complexes depositing on both surfaces of the valve in a systemic autoimmune disease", "D": "Hypercoagulable state with deposition of sterile platelet and fibrin thrombi on undamaged valve leaflets"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hypercoagulable state with deposition of sterile platelet and fibrin thrombi on undamaged valve leaflets): Non-bacterial thrombotic endocarditis produces small sterile vegetations along the line of closure on normal valves, typically in cancer-associated hypercoagulability, and often embolises. Libman-Sacks lesions are verrucous, on both leaflet surfaces, in lupus. Infective vegetations are larger and destructive. Carcinoid affects mostly right-sided valves."}, {"id": 88, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 34-year-old woman with a malar rash, arthritis and proteinuria has a new mitral regurgitant murmur. Echocardiography shows small sterile warty lesions on both the atrial and ventricular surfaces of the mitral leaflets, and blood cultures are negative. Which underlying pathology explains these vegetations?", "options": {"A": "Fibrous thickening of the endocardium by serotonin-induced fibroblast proliferation on one valve surface", "B": "Immune complex deposition with fibrinoid necrosis and organising granulation tissue beneath verrucous deposits", "C": "Streptococcal infection of valve leaflets previously scarred by rheumatic fever with microbial colonies", "D": "Hypercoagulable thrombus formation along the closure line on previously normal valve leaflets"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Immune complex deposition with fibrinoid necrosis and organising granulation tissue beneath verrucous deposits): Libman-Sacks endocarditis in systemic lupus erythematosus shows verrucous vegetations on one or both leaflet surfaces, with fibrinoid necrosis and immune complexes. Marantic lesions sit along the closure line and lack valve inflammation. Infective vegetations contain organisms, and carcinoid plaques involve right-sided surfaces through serotonin effects."}, {"id": 99, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 38-year-old woman presents with a stroke. Echocardiography shows a 15 mm defect at the fossa ovalis with a left-to-right shunt and dilated right heart chambers. Doppler of the leg veins shows an acute deep vein thrombosis. Which mechanism most likely explains her stroke?", "options": {"A": "Embolisation of a left atrial myxoma fragment through the mitral valve into the cerebral arteries", "B": "Reversal of an arterial duct shunt delivering deoxygenated blood to the lower body", "C": "Septic embolisation from tricuspid vegetations passing into the pulmonary arteries and then the brain", "D": "Paradoxical embolism of venous thrombus across the septal defect into the systemic arterial circulation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Paradoxical embolism of venous thrombus across the septal defect into the systemic arterial circulation): Secundum atrial septal defects arise at the fossa ovalis, and transient right-to-left flow, for example with straining, lets venous thrombi cross to the arterial side. Myxoma is not suggested by the venous thrombosis. Right-sided septic emboli travel to the lungs, and a ductal shunt reversal produces differential cyanosis, not stroke."}, {"id": 105, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 71 year old man who smoked heavily for fifty years has a pulsatile mass found on routine examination. Cross sectional imaging shows a fusiform infrarenal aortic dilatation with a thick layer of mural thrombus and mural calcification. Which pathological process most directly underlies the weakening of the aortic wall in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Obliterative endarteritis of the vasa vasorum causing ischaemic scarring of the outer media", "B": "Atherosclerotic intimal damage with medial elastin degradation and smooth muscle loss", "C": "Granulomatous inflammation with multinucleated giant cells fragmenting the internal elastic lamina", "D": "Cystic medial degeneration caused by a fibrillin 1 defect with excess transforming growth factor beta signalling"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Atherosclerotic intimal damage with medial elastin degradation and smooth muscle loss): Abdominal aortic aneurysms arise from advanced atherosclerosis, where intimal plaque and chronic inflammation lead to proteolytic loss of medial elastin and smooth muscle and a thinned, weak wall. Cystic medial degeneration characterises thoracic aneurysms in Marfan syndrome. Vasa vasorum endarteritis is syphilitic, and giant cell inflammation is a vasculitis."}, {"id": 108, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 63 year old hypertensive man has abrupt tearing chest pain radiating to the back. Imaging shows a flap separating a true from a false lumen beginning just distal to the left subclavian artery. Which statement best describes the basic pathological event that creates this condition?", "options": {"A": "An intimal tear allows blood to enter the media and track longitudinally along it", "B": "Transmural granulomatous inflammation causes full thickness wall rupture into the pleural cavity", "C": "Rupture of a vulnerable plaque with thrombus formation limited to the subintimal layer", "D": "Embolic occlusion of the vasa vasorum causes necrosis of the inner media with secondary dilatation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An intimal tear allows blood to enter the media and track longitudinally along it): Aortic dissection begins with an intimal tear, typically in a hypertensive aorta with medial degeneration, allowing blood to split the media longitudinally and create a false channel. The flap beyond the left subclavian origin makes this a Stanford type B dissection. Plaque rupture, vasculitis and vasa vasorum embolism do not produce a medial haematoma tracking along the aorta."}, {"id": 111, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 27 year old woman presents with fatigue, arm claudication and absent radial pulses, with a markedly lower blood pressure in the left arm than the right. Imaging shows long segment concentric wall thickening of the aortic arch branches with luminal narrowing. Which pathological description best fits the vessel wall in the active phase?", "options": {"A": "Granulomatous panarteritis with giant cells, leading to intimal fibrosis and luminal stenosis", "B": "Thrombus with inflammatory infiltrate confined to small distal extremity arteries and veins", "C": "Pauci immune necrotising inflammation with neutrophils and nuclear debris within small vessels", "D": "Fibrinoid necrosis with neutrophils across medium sized arteries sparing pulmonary vessels"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Granulomatous panarteritis with giant cells, leading to intimal fibrosis and luminal stenosis): Takayasu arteritis is a granulomatous large vessel vasculitis centred on the aorta and its major branches, which in a young woman leads to fibrous thickening, stenosis and loss of pulses. Fibrinoid necrosis of medium arteries suggests polyarteritis nodosa. Pauci immune small vessel disease describes microscopic polyangiitis, and distal vessel thrombosis in a smoker suggests thromboangiitis obliterans."}, {"id": 114, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 50 year old man has chronic sinusitis, nasal crusting, cough with haemoptysis and microscopic haematuria. Chest imaging shows several cavitating nodules. Serology is positive for cytoplasmic pattern ANCA directed at proteinase 3. Which histopathological finding best supports the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Non necrotising granulomas with asteroid bodies confined to hilar nodes and lung interstitium", "B": "Eosinophil rich vasculitis in a patient with severe asthma and peripheral blood eosinophilia", "C": "Necrotising granulomatous inflammation with vasculitis in the respiratory tract", "D": "Immune complex deposition with fibrinoid necrosis limited to medium arteries and sparing capillaries"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Necrotising granulomatous inflammation with vasculitis in the respiratory tract): Granulomatosis with polyangiitis combines necrotising granulomas of the upper and lower respiratory tract, small vessel vasculitis and glomerulonephritis, and PR3 ANCA is typical. Sarcoid granulomas are non necrotising. Eosinophilic disease needs asthma and eosinophilia, and polyarteritis nodosa is ANCA negative with no capillary lesions."}, {"id": 115, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 62 year old woman has rising creatinine, palpable purpura on the legs and alveolar haemorrhage on chest imaging. Her serology and renal function results are shown. A renal biopsy lacks granulomas and shows crescents with scant immunoglobulin staining. Which description best matches the expected renal lesion?", "options": {"A": "Mesangial immunoglobulin A deposits with proliferation and haematuria after an upper respiratory infection", "B": "Pauci immune necrotising crescentic glomerulonephritis with focal fibrinoid necrosis of capillary loops", "C": "Granular immune complex deposits along capillary walls producing subepithelial humps and diffuse hypercellularity", "D": "Linear immunoglobulin G deposition along the basement membrane with extensive crescent formation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Pauci immune necrotising crescentic glomerulonephritis with focal fibrinoid necrosis of capillary loops): Microscopic polyangiitis is an ANCA associated small vessel vasculitis and its renal lesion is pauci immune crescentic glomerulonephritis, meaning little or no immune deposition. Immune complex and anti GBM diseases show granular or linear staining. IgA nephropathy produces mesangial deposits and does not typically cause pulmonary haemorrhage.", "image": "question_images/q_115.png"}, {"id": 118, "domain": "cardiovascular_and_vascular", "question": "A 68 year old woman develops a swollen, tender left calf five days after a hip replacement. Ultrasound shows a non compressible popliteal vein with echogenic material. Which element of Virchow triad is most directly responsible for thrombus formation in the immobile leg after surgery?", "options": {"A": "Antibody mediated platelet activation with heparin dependent clumping and arterial white clots", "B": "Turbulent arterial flow combined with intimal plaque rupture and platelet adhesion to exposed collagen", "C": "Autoimmune destruction of vessel walls with perivascular T cell infiltration and fibrinoid necrosis", "D": "Venous stasis combined with endothelial injury and a surgery induced hypercoagulable state"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Venous stasis combined with endothelial injury and a surgery induced hypercoagulable state): Virchow triad comprises endothelial injury, abnormal blood flow (stasis or turbulence) and hypercoagulability. Postoperative immobility, venous manipulation and the surgical acute phase response favour venous thrombosis. Arterial plaque rupture, heparin induced thrombocytopenia and vasculitis are separate mechanisms that do not account for this picture."}, {"id": 139, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 16-year-old with recurrent chest infections, poor weight gain and chronic sputum production has CT showing bilateral upper lobe predominant bronchial dilatation with thick walls and mucus plugging. Sweat chloride testing on two occasions is elevated. Which underlying pathogenesis best explains the airway disease in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Congenital deficiency of bronchial wall cartilage leading to widespread tracheobronchial dilatation and collapse on expiration", "B": "Immotile cilia from dynein arm defects causing impaired clearance and situs inversus", "C": "Defective chloride and bicarbonate secretion producing dehydrated viscous mucus that impairs mucociliary clearance", "D": "Immune complex deposition in small airways triggered by inhaled organic antigens"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Defective chloride and bicarbonate secretion producing dehydrated viscous mucus that impairs mucociliary clearance): CFTR dysfunction reduces chloride and bicarbonate secretion and increases sodium absorption, producing thick mucus, chronic infection and bronchiectasis with an upper lobe distribution. Primary ciliary dyskinesia may share bronchiectasis but has normal sweat chloride. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is an interstitial process. Cartilage deficiency is a rare congenital cause."}, {"id": 141, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 52-year-old man dies on day four of lobar pneumonia. At autopsy, the right lower lobe is firm and grey-brown. Microscopy shows alveoli packed with fibrin, neutrophils and macrophages, with few remaining red cells. Which stage of evolution does this represent?", "options": {"A": "Congestion with vascular engorgement and proteinaceous fluid but few inflammatory cells", "B": "Resolution with enzymatic digestion of exudate and clearance by macrophages", "C": "Red hepatisation with intra-alveolar red cells and neutrophils and vascular engorgement", "D": "Grey hepatisation with fibrinosuppurative exudate and breakdown of red cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Grey hepatisation with fibrinosuppurative exudate and breakdown of red cells): Grey hepatisation occurs around days four to six, when red cells have lysed and the alveoli contain fibrin and neutrophils, giving a dry grey cut surface. Red hepatisation has abundant red cells. Congestion occurs in the first day. Resolution follows with enzymatic clearance and may leave organising pneumonia if incomplete."}, {"id": 143, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 50-year-old alcoholic man has had foul-smelling sputum and fever for two weeks after a drinking binge with vomiting. Chest radiography shows a thick-walled cavity with an air-fluid level in the posterior segment of the right upper lobe. Which pathological process best explains this lesion?", "options": {"A": "Central necrosis in a rheumatoid nodule with palisading histiocytes at the margin", "B": "Caseous necrosis within a granuloma following reactivation of a prior primary tuberculous focus in the lung", "C": "Coagulative necrosis of a wedge of lung after occlusion of a pulmonary artery branch", "D": "Liquefactive necrosis from anaerobic bacteria after aspiration of oropharyngeal contents"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Liquefactive necrosis from anaerobic bacteria after aspiration of oropharyngeal contents): Aspiration in an alcoholic patient seeds anaerobic and mixed oral flora into dependent segments, producing suppurative liquefactive necrosis and a cavity with an air-fluid level. Tuberculosis caseates and is usually apical. Infarcts are coagulative and wedge shaped. Rheumatoid nodules rarely contain air-fluid levels."}, {"id": 148, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 70-year-old man worked as a lagger in the 1960s. HRCT shows bibasal subpleural reticulation with honeycombing and calcified pleural plaques that spare the costophrenic angles. Biopsy is considered. Which histological finding is required to support a diagnosis of asbestosis rather than idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?", "options": {"A": "Interstitial fibrosis accompanied by iron-coated fibres within the lung parenchyma", "B": "Cellular bronchiolar polyps of granulation tissue extending through alveolar ducts", "C": "Diffuse alveolar septal widening by uniform inflammation with temporal homogeneity", "D": "Fibroblast foci at the advancing edge of patchy temporally heterogeneous fibrosis in subpleural lung"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Interstitial fibrosis accompanied by iron-coated fibres within the lung parenchyma): Asbestosis is diagnosed by a UIP-like fibrosis plus asbestos bodies, which are asbestos fibres coated with iron and protein. Fibroblast foci with temporal heterogeneity occur in IPF too and cannot discriminate. Uniform inflammation fits NSIP. Intraluminal granulation polyps indicate organising pneumonia."}, {"id": 152, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 48-year-old pigeon breeder has recurrent fever and cough several hours after cleaning the loft. HRCT shows ground glass opacities, poorly defined centrilobular nodules and lobular air trapping on expiratory images. Lung biopsy is performed. Which pathological triad best fits this disease process?", "options": {"A": "Eosinophilic alveolar exudate, peripheral eosinophilia, and necrotising vasculitis", "B": "Cellular bronchiolitis, interstitial chronic inflammation, and poorly formed non-necrotising granulomas", "C": "Diffuse alveolar damage, hyaline membranes, and type II pneumocyte proliferation", "D": "Fibroblast foci, honeycomb change, and temporal heterogeneity with subpleural accentuation of basal fibrosis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cellular bronchiolitis, interstitial chronic inflammation, and poorly formed non-necrotising granulomas): Hypersensitivity pneumonitis combines bronchiolocentric inflammation, interstitial lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate and small loose granulomas, with air trapping from small airway involvement. Diffuse alveolar damage characterises ARDS. Fibroblast foci and honeycombing indicate UIP. Eosinophilic disease shows prominent eosinophils rather than lymphocytes."}, {"id": 163, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 54-year-old man with old cavitary tuberculosis has recurrent haemoptysis. CT shows a mobile rounded soft tissue mass within an upper lobe cavity, with a thin crescent of air above it. Which statement best describes the pathology of this lesion?", "options": {"A": "Hypersensitivity reaction to fungal antigen producing eosinophilic mucin plugs in airways", "B": "Angioinvasive hyphae causing haemorrhagic infarction in a neutropenic host", "C": "Fungal ball of intertwined hyphae within a pre-existing cavity without tissue invasion", "D": "Granulomatous reaction to yeast forms with calcified lymph node involvement"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fungal ball of intertwined hyphae within a pre-existing cavity without tissue invasion): An aspergilloma is a saprophytic colonisation of a previous cavity, with a mobile fungal ball producing the air crescent and bleeding from the cavity wall. Angioinvasion occurs in immunocompromised hosts. Allergic disease shows eosinophilic mucin and bronchiectasis. Histoplasmosis is a granulomatous yeast infection."}, {"id": 166, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 30-year-old male smoker has dyspnoea and one previous pneumothorax. HRCT shows upper zone predominant nodules, some cavitating, and bizarre-shaped thick- and thin-walled cysts, with sparing of the costophrenic angles. Which immunohistochemical profile would be expected on the infiltrating cells in a biopsy?", "options": {"A": "CD20 positivity with a clonal B cell population on flow cytometry", "B": "Cytokeratin positivity with thyroid transcription factor 1 expression", "C": "CD1a and langerin positivity, with Birbeck granules on electron microscopy", "D": "CD68 positivity with cytoplasmic PAS positive diastase resistant granules in alveolar macrophages"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (CD1a and langerin positivity, with Birbeck granules on electron microscopy): Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis is a smoking related disease where Langerhans cells express CD1a and langerin and contain Birbeck granules, evolving from nodules to cavitating nodules to cysts. CD68 alone marks macrophages. CD20 marks B cells. Cytokeratin with TTF-1 suggests lung adenocarcinoma."}, {"id": 170, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A male infant born at 29 weeks by emergency caesarean section has grunting, retractions and cyanosis soon after delivery. Chest radiography shows a diffuse fine granular pattern with air bronchograms and low lung volumes. Which pathological finding is expected in the lungs at autopsy in this case?", "options": {"A": "Meconium and squames within alveoli with associated aspiration pneumonitis", "B": "Alveolar filling with neutrophils and group B streptococcal organisms in a lobar distribution", "C": "Cystic dilatation of terminal airways with fibrosis and squamous metaplasia after ventilation", "D": "Eosinophilic hyaline membranes lining collapsed distal airspaces and alveolar ducts"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Eosinophilic hyaline membranes lining collapsed distal airspaces and alveolar ducts): Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome results from surfactant deficiency in prematurity, with atelectasis, hyaline membranes and a ground glass granular radiographic pattern. Group B streptococcal pneumonia can mimic it but shows neutrophils and organisms. Bronchopulmonary dysplasia is a later complication of ventilation. Meconium aspiration affects term or post-term infants."}, {"id": 175, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 59-year-old heavy smoker has facial swelling, hyponatraemia with inappropriately concentrated urine, and a bulky right hilar and mediastinal mass encasing the bronchus. Bronchial biopsy shows marked crush artefact. Which description of the tumour cells is most characteristic of the underlying malignancy?", "options": {"A": "Uniform cells in organoid nests with salt-and-pepper chromatin, ample cytoplasm and very rare mitoses", "B": "Columnar cells with abundant mucin, eccentric nuclei and gland formation along fibrovascular cores", "C": "Scant cytoplasm, finely granular chromatin lacking nucleoli, nuclear moulding and a very high mitotic rate", "D": "Abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm, keratin pearls and prominent intercellular bridges between neoplastic cells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Scant cytoplasm, finely granular chromatin lacking nucleoli, nuclear moulding and a very high mitotic rate): Small cell carcinoma has scant cytoplasm, nuclear moulding, fine chromatin without nucleoli and a very high mitotic count, and crush artefact is typical because the cells are fragile. The hyponatraemia is SIADH from ectopic antidiuretic hormone. Keratin pearls point to squamous carcinoma, mucin and glands to adenocarcinoma, and organoid nests with rare mitoses to carcinoid."}, {"id": 176, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 45-year-old non-smoking woman has recurrent right middle lobe pneumonia. Bronchoscopy shows a vascular polypoid endobronchial tumour. The resected specimen contains nested neuroendocrine cells with four mitotic figures per 2 mm2 and small foci of punctate necrosis. What is the correct classification?", "options": {"A": "Typical carcinoid, defined by fewer than two mitoses per 2 mm2 and absent necrosis", "B": "Atypical carcinoid, defined by two to ten mitoses per 2 mm2 or foci of necrosis", "C": "Large cell neuroendocrine carcinoma, defined by more than ten mitoses per 2 mm2", "D": "Small cell carcinoma, defined by diffuse necrosis, nuclear moulding and crush artefact"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Atypical carcinoid, defined by two to ten mitoses per 2 mm2 or foci of necrosis): Carcinoids are separated by mitotic count and necrosis: typical has under two mitoses per 2 mm2 and no necrosis, atypical has two to ten mitoses or necrosis. This tumour has four mitoses and focal necrosis so it is atypical. High-grade large cell neuroendocrine and small cell carcinomas exceed ten mitoses and show extensive necrosis."}, {"id": 177, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 64-year-old man has right shoulder pain radiating down the medial arm and forearm, with ipsilateral ptosis, miosis and anhidrosis. Chest radiograph shows apical pleural capping with destruction of the first rib. Which structures are invaded to explain both the pain distribution and the Horner syndrome?", "options": {"A": "The intercostal nerves and azygos vein along the posterior chest wall", "B": "The upper trunk of the brachial plexus and the long thoracic nerve", "C": "The phrenic nerve and recurrent laryngeal nerve within the aortopulmonary window", "D": "Lower plexus roots (C8, T1) and the stellate ganglion or sympathetic chain"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lower plexus roots (C8, T1) and the stellate ganglion or sympathetic chain): A superior sulcus tumour grows through the apical pleura into the lower trunk and roots of the brachial plexus, producing ulnar-side arm pain. The adjacent sympathetic chain and stellate ganglion lie on the neck of the first rib, so invasion gives Horner syndrome. Phrenic and recurrent laryngeal injury cause diaphragm paralysis and hoarseness, and the upper trunk supplies the lateral arm."}, {"id": 178, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 55-year-old Asian woman who has never smoked has a peripheral lung adenocarcinoma with several small bilateral satellite nodules. Molecular testing of the biopsy is requested to guide systemic therapy. Which genetic alteration is most typically enriched in this clinical profile?", "options": {"A": "Inactivating RB1 mutation together with TP53 loss, found in high-grade neuroendocrine lung tumours", "B": "KRAS codon 12 mutation, typical of heavy smokers with mucinous histology and bronchorrhoea", "C": "ALK rearrangement through EML4 fusion, found mainly in older heavy smokers with squamous histology", "D": "Activating mutation in the EGFR tyrosine kinase domain, often an exon 19 deletion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Activating mutation in the EGFR tyrosine kinase domain, often an exon 19 deletion): EGFR activating mutations, especially exon 19 deletion and L858R, are enriched in never-smoking women of East Asian ancestry with adenocarcinoma and predict response to tyrosine kinase inhibitors. KRAS mutations track with smoking, and ALK fusion affects younger never-smokers rather than older smokers. RB1 and TP53 loss defines small cell carcinoma."}, {"id": 180, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 58-year-old woman with known breast cancer has progressive dyspnoea. She has no fever or purulent sputum. CT shows smooth and beaded interlobular septal thickening with peribronchovascular thickening, preserved lung architecture and small pleural effusions. Which pathological process best explains this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Haematogenous tumour deposits growing as discrete round nodules in the peripheral lung and subpleural zones", "B": "Tumour emboli and permeation within perilymphatic channels of the pulmonary interstitium", "C": "Non-necrotising granulomas tracking along bronchovascular bundles from sarcoid-type inflammation in hilar lymph nodes", "D": "Intra-alveolar proteinaceous exudate with alveolar septal oedema from capillary leak in acute lung injury"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Tumour emboli and permeation within perilymphatic channels of the pulmonary interstitium): Lymphangitic spread produces tumour in the lymphatics along the septa and bronchovascular bundles, thickening them in a smooth or beaded fashion while the lobular architecture remains intact. Haematogenous metastases form round nodules, airspace exudate fills alveoli, and sarcoid granulomas also follow lymphatics but do not explain the effusions in a patient with known breast cancer."}, {"id": 182, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 63-year-old smoker has proximal leg weakness that briefly improves with repeated effort, a dry mouth and absent tendon reflexes. Symptoms began insidiously over several months. CT shows a central hilar mass. Which tumour type and autoantibody target most probably explain the neurological syndrome?", "options": {"A": "Typical carcinoid tumour, with antibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels", "B": "Small cell carcinoma, with antibodies against postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors", "C": "Small cell carcinoma, with antibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels", "D": "Lung adenocarcinoma, with antibodies against the neuronal nuclear antigen Hu"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Small cell carcinoma, with antibodies against presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels): Facilitation of strength with exercise, autonomic dryness and areflexia describe Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, caused by antibodies to presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels that reduce acetylcholine release. It is most often paraneoplastic with small cell carcinoma. Postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor antibodies cause myasthenia gravis, and anti-Hu causes sensory neuronopathy."}, {"id": 184, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 66-year-old former shipyard worker has progressive dyspnoea and chest pain. CT shows nodular circumferential pleural thickening encasing the right lung, with mediastinal pleural involvement and ipsilateral volume loss. Biopsy confirms malignant mesothelioma. Which statement about histological subtype and prognosis is correct?", "options": {"A": "Biphasic is the most frequent subtype, while epithelioid disease has the poorest prognosis", "B": "Epithelioid is the most frequent subtype, while biphasic disease has the best prognosis", "C": "Sarcomatoid is the most frequent subtype, while epithelioid disease has the poorest prognosis", "D": "Epithelioid is most frequent, while sarcomatoid has the poorest prognosis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Epithelioid is most frequent, while sarcomatoid has the poorest prognosis): Epithelioid mesothelioma accounts for roughly 60 percent of cases and carries the best outlook, biphasic disease is intermediate, and sarcomatoid disease is least common with the worst survival. The CT description of a circumferential rind encasing the lung with volume loss is typical of any subtype and does not discriminate between them."}, {"id": 186, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 40-year-old man has fever and pleuritic pain two weeks after pneumonia. Enhanced CT shows a lenticular pleural collection whose enhancing visceral and parietal layers are separated by fluid. Pleural fluid pH is 6.9 and Gram stain is positive. Which process best explains the enhancing separated layers?", "options": {"A": "Neoplastic infiltration of both pleural layers by epithelioid cells forming a nodular rind", "B": "Lymphatic obstruction causing a bland transudate between normal thin pleural layers without enhancement", "C": "Calcified hyaline plaques on the parietal pleura lifted away from normal visceral pleura", "D": "Thickened parietal and visceral pleura from fibrin and inflammation enclosing purulent fluid"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Thickened parietal and visceral pleura from fibrin and inflammation enclosing purulent fluid): In the fibrinopurulent stage of a parapneumonic effusion fibrin and granulation tissue thicken both pleural layers, which enhance with contrast and are separated by pus, giving the split pleura sign. Mesothelioma forms a nodular rind, plaques are calcified parietal and non-enhancing, and a transudate would not show enhancing layers."}, {"id": 188, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 72-year-old man with previous asbestos exposure has a 4 cm peripheral subpleural round mass in the left lower lobe on CT, with bronchovascular bundles curving into it like a comet tail and adjacent pleural thickening. Which pathological mechanism best explains the mass?", "options": {"A": "Pulmonary infarct from thromboembolism with haemorrhagic wedge-shaped consolidation abutting the pleura", "B": "Infolding of lung into thickened visceral pleura with collapse of enfolded parenchyma", "C": "Peripheral adenocarcinoma with a desmoplastic scar and retraction of the overlying pleura", "D": "Organising pneumonia forming a necrotic abscess with an air-fluid level and thick wall"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Infolding of lung into thickened visceral pleura with collapse of enfolded parenchyma): Rounded atelectasis, or folded lung, arises when an effusion or pleural fibrosis causes the visceral pleura to invaginate, trapping and collapsing adjacent lung. Vessels sweep into the mass as a comet tail, and it is usually seen with asbestos pleural disease. Cancer, abscess and infarct lack this configuration."}, {"id": 195, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 16-year-old tall boy with gynaecomastia, small firm testes and learning difficulty is found to have a non-seminomatous germ cell tumour in the anterior mediastinum. Imaging shows no testicular mass. Which constitutional chromosomal abnormality best explains his increased risk of this tumour?", "options": {"A": "An additional X chromosome giving a 47,XXY constitutional karyotype", "B": "Loss of one sex chromosome giving a 45,X constitutional karyotype in females", "C": "An additional chromosome 21 giving trisomy of that autosome in every cell", "D": "Amplification of chromosome 12p as isochromosome 12p found within the tumour cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (An additional X chromosome giving a 47,XXY constitutional karyotype): Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY) carries an increased risk of mediastinal non-seminomatous germ cell tumours, and the described phenotype fits. Isochromosome 12p is a somatic change within germ cell tumours rather than an inherited predisposition. Turner and Down syndromes have different tumour associations."}, {"id": 198, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 45-year-old man with no neurological deficit has a well-circumscribed 3 cm paravertebral posterior mediastinal mass widening an intervertebral foramen, with high T2 signal and heterogeneous enhancement. Histology shows alternating cellular and loose myxoid areas with nuclear palisading. Which immunohistochemical marker is expected?", "options": {"A": "Strong cytokeratin AE1/AE3 positivity with absent S100 protein expression", "B": "Synaptophysin and chromogranin positivity with Homer Wright rosettes and neuropil", "C": "Diffuse strong nuclear and cytoplasmic S100 protein positivity", "D": "Nuclear STAT6 positivity with a branching staghorn vascular pattern"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Diffuse strong nuclear and cytoplasmic S100 protein positivity): Alternating Antoni A cellular and Antoni B myxoid areas with nuclear palisading define a schwannoma, a Schwann cell tumour that is diffusely S100 positive. The foraminal widening arises from a dumbbell extension along the nerve root. Cytokeratin marks epithelial tumours, synaptophysin marks neuroblastoma and STAT6 marks solitary fibrous tumour."}, {"id": 199, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 3-year-old has a posterior mediastinal paravertebral mass with fine stippled calcification and rib splaying. Urinary homovanillic and vanillylmandelic acid are raised. Biopsy shows small round blue cells in rosettes around neuropil. Which genetic alteration indicates the most aggressive behaviour?", "options": {"A": "PAX3-FOXO1 fusion arising from translocation between chromosomes 2 and 13", "B": "EWSR1-FLI1 fusion arising from translocation between chromosomes 11 and 22", "C": "Biallelic NF1 inactivation with loss of neurofibromin tumour suppressor function", "D": "Amplification of the MYCN oncogene on the short arm of chromosome 2"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Amplification of the MYCN oncogene on the short arm of chromosome 2): Raised catecholamine metabolites with Homer Wright rosettes in a young child establish neuroblastoma. MYCN amplification is the key adverse genetic marker. Ewing sarcoma and alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma are other small round blue cell tumours with their own fusions, and NF1 loss underlies neurofibromas rather than neuroblastoma."}, {"id": 200, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 38-year-old with no systemic symptoms has an incidental 5 cm well-defined, avidly and homogeneously enhancing middle mediastinal mass. Excision shows lymph node with small atrophic germinal centres, expanded concentric mantle layers and prominent hyalinised radial vessels. What is the most likely diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Sarcoidosis with compact non-necrotising epithelioid granulomas throughout the lymph node cortex", "B": "Follicular lymphoma with neoplastic follicles lacking mantle zones and effaced nodal architecture", "C": "Hyaline vascular Castleman disease, a localised unicentric lymphoproliferative disorder", "D": "Plasma cell type of Castleman disease with interleukin-6 driven systemic features"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Hyaline vascular Castleman disease, a localised unicentric lymphoproliferative disorder): Atrophic germinal centres with onion-skin mantle layers and penetrating hyalinised vessels give the lollipop appearance of hyaline vascular Castleman disease, typically a solitary, intensely enhancing mass without systemic symptoms. The plasma cell variant shows interfollicular plasma cells and fever, follicular lymphoma lacks mantles, and sarcoidosis shows granulomas."}, {"id": 202, "domain": "respiratory_and_mediastinum", "question": "A 52-year-old febrile man vomits violently after a heavy meal, then develops severe chest pain and subcutaneous emphysema. CT shows pneumomediastinum and a left pleural effusion with extraluminal oral contrast. Which pathology and typical site of injury are most likely?", "options": {"A": "Transmural tear of the distal left posterolateral oesophagus above the diaphragm", "B": "Perforation of the cervical oesophagus at the cricopharyngeus from endoscopic instrumentation", "C": "Mucosal-only tear at the gastro-oesophageal junction, Mallory-Weiss type, without full-thickness perforation", "D": "Rupture of the thoracic aorta at the ligamentum arteriosum after deceleration injury"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Transmural tear of the distal left posterolateral oesophagus above the diaphragm): Barotrauma from forceful vomiting against a closed upper sphincter tears the full thickness of the distal oesophagus, classically on the left posterolateral wall, leaking air and contents into the mediastinum and pleura. Mallory-Weiss tears are mucosal and cause haematemesis, and cervical perforation or aortic rupture have different mechanisms."}, {"id": 211, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 58-year-old man with twenty years of heartburn has a tongue of salmon-pink mucosa extending proximally from the gastro-oesophageal junction at endoscopy. Targeted biopsies are taken from this segment. Which histological finding most specifically identifies Barrett oesophagus in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Basal cell hyperplasia with elongated papillae and scattered intraepithelial eosinophils in squamous mucosa", "B": "Gastric cardiac-type glandular mucosa with foveolar cells lining the distal oesophageal segment", "C": "Squamous epithelium with glycogenic acanthosis and thickened superficial keratin across the lower oesophagus", "D": "Intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells replacing the squamous lining above the gastro-oesophageal junction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells replacing the squamous lining above the gastro-oesophageal junction): Barrett oesophagus is columnar metaplasia of the distal oesophagus, and goblet cells (intestinal metaplasia) are the feature that marks it as the cancer-predisposing change. Gastric-type columnar lining is tempting because it is also columnar, but it lacks goblet cells. Basal cell hyperplasia and eosinophils are changes of reflux oesophagitis within squamous mucosa. Glycogenic acanthosis is a benign squamous appearance."}, {"id": 212, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 44-year-old woman with long-standing regurgitation has distal oesophageal mucosal biopsies reported with an expanded basal zone, elongated lamina propria papillae reaching the upper epithelium, and scattered eosinophils and neutrophils. There is no columnar change. Which underlying process best accounts for these histological findings?", "options": {"A": "Allergen-driven eosinophilic infiltration producing dense intraepithelial eosinophil clusters and microabscesses", "B": "Herpes simplex infection causing multinucleate cells with ground-glass nuclear change at ulcer edges", "C": "Chronic acid and bile exposure causing epithelial injury with regenerative basal hyperplasia", "D": "Autoimmune lymphocytic destruction of squamous cells producing subepithelial bullae and desquamation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Chronic acid and bile exposure causing epithelial injury with regenerative basal hyperplasia): Basal zone expansion and elongated papillae are the reparative response to repeated acid-peptic injury of the squamous lining. A few eosinophils and neutrophils may be present, but dense clusters would point to eosinophilic oesophagitis. Herpes causes ulcers with multinucleate cells at the edge. Bullous autoimmune disease would show subepithelial splitting rather than hyperplasia."}, {"id": 218, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 45-year-old man develops severe chest pain and subcutaneous emphysema after vomiting following a binge. A contrast study shows extraluminal contrast on the left at the lower oesophagus. Which pathological description best explains this presentation compared with a Mallory-Weiss tear?", "options": {"A": "Perforation of an ulcerated squamous carcinoma into the mediastinum with invasion of nearby nodes", "B": "Transmural necrosis of the mid oesophagus from caustic ingestion with stricture formation later", "C": "Full-thickness rupture of the distal posterolateral left oesophageal wall from raised intraluminal pressure", "D": "Longitudinal mucosal and submucosal laceration at the gastro-oesophageal junction without transmural extension"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Full-thickness rupture of the distal posterolateral left oesophageal wall from raised intraluminal pressure): Boerhaave syndrome is spontaneous transmural rupture, typically of the left posterolateral distal oesophagus, after a sudden rise in pressure during vomiting, giving mediastinitis, air in the soft tissues and contrast leak. Mallory-Weiss is a mucosal tear at the junction presenting with bleeding. Caustic and tumour perforation have other histories."}, {"id": 224, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 50-year-old woman on a proton pump inhibitor for many years has multiple small sessile polyps in the gastric fundus found during surveillance. Biopsy shows cystically dilated oxyntic glands lined by parietal and chief cells. What is the most likely nature of these lesions?", "options": {"A": "Benign fundic gland polyps related to long-term acid suppression", "B": "Pre-malignant tubular adenomas of the oxyntic mucosa from intestinal metaplasia", "C": "Hamartomatous polyps with arborising smooth muscle typical of a polyposis syndrome", "D": "Hyperplastic foveolar polyps related to chronic gastritis and Helicobacter infection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Benign fundic gland polyps related to long-term acid suppression): Fundic gland polyps contain dilated oxyntic glands lined by normal parietal and chief cells and are linked to prolonged PPI use, with sporadic and FAP-related forms as well. Adenomas, Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas and hyperplastic polyps have different histology."}, {"id": 225, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 30-year-old woman with iron deficiency anaemia, bloating and weight loss has positive tissue transglutaminase antibodies. Duodenal biopsies are taken while she is on a gluten-containing diet and reviewed by the pathologist. Which combination of histological findings best fits the expected diagnosis in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes", "B": "Normal villi, subepithelial collagen thickening and increased lamina propria plasma cells", "C": "Preserved villi, lymphangiectasia and pale foamy lacteals with a normal crypt compartment", "D": "Villous blunting, macrophages with PAS-positive granules and normal intraepithelial lymphocytes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia and increased intraepithelial lymphocytes): Coeliac disease, driven by HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 restricted T-cell reaction to gliadin, produces villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia and raised intraepithelial lymphocytes. PAS-positive macrophages indicate Whipple disease, and the other options describe collagenous or lymphatic disorders with different architecture."}, {"id": 226, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 62-year-old man with coeliac disease since childhood, previously well controlled, develops weight loss, abdominal pain and recurrent diarrhoea despite strict gluten avoidance. CT shows jejunal wall thickening with ulceration and enlarged mesenteric nodes. Which complication of coeliac disease should be suspected in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Ileal neuroendocrine tumour arising from enterochromaffin cells with mesenteric fibrosis", "B": "Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma arising from clonal intraepithelial lymphocytes of the proximal small bowel", "C": "Primary small bowel gastrointestinal stromal tumour arising from interstitial cells of Cajal", "D": "Small bowel mantle cell lymphoma arising in multiple lymphomatous polyposis"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma arising from clonal intraepithelial lymphocytes of the proximal small bowel): Refractory coeliac disease and enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma are recognised complications, typically proximal jejunal with ulceration after years of disease, and small bowel adenocarcinoma risk is also raised. GIST, mantle cell lymphoma and ileal NET are not specifically linked to coeliac disease."}, {"id": 232, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 40-year-old woman has colicky abdominal pain and intermittent vomiting. CT shows a target-like bowel-within-bowel configuration in the small bowel with a mass at the leading edge. Compared with intussusception in early childhood, which statement about the adult form is correct?", "options": {"A": "The cause is usually lymphoid hyperplasia following viral infection", "B": "A pathological lead point such as a polyp or tumour is usually identified", "C": "The cause is usually an enteric duplication cyst of the terminal ileum", "D": "The cause is usually a pyloric muscular hypertrophy with proximal obstruction"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A pathological lead point such as a polyp or tumour is usually identified): Most childhood cases are idiopathic with lymphoid hyperplasia, but adult intussusception commonly has a structural lead point such as a polyp, lipoma or malignancy that is dragged into the distal bowel by peristalsis. The other options are paediatric or rare causes."}, {"id": 236, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A newborn fails to pass meconium within 48 hours and has abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectum with a transition to dilated sigmoid colon. Suction rectal biopsy is requested. Which finding would confirm the diagnosis in this infant?", "options": {"A": "Absence of ganglion cells in the submucosal and myenteric plexuses with hypertrophic nerve trunks", "B": "Increased ganglion cells with plexiform hyperplasia in the myenteric plexus of the sigmoid", "C": "Normal ganglion cells with mucosal eosinophils and deficient smooth muscle in the rectal wall", "D": "Absent interstitial cells of Cajal with preserved ganglia in the distal colon alone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Absence of ganglion cells in the submucosal and myenteric plexuses with hypertrophic nerve trunks): Hirschsprung disease reflects failure of neural crest migration (often RET mutation) so the distal bowel lacks ganglion cells and remains tonically contracted, with proximal dilatation. Biopsy shows aganglionosis with hypertrophic nerve trunks. Neuronal dysplasia and pseudo-obstruction have other histology."}, {"id": 242, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 14-year-old boy has dark macules on his lips and buccal mucosa and presents with small bowel intussusception. A resected jejunal polyp shows a branching arborising core of smooth muscle covered by normal epithelium. Which gene is mutated in this syndrome?", "options": {"A": "SMAD4, a TGF-beta signalling mediator on chromosome 18q", "B": "STK11 (LKB1), a tumour suppressor kinase on chromosome 19p", "C": "APC, a Wnt pathway regulator on chromosome 5q", "D": "MUTYH, a base excision repair glycosylase on chromosome 1p"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (STK11 (LKB1), a tumour suppressor kinase on chromosome 19p): Peutz-Jeghers syndrome combines mucocutaneous pigmentation with hamartomatous polyps showing arborising smooth muscle, caused by germline STK11 mutation, with raised risks of intussusception and cancers of the gut, breast, pancreas and gonads. APC, SMAD4 and MUTYH cause other polyposis phenotypes."}, {"id": 243, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 4-year-old boy has painless rectal bleeding. Colonoscopy shows a 1.5 cm smooth, pedunculated red rectal polyp with a surface erosion. Histology shows dilated mucus-filled cystic glands in an expanded, inflamed lamina propria without dysplasia. What is the most appropriate description of this lesion?", "options": {"A": "A hyperplastic polyp with serrated crypts, arising from sessile serrated change", "B": "A hamartomatous juvenile polyp, usually benign and solitary in a child", "C": "An adenomatous polyp with high-grade dysplasia, carrying a high chance of invasion at this age", "D": "An inflammatory pseudopolyp from ulcerative colitis, with regenerating mucosa between ulcers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A hamartomatous juvenile polyp, usually benign and solitary in a child): Solitary juvenile polyps are hamartomatous, typically in the rectum of children, with cystic mucus-filled glands in oedematous inflamed stroma, and rarely have malignant potential. Multiple polyps with a family history raise the possibility of juvenile polyposis syndrome (SMAD4 or BMPR1A)."}, {"id": 250, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 62-year-old woman has a cystic dilated appendix on CT, with calcified wall and mucinous ascites scalloping the liver margin. The appendix shows low-grade mucinous epithelium with pushing borders and no invasion. Which diagnosis fits this appendiceal lesion and its spread?", "options": {"A": "Low-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm complicated by pseudomyxoma peritonei", "B": "Appendiceal neuroendocrine tumour with carcinoid syndrome from liver metastases", "C": "Appendiceal inflammatory mucocele due to chronic obstruction by a faecolith", "D": "Appendiceal goblet cell adenocarcinoma with nodal metastasis to the mesentery"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Low-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm complicated by pseudomyxoma peritonei): Low-grade appendiceal mucinous neoplasm can rupture and seed the peritoneum, producing pseudomyxoma peritonei with gelatinous ascites and redistribution at the liver, spleen and omentum. Goblet cell carcinoma, NET and obstructive mucocele do not combine neoplastic mucinous epithelium with peritoneal mucin spread."}, {"id": 253, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 66-year-old woman on long-term NSAID therapy has chronic watery diarrhoea. Colonoscopy appears normal. Random colonic biopsies show increased intraepithelial lymphocytes, lamina propria inflammation and a thickened subepithelial band. Which diagnosis best fits these histological features in this particular patient?", "options": {"A": "Collagenous colitis, with a subepithelial collagen band thicker than 10 micrometres", "B": "Ulcerative colitis, with basal plasmacytosis and crypt distortion", "C": "Pseudomembranous colitis, with fibrin-rich volcano-like exudates and recent antibiotic exposure", "D": "Ischaemic colitis, with ghost crypts and withered mucosa"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Collagenous colitis, with a subepithelial collagen band thicker than 10 micrometres): Microscopic colitis causes watery diarrhoea with a normal-appearing mucosa; the collagenous form shows a thickened subepithelial collagen band with lymphocytosis, and NSAID use is a recognised association. Ulcerative colitis, ischaemic and pseudomembranous colitis show structural or exudative changes."}, {"id": 255, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 67-year-old man with early satiety has a distal gastric ulcer with a depressed base, irregular heaped margins and loss of mucosal fold convergence on endoscopy. Biopsies show glandular malignant cells. Which feature best distinguishes this ulcer from a benign peptic ulcer?", "options": {"A": "Multiple shallow erosions across the antrum with haemorrhagic petechiae", "B": "Heaped, irregular margins with shouldered edges and abrupt fold termination", "C": "A sharply demarcated punched-out crater with radiating folds reaching the edge", "D": "A clean fibrinous base with smooth, level margins and a surrounding halo of erythema"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Heaped, irregular margins with shouldered edges and abrupt fold termination): Malignant gastric ulcers have irregular, rolled or heaped edges, a necrotic uneven base and folds that stop abruptly at the margin, whereas benign ulcers are punched out with clean bases and converging folds. Erosive gastritis consists of shallow multiple lesions."}, {"id": 260, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 62-year-old man with hepatitis C cirrhosis has a 3 cm liver nodule on multiphase CT. It enhances avidly in the arterial phase, then becomes hypoattenuating relative to surrounding liver in the portal venous phase. Which pathological change best explains the arterial phase enhancement of this nodule?", "options": {"A": "Sinusoidal dilatation from hepatic venous outflow obstruction pooling contrast within the lesion", "B": "Arterial shunting into portal venous branches through a dilated peribiliary vascular plexus around the nodule", "C": "Expanded Kupffer cell population taking up contrast early within hepatocytes lining the lesion", "D": "Proliferation of unpaired new arteries as native portal tracts and portal venous inflow are lost"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Proliferation of unpaired new arteries as native portal tracts and portal venous inflow are lost): As a dysplastic nodule progresses to hepatocellular carcinoma, portal tracts are lost and the lesion is supplied by unpaired arteries from neoangiogenesis. This gives arterial hyperenhancement, and the loss of portal venous inflow with early venous drainage gives washout. Arterioportal shunts cause transient wedge-shaped enhancement without washout. Kupffer cell uptake and sinusoidal congestion do not produce a nodule with this wash-in and washout pattern."}, {"id": 262, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 34-year-old woman using the oral contraceptive pill has a 6 cm well-circumscribed hepatic mass on MRI with intralesional fat and no scar. She has no cirrhosis. Pathology review is requested to stratify the risk of malignant change. Which adenoma subtype carries the greatest risk of transformation to hepatocellular carcinoma?", "options": {"A": "Inflammatory subtype with IL6 signalling activation and sinusoidal dilatation", "B": "Subtype with biallelic HNF1A inactivation and diffuse steatosis on histology", "C": "Unclassified subtype lacking mutation and without a characteristic immunohistochemical pattern", "D": "Subtype with activating CTNNB1 mutation, associated with androgen exposure"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Subtype with activating CTNNB1 mutation, associated with androgen exposure): Beta-catenin activated adenomas carry the highest risk of malignant transformation, and are associated with male sex, androgens and glycogen storage disease. Intralesional fat points to the HNF1A inactivated type, which is rarely malignant. Inflammatory adenomas are the commonest subtype and more often bleed. Size above 5 cm adds risk but does not define the molecular subtype."}, {"id": 263, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 28-year-old woman has an incidental 4 cm liver lesion. MRI shows a central scar that is hyperintense on T2 and enhances on delayed phase, with the remaining lesion near isointense to liver. She takes no hormonal therapy and has a normal background liver. Which mechanism best accounts for this lesion?", "options": {"A": "Compensatory hypertrophy of liver after portal vein occlusion in neighbouring segments", "B": "Regenerative nodule formation after repeated necrosis and bridging fibrous septa", "C": "Polyclonal hyperplastic response of hepatocytes to a focal abnormality of arterial blood supply", "D": "Monoclonal neoplastic proliferation of hepatocytes driven by inactivating HNF1A mutation and steatosis"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Polyclonal hyperplastic response of hepatocytes to a focal abnormality of arterial blood supply): Focal nodular hyperplasia is a polyclonal hyperplastic response to a vascular malformation, with a central scar containing a malformed artery. The scar is T2 hyperintense and enhances late, unlike the scar of fibrolamellar carcinoma, which is low on T2. Adenomas are monoclonal. Cirrhotic nodules need a background of diffuse injury."}, {"id": 265, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 48-year-old man with arthralgia, diabetes and skin pigmentation has a liver markedly low in signal on T2-weighted MRI, with a normal-signal spleen. Genetic testing shows C282Y homozygosity. Which pattern of iron deposition on pathological examination best accounts for these findings?", "options": {"A": "Haemosiderin in centrilobular hepatocytes secondary to chronic hepatic venous congestion", "B": "Copper-associated protein within periportal hepatocytes with concurrent basal ganglia involvement", "C": "Iron within periportal hepatocytes, with relative sparing of Kupffer cells and macrophages", "D": "Iron predominantly in Kupffer cells and splenic macrophages from repeated red cell transfusion"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Iron within periportal hepatocytes, with relative sparing of Kupffer cells and macrophages): Hereditary haemochromatosis from HFE mutation causes parenchymal iron overload, beginning in periportal hepatocytes. The reticuloendothelial system is initially spared, so the spleen keeps normal signal. Transfusional siderosis deposits iron in Kupffer cells and splenic macrophages, darkening the spleen. Congestion does not cause primary periportal iron loading."}, {"id": 267, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and obesity has steatotic liver disease on ultrasound. Liver biopsy shows macrovesicular steatosis, hepatocyte ballooning and lobular inflammation. He drinks very little alcohol. Which histological feature is the strongest predictor of liver-related mortality in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Number of ballooned hepatocytes with associated Mallory-Denk body formation", "B": "Stage of fibrosis, from perisinusoidal deposition through bridging septa to cirrhosis", "C": "Intensity of lobular inflammation with mixed neutrophil and lymphocyte infiltrate", "D": "Degree of steatosis, expressed as the percentage of hepatocytes containing fat droplets"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stage of fibrosis, from perisinusoidal deposition through bridging septa to cirrhosis): Fibrosis stage is the histological feature that best predicts liver-related events and mortality in steatotic liver disease. Steatosis, ballooning and lobular inflammation contribute to activity grading, but they do not independently predict outcome as well. Steatosis may even regress as cirrhosis develops."}, {"id": 270, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with polycythaemia vera has abdominal pain and ascites. CT shows a heterogeneously enhancing liver with absent hepatic vein opacification, while the caudate lobe is enlarged and enhances normally. Why is the caudate lobe typically spared in this condition?", "options": {"A": "Its portal venous inflow arises from the splenic vein, preserving oxygenated drainage", "B": "It has a distinct hepatic arterial supply from the right gastric artery maintaining perfusion", "C": "Its parenchyma lacks sinusoids and therefore resists congestion from outflow obstruction", "D": "Its short hepatic veins drain directly into the inferior vena cava below the obstruction"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Its short hepatic veins drain directly into the inferior vena cava below the obstruction): In Budd-Chiari syndrome the main hepatic veins are occluded, producing centrilobular congestion in most of the liver. The caudate lobe drains through separate short veins directly into the inferior vena cava, so it hypertrophies. Its blood supply and sinusoidal structure are not different from the remaining liver."}, {"id": 276, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "Surveillance of a cirrhotic liver detects a 1.2 cm nodule. The pathologist debates whether it is a high-grade dysplastic nodule or early well-differentiated hepatocellular carcinoma, and requests features that separate the two. Which histological finding most reliably indicates early carcinoma?", "options": {"A": "Increased cell density with small cell change, mild nuclear crowding and a focal acinar pattern", "B": "Presence of unpaired arteries within the nodule on special stains", "C": "Stromal invasion with neoplastic hepatocytes extending into portal tracts", "D": "Focal thinning of the reticulin framework without cytological atypia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stromal invasion with neoplastic hepatocytes extending into portal tracts): Stromal invasion is the most reliable feature separating early hepatocellular carcinoma from a high-grade dysplastic nodule. Small cell change, cell density and unpaired arteries occur in dysplastic nodules too. Focal reticulin thinning alone is not diagnostic."}, {"id": 277, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 45-year-old non-smoking man has dyspnoea and CT showing basal-predominant panacinar emphysema. His liver enzymes are mildly elevated, and biopsy shows round eosinophilic cytoplasmic globules in periportal hepatocytes. Serum alpha-1 antitrypsin level is low. What are these globules and how do they stain?", "options": {"A": "Rhodanine-positive granules of copper-binding protein within lysosomes of periportal hepatocytes", "B": "Diastase-sensitive PAS-positive glycogen deposits in hepatocytes of an enlarged liver", "C": "PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules of misfolded protein retained in endoplasmic reticulum", "D": "Perls-positive granules of haemosiderin accumulating in periportal hepatocytes"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules of misfolded protein retained in endoplasmic reticulum): Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency (PiZZ) retains misfolded Z protein in hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum, seen as PAS-positive globules that resist diastase. The same genotype causes basal panacinar emphysema through unopposed neutrophil elastase. Glycogen is diastase-sensitive, and haemosiderin and copper stain differently."}, {"id": 280, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 70-year-old man has recurrent cholangitis. Ultrasound shows soft, earthy stones within dilated intrahepatic and common bile ducts. He had a choledochoduodenostomy years earlier. He is otherwise well between episodes of infection. Which factor best explains the formation of these stones?", "options": {"A": "Cholesterol supersaturation related to oestrogen exposure and rapid weight loss", "B": "Bacterial beta-glucuronidase releasing free bilirubin that precipitates as calcium salts", "C": "Cirrhosis with portal hypertension producing hypersplenism and increased pigment turnover in bile", "D": "Chronic haemolysis increasing the unconjugated bilirubin load excreted into bile"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Bacterial beta-glucuronidase releasing free bilirubin that precipitates as calcium salts): Brown pigment stones form in infected, stagnant ducts, often after biliary surgery. Bacterial enzymes deconjugate bilirubin glucuronide, and calcium bilirubinate precipitates. Black pigment stones arise in the gallbladder in haemolysis or cirrhosis. Cholesterol stones relate to supersaturation."}, {"id": 281, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 65-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes has fever and right upper quadrant pain. CT shows gas within the gallbladder lumen and wall without a gallstone obstructing the cystic duct. He is tachycardic. Which organisms are most commonly responsible for the gas?", "options": {"A": "Candida species colonising the biliary tree in immunosuppressed patients", "B": "Helicobacter species ascending from the stomach through the ampulla", "C": "Gas-forming anaerobes such as Clostridium perfringens, with Escherichia coli", "D": "Salmonella typhi in chronic carriage within gallbladder biofilm on gallstones"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gas-forming anaerobes such as Clostridium perfringens, with Escherichia coli): Emphysematous cholecystitis is a gas-forming infection of the gallbladder wall, typically in diabetic men, and often arises from vascular compromise. Clostridium perfringens and enteric organisms such as Escherichia coli are usual. It has a high risk of gangrene and perforation, so it is treated urgently."}, {"id": 286, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 50-year-old woman with fatigue and pruritus has raised alkaline phosphatase and anti-mitochondrial antibodies. MRCP of the biliary tree is normal. She also has dry eyes and mouth, and no history of colitis. Which ducts are the primary target of injury in her disease?", "options": {"A": "Terminal intrahepatic ducts at the ductal plate with arrested remodelling", "B": "Small interlobular and septal bile ducts, injured by lymphocytic cholangitis", "C": "Medium and large intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts with segmental stricturing", "D": "Common bile duct and ampulla with fibrous stenosis from chronic scarring"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Small interlobular and septal bile ducts, injured by lymphocytic cholangitis): Primary biliary cholangitis is an autoimmune injury to small interlobular and septal ducts, which explains the normal MRCP. PSC in contrast affects larger ducts and produces strictures on imaging. Ductal plate malformation is congenital."}, {"id": 290, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 45-year-old man has severe epigastric pain after binge drinking, and serial lipase results are plotted. CT shows peripancreatic stranding, and serum calcium is low at 1.9 mmol/L. At laparotomy there are chalky white plaques in the omentum. What process produces these plaques?", "options": {"A": "Dystrophic calcification of coagulative necrosis within metastatic deposits seeded across the omentum", "B": "Coagulative necrosis of omental fat from thrombosis of small epiploic arteries", "C": "Caseous necrosis of tuberculous omental granulomas with calcium salt precipitation", "D": "Lipase digestion of triglycerides to fatty acids that bind calcium as insoluble soaps"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Lipase digestion of triglycerides to fatty acids that bind calcium as insoluble soaps): In acute pancreatitis, released lipases digest fat, and the liberated fatty acids bind calcium to form soaps, called saponification. This produces chalky white plaques and can lower serum calcium. Metastases, tuberculosis and infarction have different gross and microscopic appearances.", "image": "question_images/q_290.png"}, {"id": 292, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 50-year-old woman, five weeks after severe necrotising pancreatitis, has persistent fever. CT shows a 9 cm collection with a defined enhancing wall containing heterogeneous material of non-liquid density. Her white cell count is raised. Under the revised Atlanta classification, which term applies?", "options": {"A": "Acute peripancreatic fluid collection, a homogeneous collection in interstitial oedematous pancreatitis", "B": "Pseudocyst, a mature encapsulated fluid collection with minimal or no solid component", "C": "Acute necrotic collection, an early collection without a defined wall within four weeks", "D": "Walled-off necrosis, a mature encapsulated collection containing necrotic tissue"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Walled-off necrosis, a mature encapsulated collection containing necrotic tissue): After four weeks, a necrotic collection with an enhancing wall is walled-off necrosis. A pseudocyst contains fluid only, and arises in interstitial oedematous pancreatitis. Acute necrotic collections lack a wall and occur earlier."}, {"id": 299, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 60-year-old woman has an incidental 4 cm lobulated pancreatic head lesion with innumerable tiny cysts and a central stellate scar with sunburst calcification. She has mild epigastric discomfort, normal lipase and no history of pancreatitis. Which cell type lines the cysts?", "options": {"A": "Discohesive polygonal cells with eosinophilic cytoplasm and hyaline globules", "B": "Clear cuboidal cells rich in glycogen, forming a benign serous neoplasm", "C": "Neuroendocrine cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin arranged in trabecular nests", "D": "Tall columnar cells with apical mucin, forming a premalignant neoplasm"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Clear cuboidal cells rich in glycogen, forming a benign serous neoplasm): Serous cystadenomas are lined by glycogen-rich cuboidal cells, giving a honeycomb of microcysts and often a central scar with calcification. They are benign and are associated with von Hippel-Lindau disease. Mucinous lesions are lined by mucin-secreting columnar cells."}, {"id": 307, "domain": "gastrointestinal_and_hepatobiliary", "question": "A 59-year-old man with a known gastric adenocarcinoma has new ascites. CT shows nodular deposits in the pouch of Douglas and along the right paracolic gutter. Cytology of the ascites shows malignant cells. By which route do these deposits most plausibly arise?", "options": {"A": "Haematogenous embolisation via the portal vein, seeding peritoneal surfaces from the liver", "B": "Contiguous invasion across the serosa, limited to organs touching the stomach", "C": "Lymphatic spread through the thoracic duct reaching the peritoneal cavity from distant nodes", "D": "Transcoelomic shedding of tumour cells that settle where peritoneal fluid pools"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Transcoelomic shedding of tumour cells that settle where peritoneal fluid pools): Peritoneal metastases arise by transcoelomic spread, with cells shed from a serosa-penetrating tumour carried by peritoneal fluid flow. They collect in the pouch of Douglas, the right paracolic gutter and the subphrenic space. Haematogenous and lymphatic routes do not explain this distribution."}, {"id": 313, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes for 22 years has heavy proteinuria, hypertension and retinopathy. Kidneys are normal in size with smooth outlines on ultrasound. Which light microscopic finding in the glomeruli is most specific for his renal disease?", "options": {"A": "Wire-loop thickening of capillary walls from extensive subendothelial immune complex deposits", "B": "Ovoid acellular mesangial nodules of sclerosis at the centre of lobulated glomerular tufts", "C": "Segmental obliteration of capillary loops by hyaline sclerosis with podocyte foot-process effacement", "D": "Extracellular deposits staining with Congo red and showing apple-green birefringence under polarised light"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Ovoid acellular mesangial nodules of sclerosis at the centre of lobulated glomerular tufts): Long-standing diabetes with retinopathy and proteinuria produces diffuse glomerulosclerosis, and the nodular form is the most specific lesion, called the Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodule. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis of both afferent and efferent arterioles accompanies it. Amyloid can mimic nodules but stains with Congo red and is not the likely cause here, and wire loops indicate lupus."}, {"id": 317, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 31-year-old woman has severe hypertension, a right kidney smaller than the left by 1.5 cm and elevated plasma renin. CT angiography shows alternating segments of narrowing and aneurysmal dilatation in the mid to distal right renal artery. Which pathological process underlies this vascular lesion?", "options": {"A": "Atheromatous plaque at the renal artery origin with intimal thickening and calcification", "B": "Necrotising inflammation of medium arteries with microaneurysms at branch points, associated with hepatitis B", "C": "Granulomatous inflammation of the aorta and its branches with giant cells in the media", "D": "Medial fibroplasia, with irregular fibrous thickening of the media alternating with thinning, sparing the intima"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Medial fibroplasia, with irregular fibrous thickening of the media alternating with thinning, sparing the intima): A young woman with renovascular hypertension and alternating narrowing and dilatation in the distal artery has fibromuscular dysplasia. The commonest histological type is medial fibroplasia, where thickened fibrous media alternate with thinned segments, producing the angiographic chain appearance. Atherosclerosis affects the origin in older patients, and arteritis shows inflammation."}, {"id": 319, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 61-year-old smoker has haematuria and a 9 cm heterogeneously enhancing upper pole renal mass with tumour extending into the renal vein. Histology shows clear cells in nests with delicate vasculature. Which molecular alteration is most typical of this tumour?", "options": {"A": "Loss of VHL tumour suppressor function on chromosome 3p with stabilised hypoxia-inducible factor", "B": "Activating MET proto-oncogene mutation on chromosome 7 driving papillary growth in a hereditary setting", "C": "Germline fumarate hydratase mutation causing accumulation of fumarate in hereditary leiomyomatosis syndrome", "D": "TFE3 gene translocation on the X chromosome in a young patient"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Loss of VHL tumour suppressor function on chromosome 3p with stabilised hypoxia-inducible factor): Clear cell carcinoma is the commonest renal cancer and nearly all cases have inactivation of VHL on 3p, sporadic or from germline von Hippel-Lindau disease. Loss of VHL stabilises hypoxia-inducible factor and drives angiogenic growth factor expression, explaining the vascularity and renal vein extension. MET, fumarate hydratase and TFE3 alterations belong to other subtypes."}, {"id": 322, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 28-year-old woman with epilepsy, intellectual disability and facial angiofibromas presents with sudden left flank pain and hypotension. CT shows bilateral fat-containing renal masses and a large retroperitoneal haematoma. Her haemoglobin has fallen by 30 g/L since admission. What is the mechanism of the bleeding?", "options": {"A": "Rupture of thick-walled aneurysmal vessels lacking an elastic lamina within the tumour", "B": "Spontaneous rupture of a simple cortical cyst after minor trauma", "C": "Haemorrhage into a malignant cystic component of a hereditary clear cell carcinoma", "D": "Necrosis of the tumour with erosion into the main renal vein"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Rupture of thick-walled aneurysmal vessels lacking an elastic lamina within the tumour): Tuberous sclerosis commonly causes multiple bilateral angiomyolipomas, benign tumours of fat, smooth muscle and dysmorphic vessels. The vessels are thick walled and aneurysmal without a normal elastic lamina, so larger tumours can bleed catastrophically into the retroperitoneum, known as Wunderlich syndrome. Clear cell carcinoma rarely contains macroscopic fat."}, {"id": 325, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 37-year-old man with Crohn disease and prior ileal resection has recurrent renal colic. Urine pH is 5.5, urinary calcium is low and urinary oxalate is raised. CT shows several radiopaque stones in the right kidney. Which mechanism most likely explains his stones?", "options": {"A": "Increased intestinal calcium absorption from excess vitamin D leading to hypercalciuria and stones", "B": "Urease-producing bacteria raising urinary pH and precipitating magnesium ammonium phosphate", "C": "Fat malabsorption binds luminal calcium, leaving free oxalate that is absorbed in the colon", "D": "Overproduction of uric acid from purine turnover with persistently acidic urine"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Fat malabsorption binds luminal calcium, leaving free oxalate that is absorbed in the colon): Ileal disease causes fat malabsorption, and unabsorbed fatty acids bind calcium so that oxalate stays soluble and is absorbed across the colon, causing hyperoxaluria and calcium oxalate stones. Low urinary calcium argues against hypercalciuria. Uric acid stones are radiolucent and struvite requires alkaline urine."}, {"id": 328, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 52-year-old diabetic woman has chronic flank pain, fever and weight loss over the past four months. CT shows an enlarged non-functioning kidney with a staghorn calculus and multiple low-density rounded cavities surrounding contracted calyces. Which pathological finding is expected after nephrectomy?", "options": {"A": "Gas within the renal parenchyma from glucose-fermenting Escherichia coli with necrotising suppuration", "B": "Sheets of foamy macrophages with chronic inflammation, linked to obstruction and Proteus infection", "C": "Neutrophilic microabscesses scattered across the cortex with minimal destruction of tubules", "D": "Caseating granulomas with calcification and autoamputation of the renal papillae"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Sheets of foamy macrophages with chronic inflammation, linked to obstruction and Proteus infection): A destroyed kidney with a staghorn stone and a bear-paw arrangement of low-density cavities is xanthogranulomatous pyelonephritis. Chronic obstruction and infection, often with Proteus, lead to sheets of lipid-laden macrophages that can mimic renal cell carcinoma. Gas-forming infection is acute, and tuberculosis shows caseation."}, {"id": 329, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 66-year-old retired textile dye worker with a 40 pack-year smoking history has painless macroscopic haematuria. Cystoscopy shows a papillary tumour on the lateral wall. Urine cytology shows malignant cells. Which agent best explains the occupational contribution to his risk?", "options": {"A": "Chronic Schistosoma haematobium infection with squamous metaplasia of the bladder wall", "B": "Pelvic irradiation for prostate carcinoma decades earlier with delayed urothelial damage", "C": "Aromatic amines such as 2-naphthylamine and benzidine, with tobacco smoke as an additive risk", "D": "Prolonged cyclophosphamide therapy with excretion of the metabolite acrolein in urine"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Aromatic amines such as 2-naphthylamine and benzidine, with tobacco smoke as an additive risk): Aromatic amines used in dye manufacture are classic occupational urothelial carcinogens and act together with cigarette smoking, the most common risk factor. Schistosomiasis causes squamous carcinoma, and cyclophosphamide and radiotherapy are recognised causes but are not suggested by the history."}, {"id": 331, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 41-year-old man has haematuria and passes mucus in the urine. CT shows a midline supravesical mass at the bladder dome extending toward the umbilicus, with punctate calcification, and the surface mucosa is otherwise normal. Which tumour is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Urothelial carcinoma of the dome arising from flat carcinoma in situ", "B": "Squamous cell carcinoma related to chronic catheter-associated irritation of the mucosa", "C": "Leiomyosarcoma arising from the muscularis propria of the anterior bladder wall", "D": "Adenocarcinoma arising from urachal remnant tissue at the dome, often with a colloid pattern"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Adenocarcinoma arising from urachal remnant tissue at the dome, often with a colloid pattern): A midline mass at the dome extending toward the umbilicus with calcification and mucus points to a urachal tumour, which is an adenocarcinoma with a colonic or mucinous pattern in most cases. The overlying mucosa is normal because the tumour arises extramucosally. Urothelial and squamous tumours arise from the lining."}, {"id": 333, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 63-year-old woman has a papillary urothelial carcinoma resected transurethrally from a 2 cm bladder tumour. Pathology shows tumour cells invading the subepithelial connective tissue, with no muscularis propria involvement in the sampled detrusor muscle. Which stage applies, and what is the main clinical consequence?", "options": {"A": "Ta, non-invasive papillary carcinoma with low progression risk and routine cystoscopic surveillance", "B": "T2, muscle-invasive disease needing radical cystectomy with neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "C": "Tis, flat high-grade carcinoma confined to urothelium, treated with intravesical BCG immunotherapy", "D": "T1, with high risk of progression, so repeat resection and intravesical therapy are considered"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (T1, with high risk of progression, so repeat resection and intravesical therapy are considered): Invasion of the lamina propria without muscularis propria involvement is T1. Because T1 high-grade tumours have a significant risk of progression, repeat resection and intravesical therapy are considered. Ta does not breach the basement membrane, Tis is flat disease, and T2 needs muscle invasion."}, {"id": 335, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 66-year-old man has a PSA of 12 ng/mL. Biopsy cores show adenocarcinoma with a predominant component of fused glands and a smaller component of well-formed discrete glands, the fused glands making up 70 percent of the tumour. Which ISUP grade group applies?", "options": {"A": "Grade group 2, corresponding to Gleason score 3+4=7 with a predominant pattern 3", "B": "Grade group 4, corresponding to Gleason score 4+4=8 with no pattern 3 component", "C": "Grade group 3, corresponding to Gleason score 4+3=7 with a predominant pattern 4", "D": "Grade group 1, corresponding to Gleason score 3+3=6 with well-formed glands throughout"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Grade group 3, corresponding to Gleason score 4+3=7 with a predominant pattern 4): Fused glands are Gleason pattern 4 and well-formed discrete glands are pattern 3. With pattern 4 predominant the score is 4+3=7, which is grade group 3, whereas 3+4=7 is group 2. Group 4 needs a score of 8, and group 1 is score 6 or less."}, {"id": 338, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 71-year-old man with prostate adenocarcinoma has an MRI showing tumour breaching the capsule and a low-signal mass replacing the left seminal vesicle. The rectal wall is spared, the bladder neck is clear and pelvic nodes appear normal. What is the clinical T category?", "options": {"A": "T3b, since tumour has invaded the seminal vesicle beyond the gland", "B": "T2c, since tumour is organ confined but involves both lobes of the gland", "C": "T3a, since tumour shows extraprostatic extension limited to periprostatic fat or bladder neck", "D": "T4, since tumour is fixed or invades adjacent structures other than the seminal vesicles"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (T3b, since tumour has invaded the seminal vesicle beyond the gland): Extension beyond the capsule is T3, and seminal vesicle invasion defines T3b. T3a is extraprostatic extension without seminal vesicle involvement. T2 is organ confined, and T4 requires fixation or invasion of structures such as the rectum, levator muscles or pelvic wall."}, {"id": 339, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 74-year-old man has progressive back pain, weight loss and a PSA of 480 ng/mL. Radiographs of the lumbar spine and pelvis show multiple dense ill-defined lesions. Which statement best explains the pathology and route of spread to the vertebrae?", "options": {"A": "Osteolytic metastases seeded via lymphatic channels to red marrow with osteoclast-driven bone resorption", "B": "Osteoblastic metastases seeded via the valveless vertebral venous plexus connecting with pelvic veins", "C": "Direct arterial embolisation to metaphyseal marrow producing mixed lytic lesions with cortical destruction", "D": "Reactive periosteal bone formation due to paraneoplastic hypertrophic osteoarthropathy without bone invasion"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Osteoblastic metastases seeded via the valveless vertebral venous plexus connecting with pelvic veins): Prostate cancer characteristically forms sclerotic osteoblastic metastases in the axial skeleton. Tumour cells travel retrograde through the valveless Batson vertebral venous plexus, which connects with the prostatic and pelvic veins. Osteoblastic reaction is driven by tumour factors that stimulate new bone formation."}, {"id": 340, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 33-year-old man has a painless testicular swelling. Ultrasound shows a homogeneous hypoechoic mass without calcification or cysts, and serum alpha-fetoprotein is normal while placental alkaline phosphatase is raised. The orchidectomy specimen is a lobulated, cream-coloured homogeneous mass. Which microscopic appearance is characteristic?", "options": {"A": "Cartilage, neural tissue and glandular epithelium arranged haphazardly around cysts", "B": "Reticular pattern with perivascular papillae resembling glomeruli surrounded by a space", "C": "Glands and papillary structures lined by anaplastic cells with extensive haemorrhage and necrosis", "D": "Sheets of uniform polygonal clear cells divided by fibrous septa infiltrated by lymphocytes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Sheets of uniform polygonal clear cells divided by fibrous septa infiltrated by lymphocytes): A uniform, cream, fleshy tumour with normal alpha-fetoprotein in a man in the third to fourth decade fits seminoma. Histology shows nests and sheets of large uniform cells with clear cytoplasm separated by fibrous septa containing lymphocytes. Necrosis and haemorrhage suggest embryonal carcinoma, and glomeruloid structures indicate yolk sac tumour."}, {"id": 344, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 30-year-old man, evaluated at a fertility clinic, with cryptorchidism and infertility has a testicular biopsy adjacent to a seminoma. Tubules contain large atypical germ cells with clear cytoplasm lying along the tubular basement membrane, positive for OCT3/4. Which precursor lesion is this?", "options": {"A": "Intratubular spermatocytic proliferation, the precursor of spermatocytic tumour in older men", "B": "Leydig cell hyperplasia, with sheets of eosinophilic cells containing Reinke crystals between tubules", "C": "Sertoli cell-restricted pattern, with absent germ cells and thickened tubular basement membranes", "D": "Germ cell neoplasia in situ, the precursor of most post-pubertal germ cell tumours"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Germ cell neoplasia in situ, the precursor of most post-pubertal germ cell tumours): Atypical OCT3/4-positive germ cells along the basement membrane of tubules are germ cell neoplasia in situ. It precedes most post-pubertal seminomas and non-seminomatous tumours, and cryptorchidism and infertility are risk factors. Spermatocytic tumour arises independently and Leydig and Sertoli changes do not contain atypical germ cells."}, {"id": 353, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 38-year-old woman has a tender neck, fever and malaise two weeks after an upper respiratory infection. Thyroid uptake on scintigraphy is very low, and ESR is raised. Biopsy would show which of the following histological findings in the thyroid?", "options": {"A": "Granulomas with multinucleate giant cells around disrupted follicles and a mixed inflammatory infiltrate", "B": "Dense hyalinised fibrosis replacing the gland and invading adjacent muscle with eosinophils and plasma cells", "C": "Lymphoid follicles with germinal centres and oncocytic follicular cells with large hyperchromatic nuclei", "D": "Diffuse follicular hyperplasia with scalloped colloid and tall columnar epithelium lining the follicles"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Granulomas with multinucleate giant cells around disrupted follicles and a mixed inflammatory infiltrate): A painful gland with raised ESR and suppressed uptake after a viral illness is de Quervain thyroiditis, with colloid granulomas and giant cells. Lymphoid follicles with Hurthle cells are Hashimoto thyroiditis, which is painless. Hyperplasia with scalloped colloid is Graves disease, which has high uptake. Dense fibrosis with muscle invasion is Riedel thyroiditis."}, {"id": 357, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 35-year-old man has a firm thyroid nodule at the junction of the upper and middle thirds of the lobe, with coarse calcification and calcified nodal metastases. Calcitonin is markedly raised. His sister had a similar tumour. Which germline abnormality is most likely?", "options": {"A": "Activating point mutation of the RET proto-oncogene, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern", "B": "Inactivating mutation of the MEN1 gene encoding menin on chromosome 11 with loss of tumour suppression", "C": "Activating BRAF V600E mutation producing constitutive MAPK signalling in thyroid follicular epithelium", "D": "Loss of function mutation in the APC gene producing numerous colonic polyps and thyroid cribriform tumours"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Activating point mutation of the RET proto-oncogene, inherited in an autosomal dominant pattern): A calcitonin secreting tumour in the upper thyroid lobes, where C cells concentrate, with a family history indicates hereditary medullary carcinoma from activating RET mutations (MEN2). MEN1 involves parathyroid, pituitary and pancreatic tumours. APC mutation causes a cribriform morular papillary variant, not a calcitonin tumour. BRAF V600E is a somatic papillary carcinoma driver."}, {"id": 366, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A newborn girl has ambiguous genitalia, vomiting and poor feeding on day 7. Her serum results are shown. Pelvic ultrasound shows a uterus and ovaries, with both adrenal glands enlarged and a cerebriform surface pattern. Which enzyme deficiency is most likely?", "options": {"A": "11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency causing deoxycorticosterone excess, hypertension and virilisation with hypokalaemia", "B": "3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency causing salt wasting with incomplete masculinisation in boys and mild virilisation in girls", "C": "21-hydroxylase deficiency, impairing cortisol and aldosterone synthesis and shunting precursors toward androgen production", "D": "17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency causing hypertension, hypokalaemia and absent sex steroid production with female phenotype"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (21-hydroxylase deficiency, impairing cortisol and aldosterone synthesis and shunting precursors toward androgen production): Virilisation of a 46,XX infant with salt wasting is classic salt wasting 21-hydroxylase deficiency, the commonest cause of congenital adrenal hyperplasia, with ACTH driven bilateral adrenal enlargement. 11-beta-hydroxylase deficiency causes hypertension and hypokalaemia. 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency gives only mild virilisation of girls. 17-alpha-hydroxylase deficiency gives hypertension and absent virilisation.", "image": "question_images/q_366.png"}, {"id": 368, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 19-year-old develops fever, a purpuric rash and profound hypotension. CT shows bilateral enlarged adrenal glands with high attenuation and peri-adrenal stranding. Adrenal insufficiency is suspected. Blood cultures are pending at the time of imaging. Which organism classically causes the underlying adrenal syndrome?", "options": {"A": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa sepsis causing adrenal infarction from direct vascular invasion by bacilli and thrombosis", "B": "Streptococcus pyogenes infection causing toxic shock with bilateral adrenal vein thrombosis", "C": "Staphylococcus aureus bacteraemia causing bilateral adrenal abscess formation with cortical suppuration", "D": "Neisseria meningitidis septicaemia causing disseminated intravascular coagulation and adrenal haemorrhage"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Neisseria meningitidis septicaemia causing disseminated intravascular coagulation and adrenal haemorrhage): Fulminant meningococcal septicaemia with purpura and bilateral adrenal haemorrhage defines Waterhouse-Friderichsen syndrome, with DIC causing haemorrhagic necrosis of the cortex. Staphylococcus and pseudomonas can cause shock but are not the classic cause. Streptococcal toxic shock is not classically linked to adrenal haemorrhage. Imaging shows enlarged high attenuation glands."}, {"id": 370, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 24-year-old man has a 6 cm extra-adrenal retroperitoneal paraganglioma near the aortic bifurcation and raised normetanephrine. Several months later, he develops sclerotic bone lesions. Family history is unremarkable. Which germline gene mutation is most strongly associated with this behaviour?", "options": {"A": "VHL mutation, associated with predominantly noradrenergic tumours that are usually benign and bilateral", "B": "NF1 mutation, associated with neurofibromas and typically solitary benign adrenal tumours in older adults", "C": "RET mutation, associated with predominantly adrenaline-secreting bilateral adrenal tumours that are rarely malignant", "D": "SDHB mutation, associated with succinate dehydrogenase loss and a high risk of metastatic disease"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (SDHB mutation, associated with succinate dehydrogenase loss and a high risk of metastatic disease): Extra-adrenal paragangliomas with metastases in a young patient strongly suggest an SDHB germline mutation, which carries the highest malignant potential among the hereditary forms. Malignancy is defined by metastasis to sites without chromaffin tissue, such as bone. NF1 tumours are usually benign adrenal tumours. VHL and RET tumours are usually benign bilateral adrenal tumours."}, {"id": 376, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 58-year-old woman has weight loss, new diabetes and a migratory erythematous rash with blistering around the groin and perineum. CT shows a 6 cm hypervascular mass in the pancreatic tail with liver metastases. Which hormone is most likely secreted by the tumour?", "options": {"A": "Vasoactive intestinal peptide, causing watery diarrhoea, hypokalaemia and achlorhydria with secretory diabetes in some patients", "B": "Pancreatic polypeptide, which usually produces no clear syndrome but may cause mild diabetes and weight loss", "C": "Somatostatin secreted by delta cells, causing diabetes, gallstones and steatorrhoea through inhibited insulin release", "D": "Glucagon secreted by pancreatic alpha cells, causing hyperglycaemia through increased hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Glucagon secreted by pancreatic alpha cells, causing hyperglycaemia through increased hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis): Necrolytic migratory erythema with diabetes and weight loss indicates a glucagonoma, typically large, in the pancreatic tail and often metastatic at diagnosis. Somatostatinoma causes the diabetes, gallstones and steatorrhoea triad without a rash. VIPoma gives watery diarrhoea. Pancreatic polypeptide tumours do not produce this skin syndrome."}, {"id": 379, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 58-year-old man with type 2 diabetes for 18 years has persistent albuminuria and rising creatinine. Renal ultrasound is normal in size and echogenicity. Blood pressure is 160/95 mmHg. Biopsy shows mesangial nodules. Which additional change is most characteristic of this glomerular lesion?", "options": {"A": "Subepithelial immune complex humps with spikes on silver stain and granular immunofluorescence for IgG", "B": "Crescent formation with fibrin deposition and rupture of the glomerular basement membrane", "C": "Apple-green birefringent Congo red deposits in mesangium with fibrils about ten nanometres thick", "D": "Hyaline thickening of afferent and efferent arterioles with diffusely thickened glomerular basement membranes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hyaline thickening of afferent and efferent arterioles with diffusely thickened glomerular basement membranes): Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules sit alongside diffuse glomerular basement membrane thickening and hyaline arteriolosclerosis of both afferent and efferent arterioles, a pattern more specific for diabetes than hypertension alone. Subepithelial humps with spikes are membranous nephropathy. Crescents indicate rapidly progressive disease. Congo red positive fibrils define amyloidosis, a nodular mimic."}, {"id": 383, "domain": "genitourinary_adrenal_and_endocrine", "question": "A 12-year-old child presents in diabetic ketoacidosis with positive anti-GAD antibodies. Blood glucose is 28 mmol/L at presentation. Pancreatic biopsy from a research protocol is performed in the early phase of disease. Which islet histology would be expected at this stage?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse acinar and islet destruction by lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate rich in IgG4 positive plasma cells", "B": "Insulitis with CD8 positive T cell infiltration and selective loss of beta cells", "C": "Islet amyloid deposition from islet amyloid polypeptide with beta cell loss and preserved alpha cells", "D": "Islet hyperplasia with enlarged hypergranulated beta cells from sustained insulin resistance and compensation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Insulitis with CD8 positive T cell infiltration and selective loss of beta cells): Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune disease with CD8 T cell insulitis that selectively destroys beta cells. Islet amyloid and compensatory hyperplasia are changes of type 2 diabetes. Autoimmune pancreatitis shows IgG4 rich lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate of ducts and acini and does not selectively remove beta cells."}, {"id": 386, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 63-year-old woman has abdominal distension and a raised CA-125. Imaging shows bilateral complex solid-cystic adnexal masses, omental caking and ascites. Her sister had breast cancer at 45. Biopsy shows papillary and solid growth with marked nuclear pleomorphism and brisk mitotic activity. Which molecular abnormality is most characteristic of this tumour?", "options": {"A": "Activating KRAS or BRAF mutation in an indolent tumour with uniform, low-grade nuclei", "B": "FOXL2 point mutation in a sex cord stromal tumour forming Call-Exner bodies", "C": "Loss of ARID1A with PIK3CA mutation in a tumour arising from endometriosis", "D": "Near universal TP53 mutation, with BRCA pathway defects in a minority"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Near universal TP53 mutation, with BRCA pathway defects in a minority): Bilateral solid-cystic masses with omental disease and marked atypia indicate high-grade serous carcinoma, which carries TP53 mutation in nearly every case and homologous recombination defects including BRCA. KRAS and BRAF belong to low-grade serous tumours with bland nuclei. ARID1A and PIK3CA changes mark clear cell and endometrioid carcinomas. FOXL2 is the signature of adult granulosa cell tumour."}, {"id": 398, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 25-year-old woman presents with acute pelvic pain and vomiting. Ultrasound shows an enlarged 8 cm oedematous left ovary with multiple peripheral follicles, free fluid and a twisted vascular pedicle. Arterial Doppler flow is still detectable. Which pathological sequence best explains the ovarian enlargement?", "options": {"A": "Inflammatory infiltration from pelvic infection, causing a tubo-ovarian abscess with ovarian swelling", "B": "Venous and lymphatic obstruction first, causing oedema and haemorrhage before arterial flow stops", "C": "Arterial occlusion first, causing early coagulative necrosis with an unchanged, small ovarian volume", "D": "Primary follicular haemorrhage within a corpus luteum, causing expansion before any pedicle twisting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Venous and lymphatic obstruction first, causing oedema and haemorrhage before arterial flow stops): The thin-walled veins and lymphatics are compressed before the thick-walled arteries, so the ovary becomes swollen and oedematous with peripheral follicles, and preserved Doppler arterial flow does not exclude torsion. Early arterial occlusion would give a small infarcted ovary. Haemorrhage is secondary. A tubo-ovarian abscess would show a thick enhancing wall and fever."}, {"id": 403, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 33-year-old woman with previous pelvic inflammatory disease has infertility. Pelvic MRI shows a tubular, convoluted, thin-walled fluid-filled adnexal structure with incomplete folds along its inner wall, and no enhancing wall thickening. She has no fever. Which pathology best explains this structure?", "options": {"A": "Tubal carcinoma with papillary soft tissue and fluid retention from outflow obstruction", "B": "Tubo-ovarian abscess with thick enhancing wall and purulent exudate involving the adjacent ovary", "C": "Paratubal cyst arising from Müllerian remnants with a thin wall, separate from the tube", "D": "Distal tubal occlusion after chronic salpingitis with retained serous fluid"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Distal tubal occlusion after chronic salpingitis with retained serous fluid): A tubular fluid-filled structure with incomplete folds and a thin wall is hydrosalpinx, the end result of chronic salpingitis with fimbrial scarring and sealing of the distal tube. A tubo-ovarian abscess has a thick enhancing wall and acute features. A paratubal cyst is separate from the tube and lacks folds. Tubal carcinoma would contain enhancing solid tissue."}, {"id": 405, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 29-year-old woman with polycystic ovary syndrome has had three menses in two years. Her BMI is 35 and fasting insulin is elevated. Endometrial sampling shows crowded glands with architectural complexity but no cytological atypia. Which mechanism most directly explains this endometrial change?", "options": {"A": "Chronic anovulation with unopposed oestrogen, lacking cyclical progesterone to induce secretory change", "B": "Raised androgen levels directly stimulating endometrial gland proliferation through androgen receptor mediated mitosis", "C": "Hyperinsulinaemia suppressing sex hormone binding globulin and so lowering free oestradiol available to tissue", "D": "Elevated progesterone from luteinised stroma producing glandular crowding without a preceding ovulatory event"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chronic anovulation with unopposed oestrogen, lacking cyclical progesterone to induce secretory change): Anovulation leaves the endometrium exposed to oestrogen without a luteal progesterone phase, giving proliferative change, hyperplasia and a higher carcinoma risk. Androgens do not drive gland proliferation directly. Hyperinsulinaemia lowers sex hormone binding globulin and raises, not lowers, free oestradiol. Progesterone would induce secretory change and limit growth."}, {"id": 410, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 30-year-old woman has a 3 cm cystic lesion on the anterolateral vaginal wall. MRI shows a smooth, thin-walled T2-hyperintense cyst. She has known ipsilateral renal agenesis. She has no urinary symptoms. Which embryological structure is the source of the cyst?", "options": {"A": "Paramesonephric (Müllerian) duct remnant, lined by mucinous columnar epithelium", "B": "Mesonephric (Wolffian) duct remnant, lined by non-mucinous cuboidal epithelium", "C": "Skene gland duct, obstructed and lined by stratified squamous epithelium", "D": "Urogenital sinus remnant, lined by transitional epithelium of the bladder type"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Mesonephric (Wolffian) duct remnant, lined by non-mucinous cuboidal epithelium): A cyst along the anterolateral vaginal wall with ipsilateral renal agenesis points to a Gartner duct cyst from the mesonephric duct, whose maldevelopment also disrupts the ureteric bud. It has a cuboidal, non-mucinous lining. Müllerian cysts have mucinous or tubal-type lining. A Skene gland cyst lies beside the urethral meatus."}, {"id": 411, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 19-year-old woman, whose mother took diethylstilboestrol in pregnancy, has abnormal vaginal bleeding. A 3 cm polypoid mass is biopsied from the anterior upper vaginal wall. Histology shows clear and hobnail cells in tubulocystic and papillary patterns. Which precursor lesion is characteristic of this tumour?", "options": {"A": "Endometriosis within the rectovaginal septum showing clear cell change of the glands", "B": "Gartner duct remnant lined by cuboidal mesonephric epithelium along the lateral wall", "C": "Vaginal intraepithelial neoplasia grade 3 associated with persistent high-risk HPV infection", "D": "Vaginal adenosis, with Müllerian glandular epithelium replacing the normal squamous mucosa"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Vaginal adenosis, with Müllerian glandular epithelium replacing the normal squamous mucosa): Clear cell adenocarcinoma of the vagina in a young woman exposed in utero to diethylstilboestrol arises in vaginal adenosis, in which Müllerian glandular epithelium persists in the upper vagina. Squamous intraepithelial neoplasia is HPV-related and squamous. Gartner remnants are mesonephric. Endometriosis-related clear cell carcinoma occurs in the ovary in older women."}, {"id": 412, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 35-year-old woman with no prior abnormal results has a high-grade cervical lesion on biopsy, and molecular testing detects high-risk HPV type 16 DNA integrated into host cells. Which mechanism most directly explains how viral oncoproteins promote carcinogenesis in the transformed squamous epithelium?", "options": {"A": "Viral integration causes loss of p16 expression, removing inhibition of cyclin-dependent kinases", "B": "E6 inactivates the retinoblastoma protein, and E7 promotes degradation of p53 through ubiquitination", "C": "E6 promotes degradation of p53, and E7 inactivates the retinoblastoma protein, driving cell cycle entry", "D": "E2 overexpression activates RAS signalling, producing proliferation without any need for tumour suppressor loss"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (E6 promotes degradation of p53, and E7 inactivates the retinoblastoma protein, driving cell cycle entry): E6 binds p53 and targets it for ubiquitin-mediated degradation, while E7 binds and inactivates Rb, releasing E2F and driving the cell cycle. The targets are not interchangeable. Loss of Rb function causes p16 to be strongly overexpressed, which is why block-positive p16 is a surrogate marker. HPV oncogenesis does not depend on RAS activation."}, {"id": 415, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 52-year-old woman has watery vaginal discharge. MRI shows a deeply infiltrating cervical mass with small cystic spaces in the stroma. Cytology and high-risk HPV testing have been repeatedly negative. Histology shows mucinous glands with abundant pale foamy cytoplasm, distinct cell borders and minimal nuclear atypia. Which diagnosis fits best?", "options": {"A": "Usual-type endocervical adenocarcinoma, associated with high-risk HPV 16 and 18 and block-positive p16", "B": "Clear cell carcinoma, arising in women exposed to diethylstilboestrol with hobnail cells", "C": "Gastric-type endocervical adenocarcinoma, an HPV-independent tumour with an aggressive course", "D": "Mesonephric adenocarcinoma, arising from Wolffian remnants with eosinophilic secretions and GATA3 positivity"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Gastric-type endocervical adenocarcinoma, an HPV-independent tumour with an aggressive course): Negative HPV testing, deep stromal infiltration and mucinous glands with foamy cytoplasm and bland nuclei indicate gastric-type adenocarcinoma, an HPV-independent endocervical tumour that is often missed on cytology and has a poor prognosis. Usual-type disease is HPV-driven and p16 positive. Mesonephric carcinoma shows eosinophilic secretions and GATA3. Clear cell carcinoma has hobnail cells."}, {"id": 417, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 47-year-old woman has a 5 cm cervical squamous carcinoma. MRI shows disruption of the low-signal cervical stromal ring, with soft tissue extending into the parametrium but not to the pelvic sidewall. The lower third of the vagina is uninvolved and there is no hydronephrosis. What is the FIGO 2018 stage?", "options": {"A": "Stage IB3, because stromal ring disruption remains confined within the cervical stroma", "B": "Stage IIB, because of parametrial involvement that stops short of the pelvic sidewall", "C": "Stage IIIB, because stromal ring disruption indicates extension to the pelvic sidewall", "D": "Stage IIA2, because the tumour exceeds 4 cm and involves the upper vagina"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stage IIB, because of parametrial involvement that stops short of the pelvic sidewall): Tumour extending beyond the cervix into the parametrium without reaching the pelvic sidewall, the lower vagina or causing hydronephrosis is stage IIB. IIA involves the upper two thirds of the vagina without parametrial invasion. IIIB needs sidewall extension or hydronephrosis. IB3 is confined to the cervix, which stromal ring disruption with parametrial soft tissue excludes."}, {"id": 418, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 55-year-old woman with biopsy-proven cervical carcinoma has new right hydronephrosis on CT. The tumour does not reach the pelvic sidewall on examination or MRI, and the lower vagina is uninvolved. Which FIGO 2018 stage applies, and what mechanism explains the hydronephrosis?", "options": {"A": "Stage IIIA, because hydronephrosis reflects extension to the lower third of the vagina", "B": "Stage IIIB, from ureteric obstruction by parametrial tumour or compressing lymph nodes", "C": "Stage IIB, because hydronephrosis does not change the stage unless the sidewall is reached", "D": "Stage IVA, because hydronephrosis indicates tumour invasion of the bladder mucosa"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Stage IIIB, from ureteric obstruction by parametrial tumour or compressing lymph nodes): FIGO 2018 classifies extension to the pelvic sidewall and any hydronephrosis or nonfunctioning kidney, not explained by another cause, as stage IIIB, because ureteric obstruction implies parametrial or nodal tumour reaching the pelvic wall. Stage IVA requires biopsy-proven mucosal invasion of bladder or rectum. Stage IIIA concerns the lower third of the vagina."}, {"id": 421, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 58-year-old obese postmenopausal woman has abnormal uterine bleeding. Pelvic ultrasound shows a diffusely thickened endometrium measuring 14 mm. Curettage reveals crowded glands with cytological atypia and minimal intervening stroma, without stromal invasion. What is the most important clinical implication of this histological diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Progression to serous carcinoma through an early TP53 mutation is the usual outcome", "B": "Spread to pelvic lymph nodes is expected, so lymphadenectomy is required at diagnosis", "C": "The lesion mimics carcinoma and regresses with sustained weight reduction in many patients", "D": "Many patients have a coexisting endometrioid carcinoma in the hysterectomy specimen"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Many patients have a coexisting endometrioid carcinoma in the hysterectomy specimen): Atypical hyperplasia (endometrioid intraepithelial neoplasia) is the precursor of endometrioid carcinoma, and a sizeable fraction of biopsies hide invasive cancer elsewhere in the uterus. For this reason hysterectomy specimens are examined carefully. Serous carcinoma arises by a different, TP53 driven route from atrophic endometrium. Nodal spread belongs to invasive disease, not to a lesion without stromal invasion."}, {"id": 425, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 41-year-old woman has heavy menstrual bleeding. At hysterectomy the myometrium contains a sharply circumscribed, firm, whorled grey-white nodule 5 cm across that bulges from the cut surface. Histology shows interlacing fascicles of bland spindle cells without necrosis. Which genetic abnormality is most commonly found in this lesion?", "options": {"A": "JAZF1-SUZ12 gene fusion producing a chimeric transcription factor in the lesion", "B": "Loss of RB1 together with TP53 mutation and a complex karyotype", "C": "Germline fumarate hydratase mutation explaining most sporadic examples of this lesion", "D": "Somatic mutation of the MED12 gene in a large proportion of cases"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Somatic mutation of the MED12 gene in a large proportion of cases): Most sporadic leiomyomas carry MED12 exon 2 mutations. The JAZF1-SUZ12 fusion belongs to low grade endometrial stromal sarcoma. Complex karyotypes with RB1 and TP53 loss are typical of leiomyosarcoma. Fumarate hydratase mutations underlie hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell carcinoma but account for a small minority."}, {"id": 428, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 52-year-old postmenopausal woman has a rapidly enlarging pelvic mass over three months. MRI shows a large uterine mass with irregular margins, heterogeneous T2 signal, haemorrhage and non-enhancing necrosis. Which histological combination most reliably establishes a malignant smooth muscle tumour?", "options": {"A": "Diffuse moderate to severe nuclear atypia, brisk mitotic activity, and coagulative tumour cell necrosis", "B": "Spindle cells in fascicles with bland nuclei and absent mitotic figures throughout the tumour", "C": "Hyaline degeneration, cystic change and calcification within a circumscribed whorled nodule", "D": "Intravascular proliferation of cells resembling proliferative endometrial stroma with minimal nuclear atypia"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Diffuse moderate to severe nuclear atypia, brisk mitotic activity, and coagulative tumour cell necrosis): Leiomyosarcoma is diagnosed on at least two of three features: significant atypia, high mitotic count and tumour cell necrosis with a coagulative pattern. Degenerative changes such as hyaline change are common in fibroids and not diagnostic. Bland fascicles with no mitoses describe an ordinary leiomyoma. Intravascular stromal-like cells indicate endometrial stromal sarcoma."}, {"id": 430, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 71-year-old woman presents with postmenopausal bleeding and a bulky polypoid uterine mass protruding through the cervical os. Histology shows high-grade glandular elements admixed with malignant spindle cells, some with rhabdomyoblastic differentiation. Which statement best describes current understanding of this tumour?", "options": {"A": "It is a leiomyosarcoma variant showing heterologous elements arising from smooth muscle cells", "B": "It is a collision of two independent clonal tumours arising from different cell lineages", "C": "It is a metaplastic carcinoma in which the spindle component derives from epithelial cells", "D": "It is an endometrial stromal sarcoma with secondary entrapment of benign endometrial glands"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (It is a metaplastic carcinoma in which the spindle component derives from epithelial cells): Molecular studies show that the epithelial and mesenchymal components share common mutations, supporting monoclonal origin as a carcinoma that has undergone epithelial to mesenchymal transition. The old collision theory has been abandoned. High-grade malignant glands exclude entrapped benign glands, and the glandular component excludes a pure leiomyosarcoma. It is staged and treated as a high grade carcinoma."}, {"id": 431, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 44-year-old woman is diagnosed with endometrioid endometrial carcinoma. Her mother had colon cancer at 46 and a maternal aunt had ovarian cancer. Immunohistochemistry shows loss of MSH2 and MSH6 with retained MLH1 and PMS2. What is the most likely underlying cause?", "options": {"A": "Somatic POLE exonuclease domain mutation giving an ultramutated phenotype with excellent prognosis", "B": "Somatic MLH1 promoter hypermethylation as seen in sporadic microsatellite unstable tumours", "C": "Germline mutation in a DNA mismatch repair gene, causing a hereditary cancer syndrome", "D": "Germline BRCA1 mutation causing homologous recombination deficiency in the tumour cells"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Germline mutation in a DNA mismatch repair gene, causing a hereditary cancer syndrome): Loss of MSH2 and MSH6 with preserved MLH1 and PMS2 points to a germline mismatch repair defect, as in Lynch syndrome, and this is confirmed by germline testing. Sporadic mismatch repair loss usually involves MLH1 methylation with PMS2 loss as well. BRCA1 defects do not alter mismatch repair staining. POLE mutated tumours keep normal mismatch repair protein expression."}, {"id": 432, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 60-year-old woman who has taken tamoxifen for four years after breast cancer presents with vaginal spotting and mild pelvic discomfort. Ultrasound shows a thickened endometrium with multiple small cystic spaces. Which statement best explains the effect of tamoxifen on the uterus?", "options": {"A": "Anti-oestrogen effect causes uniform atrophy, so spotting is explained by a benign process", "B": "Progesterone antagonism leads to gland hypersecretion and decidual change in the stroma", "C": "Direct genotoxic metabolite accumulation leads to endometrial stromal sarcoma in treated women", "D": "Partial oestrogen agonist activity in the endometrium promotes polyps, hyperplasia and cancer"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Partial oestrogen agonist activity in the endometrium promotes polyps, hyperplasia and cancer): Tamoxifen blocks oestrogen receptors in breast tissue but acts as a partial agonist in the endometrium, so polyps, hyperplasia and carcinoma are more frequent. Cystic change is common but does not establish a benign process, so persistent bleeding needs sampling. Stromal sarcoma and decidual change are not the recognised mechanisms."}, {"id": 434, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 24-year-old woman at 10 weeks of amenorrhoea has vaginal bleeding and a uterus larger than dates. Serum beta-hCG is markedly elevated. Ultrasound shows an echogenic mass with numerous small cystic spaces and no fetus. Which genetic composition is most typical of this lesion?", "options": {"A": "Diploid 46,XX karyotype with one maternal and one paternal haploid set", "B": "Trisomy 16 resulting from maternal meiotic nondisjunction in the oocyte", "C": "Triploid 69,XXY karyotype with two paternal and one maternal haploid set", "D": "Diploid 46,XX karyotype in which the nuclear DNA is entirely paternal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Diploid 46,XX karyotype in which the nuclear DNA is entirely paternal): Complete moles are androgenetic: an empty egg is fertilised by one sperm that duplicates, or occasionally by two sperm, giving 46,XX or 46,XY with entirely paternal nuclear DNA. Triploidy characterises partial mole. A biparental diploid set is normal. Trisomy 16 is a frequent cause of spontaneous miscarriage but does not produce diffuse villous swelling."}, {"id": 435, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 31-year-old woman has a missed miscarriage at 11 weeks. Curettage shows two populations of villi, some enlarged with cavitation and others normal, with focal trophoblastic hyperplasia and fetal red cells in vessels. Immunostaining for p57 is positive in villous cytotrophoblast. Which cytogenetic finding is expected?", "options": {"A": "Triploid karyotype resulting from fertilisation of one egg by two sperm", "B": "Diploid androgenetic karyotype from duplication of a single sperm genome", "C": "Tetraploid karyotype from failed cytokinesis of the fertilised egg", "D": "Monosomy X from loss of the paternal sex chromosome after fertilisation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Triploid karyotype resulting from fertilisation of one egg by two sperm): Partial moles are usually triploid, with two paternal and one maternal haploid set, and retain maternal DNA, so p57 is positive. Fetal tissue or fetal red cells may be present. A complete mole is androgenetic and p57 negative. Monosomy X typically causes early loss with hydrops, and tetraploidy is uncommon."}, {"id": 436, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 28-year-old woman has persistent bleeding after evacuation of a complete mole. Serial beta-hCG results are plotted. Chest radiograph shows multiple pulmonary nodules. A uterine biopsy shows sheets of atypical cells with extensive haemorrhage and necrosis, and no chorionic villi. Which histological feature is characteristic?", "options": {"A": "Monomorphic intermediate trophoblast infiltrating myometrial fibres with abundant human placental lactogen staining", "B": "A biphasic population of mononuclear cytotrophoblast and multinucleated syncytiotrophoblast cells", "C": "Epithelioid trophoblast forming nodules with eosinophilic hyalinised material and squamous-like appearance", "D": "Hydropic villi with circumferential trophoblastic hyperplasia and stromal oedema"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (A biphasic population of mononuclear cytotrophoblast and multinucleated syncytiotrophoblast cells): Choriocarcinoma consists of cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast without villi, with haemorrhage and necrosis, and it spreads through the blood to lungs, so hCG stays high. Placental site trophoblastic tumour has intermediate trophoblast and human placental lactogen. Hydropic villi indicate a mole or invasive mole. Epithelioid trophoblastic tumour forms nodules of intermediate trophoblast.", "image": "question_images/q_436.png"}, {"id": 439, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 27-year-old woman with a previous chlamydial pelvic infection presents at 7 weeks of amenorrhoea with sudden severe lower abdominal pain and shock. Ultrasound shows free pelvic fluid and an empty uterus. Salpingectomy is performed. Which tubal change most likely predisposed to this event?", "options": {"A": "Congenital absence of the fimbrial end of the fallopian tube with blind ending", "B": "Endometriosis lining the tubal lumen with complete obliteration of the lumen", "C": "Mucosal scarring with fusion of the plicae from prior salpingitis, delaying ovum transport", "D": "Ciliary hyperactivity from progestogen exposure that accelerates embryo transport through the tube"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Mucosal scarring with fusion of the plicae from prior salpingitis, delaying ovum transport): Chronic salpingitis scars and fuses the mucosal folds and damages cilia, so the fertilised ovum is trapped and implants in the tube, usually the ampulla. Congenital tubal anomalies are rare. Progestogen exposure slows rather than speeds transport. Complete obliteration prevents fertilisation instead of allowing implantation."}, {"id": 445, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 33-year-old cocaine user at 35 weeks has sudden painful vaginal bleeding with a rigid tender uterus. Ultrasound shows a retroplacental echogenic collection. The delivered placenta has an adherent organised clot indenting the maternal surface. Which pathogenesis is most direct?", "options": {"A": "Implantation over the internal os with bleeding from the lower uterine segment", "B": "Tear of fetal vessels running within the membranes across the lower segment over the cervical os", "C": "Rupture of maternal decidual vessels with haemorrhage that strips the placenta from its bed", "D": "Rupture of a previous uterine scar with extrusion of the fetus into the peritoneum"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Rupture of maternal decidual vessels with haemorrhage that strips the placenta from its bed): Abruption follows rupture of decidual arterioles, often with hypertension, cocaine or trauma, and an expanding retroplacental clot detaches the placenta and leaves an indentation on the maternal surface. Vasa praevia and placenta praevia give painless bleeding. Uterine scar rupture presents with loss of fetal station and abdominal pain, not a retroplacental clot."}, {"id": 448, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A term placenta from a hypertensive mother has a firm, sharply demarcated, wedge-shaped area based on the maternal surface, red when fresh and later pale tan. Microscopy shows crowded villi with ghost outlines and loss of nuclear staining. What is the underlying mechanism?", "options": {"A": "Massive perivillous fibrin deposition entrapping villi within a dense lattice of fibrinoid material", "B": "Interruption of maternal spiral artery supply with ischaemic necrosis of downstream villi", "C": "Ascending infection with neutrophils filling the intervillous space and the villi", "D": "Fetal stem vessel thrombosis with distal avascular villi in the same territory"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Interruption of maternal spiral artery supply with ischaemic necrosis of downstream villi): A wedge shaped infarct with the base on the maternal surface follows occlusion of a maternal spiral artery, commonly in hypertension or pre-eclampsia, and the villi undergo coagulative necrosis. Fetal thrombotic vasculopathy affects fetal vessels with avascular villi but not in a maternal wedge. Infection produces neutrophils, and massive perivillous fibrin deposition has a different pattern."}, {"id": 449, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has polyhydramnios at 30 weeks and a well-circumscribed 5 cm round mass in the placenta near the cord insertion. After delivery the mass is firm, red-tan and lies beneath the chorionic plate. What is the most likely pathology?", "options": {"A": "Chorangioma, a benign proliferation of capillaries within villous stroma", "B": "Subchorionic haematoma from rupture of maternal vessels beneath the chorionic plate", "C": "Placental site nodule composed of intermediate trophoblast with hyalinised stroma", "D": "Metastatic maternal melanoma deposit within the intervillous space of the placenta"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chorangioma, a benign proliferation of capillaries within villous stroma): Chorangioma is the most common placental tumour, a hamartoma like capillary proliferation in villous stroma. Large lesions can cause polyhydramnios, fetal anaemia and hydrops. Placental site nodules are small and incidental. Metastasis to the placenta is rare. A subchorionic haematoma is a blood clot, not a circumscribed vascular mass."}, {"id": 463, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 52-year-old woman's core biopsy of a cluster of calcifications reports atypical ductal hyperplasia, with a single focus spanning 1.5 mm. There is no family history of breast cancer. Which of the following best states the associated risk and the usual consequence for management?", "options": {"A": "Tenfold relative risk, equivalent to a diagnosis of ductal carcinoma in situ managed by mastectomy", "B": "About fourfold to fivefold higher relative risk in both breasts, with possible upgrade on surgical excision", "C": "Risk confined to the ipsilateral breast, equivalent to risk after a diagnosis of classic lobular neoplasia", "D": "No increased risk beyond the population baseline, so routine screening is appropriate without further sampling"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (About fourfold to fivefold higher relative risk in both breasts, with possible upgrade on surgical excision): Atypical ductal hyperplasia carries about a four to five fold relative risk, applying to both breasts, and core biopsy diagnoses are commonly upgraded to DCIS or invasive disease at excision. No added risk would describe non-proliferative change. Atypia risk is bilateral. A tenfold figure and mastectomy overstate a lesion below the size and extent of DCIS."}, {"id": 464, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "During excision of a fibroadenoma in a 48-year-old woman, an incidental focus is found in which acini are expanded and filled by monomorphic, discohesive cells with intracytoplasmic mucin vacuoles. The cells lack membranous E-cadherin staining. Which statement about this lesion is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Classic lobular carcinoma in situ, a marker of increased risk in both breasts, often multicentric", "B": "Pleomorphic lobular carcinoma in situ with comedonecrosis, usually treated by complete excision to clear margins", "C": "Atypical ductal hyperplasia, a clonal lesion with uniformly retained membranous E-cadherin expression", "D": "Low-grade ductal carcinoma in situ, a direct precursor confined to the ipsilateral breast quadrant"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Classic lobular carcinoma in situ, a marker of increased risk in both breasts, often multicentric): Discohesive monomorphic cells filling acini with E-cadherin loss are classic LCIS, which is a risk indicator for invasive carcinoma in either breast and is often multifocal and bilateral. Ductal lesions retain E-cadherin. Pleomorphic LCIS shows large atypical nuclei and necrosis and is managed with clear excision."}, {"id": 465, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 58-year-old woman has screening mammography showing linear, branching pleomorphic calcifications over 3 cm. Core biopsy shows distended ducts lined by markedly atypical cells with large central areas of necrosis and dense calcification. Which pathological feature best explains the calcification pattern seen on mammography?", "options": {"A": "Dystrophic mineralisation of necrotic luminal debris within a high-grade intraductal carcinoma", "B": "Dystrophic calcification of hyalinised stroma in an involuting fibroadenoma, producing coarse popcorn forms", "C": "Calcium oxalate crystals deposited within apocrine cyst contents, visible with polarised light", "D": "Secretory calcification of luminal material in dilated ducts, producing coarse rod-like forms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Dystrophic mineralisation of necrotic luminal debris within a high-grade intraductal carcinoma): Calcification of comedo-type necrosis inside ducts in high-grade DCIS produces fine linear branching pleomorphic forms following the duct. Secretory calcification is coarse and rod-like in benign ducts. Calcium oxalate crystals sit in apocrine cysts and are seen on polarisation. Stromal calcification is coarse."}, {"id": 469, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A woman treated six years earlier for an ER positive invasive breast carcinoma presents with abdominal distension and early satiety. CT shows diffuse thickening of the gastric wall and peritoneal deposits, with a normal liver and lungs. Which primary breast histological type best explains this pattern of spread?", "options": {"A": "Tubular carcinoma, which has a propensity for axillary nodal and gastric wall involvement", "B": "Invasive carcinoma of no special type, which preferentially spreads to lung, liver and brain parenchyma", "C": "Invasive lobular carcinoma, which spreads to serosal surfaces, gastrointestinal tract and ovaries", "D": "Mucinous carcinoma, which commonly disseminates by peritoneal seeding producing pseudomyxoma-like deposits"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Invasive lobular carcinoma, which spreads to serosal surfaces, gastrointestinal tract and ovaries): Late diffuse infiltration of the gastric wall and peritoneum with sparing of liver and lung is typical of metastatic invasive lobular carcinoma. Carcinoma of no special type favours bone, lung, liver and brain. Pure mucinous and tubular carcinomas rarely metastasise and do not show this pattern."}, {"id": 470, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 56-year-old woman has a small spiculated mammographic mass. Excision of the 9 mm tumour shows haphazardly arranged angulated glands with open lumina lined by a single layer of cells bearing apical snouts, lacking myoepithelium, infiltrating fat. What are the typical prognosis and receptor profile of this tumour?", "options": {"A": "Intermediate outcome with frequent nodal spread, with a triple negative receptor profile", "B": "Excellent outcome with rare nodal metastasis, with strong ER positivity and HER2 negativity", "C": "Excellent outcome due to encapsulation, with frequent loss of ER but retained PR expression", "D": "Poor outcome with early haematogenous metastasis, with HER2 amplification in most cases"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Excellent outcome with rare nodal metastasis, with strong ER positivity and HER2 negativity): Tubular carcinoma is a well-differentiated special type with an excellent prognosis, rare nodal involvement, and an ER positive, PR positive, HER2 negative profile. Triple negative and HER2 amplified patterns are typical of high-grade tumours. It is an infiltrative tumour with a stellate edge, not an encapsulated one."}, {"id": 479, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "A 40-year-old woman has a 6 cm firm mobile breast mass that doubled in size over four months. Core biopsy shows biphasic fibroepithelial tissue with lobulated fragments. The pathologist considers fibroadenoma and phyllodes tumour. Which histological feature best favours phyllodes tumour in this sample?", "options": {"A": "Compressed slit-like ducts embedded in myxoid stroma of uniform low cellularity", "B": "Hyalinised stroma containing coarse calcification and sparse bland spindle cells", "C": "Stromal hypercellularity accentuated around ducts, with exaggerated leaf-like clefts", "D": "Usual-type epithelial hyperplasia with focal apocrine metaplasia within the glandular component"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Stromal hypercellularity accentuated around ducts, with exaggerated leaf-like clefts): Phyllodes tumour shows leaf-like architecture, periductal stromal condensation and stromal cellularity exceeding that of fibroadenoma, and rapid growth supports it. Slit-like ducts in uniform myxoid stroma, epithelial hyperplasia with apocrine change, and hyalinised calcified stroma all occur in fibroadenoma."}, {"id": 486, "domain": "obstetric_gynaecological_and_breast", "question": "Sentinel lymph node biopsy in a 49-year-old woman with invasive carcinoma shows a metastatic deposit measuring 1.4 mm in greatest dimension within one node. No other nodes are involved and no extranodal extension is present. Which pathological nodal category applies?", "options": {"A": "pN1mi, because the deposit exceeds 0.2 mm but does not exceed 2.0 mm", "B": "pN2a, because a deposit exceeding 1 mm in a node defines this category", "C": "pN0(i+), because deposits measuring under 2.0 mm are classified as isolated tumour cells", "D": "pN1a, because one involved node defines this category irrespective of the size of the deposit"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (pN1mi, because the deposit exceeds 0.2 mm but does not exceed 2.0 mm): Isolated tumour cells are 0.2 mm or less. A deposit greater than 0.2 mm up to 2.0 mm is a micrometastasis, pN1mi. A macrometastasis exceeds 2.0 mm and gives pN1a. The pN2a category requires four to nine involved nodes."}, {"id": 491, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 34-year-old man has a mid-shaft tibial fracture treated with a cast. Two weeks later, follow-up radiographs show a faint fluffy density bridging the fracture gap, with no mature trabecular bone yet. Which tissue component makes up most of this early bridging callus?", "options": {"A": "Woven bone deposited directly on the periosteal surface by intramembranous ossification at the margins", "B": "Granulation tissue rich in type III collagen and capillaries, without any cartilage present", "C": "Mature lamellar bone remodelled along lines of mechanical stress and load across the gap", "D": "Hyaline cartilage and fibrous tissue within the organising haematoma"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Hyaline cartilage and fibrous tissue within the organising haematoma): At about two weeks the haematoma has been replaced by a soft callus of fibrocartilage and hyaline cartilage, which is radiolucent or faintly dense. Lamellar bone and remodelling appear months later. Periosteal woven bone forms a hard callus at the periphery but does not account for most of the central bridge. Granulation tissue precedes cartilage and is already being replaced."}, {"id": 493, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 22-year-old man develops breathlessness, confusion and a petechial rash over the chest, axillae and conjunctivae 36 hours after a closed femoral fracture treated with intramedullary nailing. Chest imaging shows bilateral patchy opacities. Which finding best explains the pathogenesis of these multisystem features?", "options": {"A": "Septic emboli from contaminated bone fragments seeding the pulmonary microvasculature after surgery", "B": "Air drawn into torn venous channels during surgical fixation causing a mechanical airlock", "C": "Thrombus formed in deep calf veins travelling to the main pulmonary arteries and lodging", "D": "Marrow fat globules entering torn veins and lodging in capillary beds"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Marrow fat globules entering torn veins and lodging in capillary beds): Fat embolism follows fractures of long bones when marrow fat enters ruptured venules and lodges in lung capillaries, then free fatty acids cause endothelial injury and pneumonitis. Passage through the lung into systemic circulation explains the cerebral and skin features. Pulmonary thromboembolism gives a different pattern, and the petechiae with delayed onset are typical of fat rather than air or septic emboli."}, {"id": 497, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 48-year-old woman with kidney stones has hand radiographs showing subperiosteal resorption along the radial margins of the middle phalanges, and a well-defined lytic lesion in the mandible. Her serum biochemistry results are shown. Which histological description best fits the lytic lesion?", "options": {"A": "Small round blue cells with clear glycogen-rich cytoplasm and geographic tumour necrosis", "B": "Lobules of hyaline cartilage with binucleate chondrocytes and areas of myxoid change", "C": "Multinucleated giant cells in a vascular fibrous stroma with haemosiderin", "D": "Sheets of atypical plasma cells with eccentric nuclei and clock-face chromatin pattern"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Multinucleated giant cells in a vascular fibrous stroma with haemosiderin): This is a brown tumour of hyperparathyroidism, a reparative giant cell lesion in which haemorrhage and haemosiderin give the brown colour. Subperiosteal phalangeal resorption supports the diagnosis. Myeloma, Ewing sarcoma and chondrosarcoma can look lytic but have distinct cell populations.", "image": "question_images/q_497.png"}, {"id": 499, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 41-year-old man on long-term prednisolone for vasculitis has progressive groin and hip pain. MRI shows a serpiginous low signal rim in the anterosuperior femoral head, with a crescent of subchondral collapse. Which gross appearance best describes the underlying pathology at surgical resection?", "options": {"A": "Focal destructive lesion containing pus and a central dead cortical bone fragment", "B": "Wedge-shaped subchondral infarct with its base against the articular surface", "C": "Diffusely softened cartilage with marginal osteophytes and subchondral cyst formation in the head", "D": "Expansile metaphyseal lesion with lamellated onion-skin periosteal response and soft tissue extension"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Wedge-shaped subchondral infarct with its base against the articular surface): Osteonecrosis is ischaemic death of bone and marrow, producing a triangular or wedge-shaped infarct beneath the cartilage with the apex toward the centre of the head. The double line rim is the reactive interface. Subchondral collapse gives the crescent. Osteoarthritis, osteomyelitis and Ewing sarcoma give different gross findings."}, {"id": 504, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 38-year-old migrant has back pain and weight loss. MRI shows destruction of adjacent thoracic vertebral bodies with relative preservation of the intervening disc and a large paravertebral collection with calcification. Which feature best explains disc preservation early in this disease?", "options": {"A": "Absence of proteolytic enzymes from the organism, unlike pyogenic bacteria", "B": "Strongly avascular nature of the disc preventing any granulomatous reaction", "C": "Rapid caseous necrosis confined to the central nucleus pulposus", "D": "Spread beneath the anterior longitudinal ligament sparing the endplates"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Absence of proteolytic enzymes from the organism, unlike pyogenic bacteria): Mycobacterium tuberculosis lacks the proteolytic enzymes of pyogenic bacteria, so the disc stays relatively intact until late, in contrast to bacterial discitis where the disc is destroyed early. Subligamentous spread occurs and contributes to anterior scalloping, but does not explain disc sparing. Caseating granulomas drive the destruction of bone."}, {"id": 509, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 15-year-old has a painless bony projection from the distal femoral metaphysis pointing away from the knee. Imaging shows cortex and marrow continuous with the parent bone. Which tissue forms the outer covering of this lesion, and what complication is linked to its thickness in an adult?", "options": {"A": "Synovial lining, with transformation to synovial sarcoma in adulthood", "B": "Cartilage cap, with malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma when thick in adulthood", "C": "Periosteal fibrous cap, with transformation to osteosarcoma in adulthood", "D": "Articular cartilage layer, with transformation to chordoma in adulthood"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Cartilage cap, with malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma when thick in adulthood): Osteochondromas have a hyaline cartilage cap, which grows by endochondral ossification and arises from displaced physeal tissue, associated with EXT1 or EXT2 changes in hereditary multiple exostoses. A cap exceeding about 2 cm in an adult raises concern for secondary chondrosarcoma. The other options misstate tissue and tumour type."}, {"id": 523, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 4-year-old child has a tender scalp swelling and mild polyuria. Skull radiographs show a sharply punched-out lytic lesion without a sclerotic margin. Biopsy shows histiocytes with grooved nuclei admixed with many eosinophils. Which immunohistochemical or ultrastructural finding confirms the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "CD1a and langerin positivity with Birbeck granules on electron microscopy", "B": "Strong CD99 membranous staining with an EWSR1 translocation demonstrated by FISH testing", "C": "Negative S100 with strong CD68 and lysozyme staining in a non-dendritic histiocytic population", "D": "Kappa or lambda light chain restriction in a population of mature plasma cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (CD1a and langerin positivity with Birbeck granules on electron microscopy): Langerhans cell histiocytosis consists of clonal dendritic cells with BRAF V600E mutation in many cases, expressing S100, CD1a and langerin, with tennis-racket Birbeck granules ultrastructurally. Eosinophils accompany the cells. Ewing sarcoma and myeloma have distinct markers."}, {"id": 525, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 2-year-old boy has pallor, hepatosplenomegaly and a facial nerve palsy. Skeletal radiographs show uniformly dense bones with Erlenmeyer flask deformity of the metaphyses and a bone-within-bone appearance of the vertebrae. Which mechanism best explains his anaemia and extramedullary haematopoiesis?", "options": {"A": "Marrow space obliteration by retained primary spongiosa from failed osteoclast resorption", "B": "Marrow suppression due to aluminium deposition at the mineralisation front of trabecular bone", "C": "Marrow infiltration by diffusely proliferating malignant plasma cells with lytic skeletal lesions", "D": "Marrow replacement by fibrous tissue from sustained excess of parathyroid hormone"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Marrow space obliteration by retained primary spongiosa from failed osteoclast resorption): Osteopetrosis results from osteoclast dysfunction, commonly impaired acid secretion, leaving unresorbed calcified cartilage and bone that crowd the marrow. This leads to anaemia, extramedullary haematopoiesis and cranial nerve compression by narrowed foramina. The other options produce different patterns."}, {"id": 528, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 25-year-old with splenomegaly and thrombocytopenia has hip and thigh bone pain for several months. Radiographs show flask-shaped distal femoral metaphyses and focal lucent areas with sclerosis in the femoral head. A marrow aspirate would most likely show cells with which appearance?", "options": {"A": "Large foamy macrophages containing numerous acid-fast rods within the cytoplasm", "B": "Histiocytes with grooved nuclei and intracytoplasmic tennis-racket shaped granules", "C": "Sheets of plasma cells with eccentric nuclei and cytoplasmic Russell bodies", "D": "Macrophages with wrinkled paper cytoplasm from glucocerebroside storage"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Macrophages with wrinkled paper cytoplasm from glucocerebroside storage): Gaucher disease is a lysosomal storage disorder from glucocerebrosidase deficiency, and engorged macrophages with fibrillar, wrinkled cytoplasm accumulate in marrow, causing remodelling defects (Erlenmeyer flask), infarcts and avascular necrosis. The other options describe different marrow infiltrates."}, {"id": 529, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 58-year-old man has had a draining sinus over the tibia for 25 years following childhood osteomyelitis. The sinus margin has recently become ulcerated with raised everted edges and a foul discharge, and the surrounding skin is indurated. Which malignancy is the most likely complication?", "options": {"A": "Osteosarcoma arising from the reactive periosteal new bone formed around the infected segment", "B": "Squamous cell carcinoma arising in the epithelium lining the sinus tract", "C": "Malignant lymphoma arising in the chronically stimulated marrow adjacent to the infection", "D": "Fibrosarcoma arising from the scarred and thickened fibrous wall of the chronic sinus"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Squamous cell carcinoma arising in the epithelium lining the sinus tract): Long-standing sinus tracts from chronic osteomyelitis can develop squamous cell carcinoma, termed a Marjolin ulcer when arising in chronic wounds. It is usually well differentiated and slow to metastasise. Sarcoma and lymphoma are not classically linked to chronic infection of bone."}, {"id": 530, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 68-year-old woman with a body mass index of 34 has years of activity-related knee pain. Weight-bearing radiographs show asymmetric medial joint space narrowing, marginal osteophytes and subchondral sclerosis. Which pathological process best explains the loss of joint space on imaging?", "options": {"A": "Synovial pannus overgrowth eroding cartilage and bare area bone from the joint margins", "B": "Deposition of monosodium urate crystals within cartilage causing chondrolysis and secondary erosion", "C": "Progressive fibrillation and loss of articular cartilage with failed chondrocyte repair", "D": "Primary subchondral bone collapse following ischaemic necrosis of the femoral condyle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Progressive fibrillation and loss of articular cartilage with failed chondrocyte repair): Osteoarthritis is primarily a disease of articular cartilage: surface fibrillation, loss of proteoglycan and failed repair thin the cartilage, producing asymmetric loaded-compartment narrowing. Sclerosis and osteophytes are the bone response. Pannus erosion would give uniform narrowing with marginal erosions, urate would give tophi and punched out erosions, and osteonecrosis begins in bone with subchondral collapse."}, {"id": 534, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 38-year-old man with scaly plaques on the elbows has a swollen third finger. Radiographs show distal interphalangeal erosions with adjacent fluffy periostitis, normal bone density and one joint with a pencil-in-cup deformity. Which pathological feature best explains the periosteal reaction?", "options": {"A": "Urate crystal granulomas eroding cortex with an overhanging sclerotic rim", "B": "Enthesis and periosteum centred inflammation with new bone formation", "C": "Infection of the phalanx producing sequestrum and involucrum formation", "D": "Pannus invasion of bone at the bare areas with osteoclastic resorption and no new bone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Enthesis and periosteum centred inflammation with new bone formation): Psoriatic arthritis combines erosive change with enthesitis and periostitis, so bone destruction coexists with fluffy new bone formation, and bone density is typically preserved. Pencil-in-cup deformity results from erosion with adjacent proliferation. Rheumatoid disease gives erosion with osteopenia, gout gives overhanging edges, and infection is usually monoarticular and does not follow the skin disease."}, {"id": 539, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 5-month-old infant has had fever for two days and now has a swollen, irritable hip held flexed and abducted. Ultrasound shows a large effusion with echogenic debris. If untreated, which pathological process most directly explains rapid loss of cartilage and later hip subluxation in this infant?", "options": {"A": "Neutrophil and bacterial proteases digesting cartilage as pus raises intracapsular pressure", "B": "Deposition of haemosiderin from repeated bleeding into the synovium of the hip joint", "C": "Subchondral bone collapse caused by loss of the epiphyseal blood supply after trauma", "D": "Autoimmune pannus formation eroding cartilage from the margins of the joint over months"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Neutrophil and bacterial proteases digesting cartilage as pus raises intracapsular pressure): In septic arthritis, neutrophil proteases, matrix metalloproteinases and bacterial toxins degrade cartilage within days, and raised intracapsular pressure in the infant hip can compromise epiphyseal perfusion, leading to dislocation and growth disturbance. Pannus and haemosiderin require a chronic course, and vascular compromise is a contributor rather than the sole cause."}, {"id": 541, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 32-year-old woman has chronic knee swelling over two years and a haemorrhagic effusion on aspiration. MRI shows lobulated synovial masses of low signal on all sequences with marked blooming on gradient echo. Which pathological finding best explains these signal characteristics?", "options": {"A": "Mucoid matrix producing high signal on fluid sensitive sequences", "B": "Dense fibrous tissue with calcification forming an old organised haematoma in the joint", "C": "Intracellular lipid and fat droplets within proliferating synovial cells and foam cells", "D": "Haemosiderin in macrophages and stromal cells within a proliferating synovial mass"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Haemosiderin in macrophages and stromal cells within a proliferating synovial mass): Tenosynovial giant cell tumour, formerly pigmented villonodular synovitis, is a neoplasm driven by CSF1 rearrangement, and contains haemosiderin laden macrophages, foam cells and multinucleate giant cells. Haemosiderin causes marked susceptibility effect, hence blooming. Fat, calcification and myxoid change give different signal behaviours."}, {"id": 550, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 19-year-old man with multiple cafe-au-lait spots has a bag-of-worms mass of the left thigh. MRI shows multiple T2 hyperintense lesions along nerve branches with a central low signal dot in each. Which statement about this lesion is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "It arises from perineurial cells and contains no axons", "B": "It is pathognomonic for NF1 and carries a risk of malignant transformation", "C": "It is typical of NF2 and is associated with vestibular lesions", "D": "It is a sporadic encapsulated tumour with a very low risk of malignant change"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (It is pathognomonic for NF1 and carries a risk of malignant transformation): Plexiform neurofibroma is a diffuse expansion of nerve fascicles and is virtually specific for NF1. The central low signal dot corresponds to fibrous tissue. Lifetime risk of malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumour is increased, and rapid growth, pain or loss of the target sign should prompt concern."}, {"id": 554, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 66-year-old retired labourer has an incidental, poorly defined, bilateral soft tissue lesion between the inferior scapular tip and chest wall. It shows signal similar to muscle with interspersed fatty streaks and no surrounding oedema. Which pathological process is most likely?", "options": {"A": "A reactive pseudotumour with abnormal elastic fibres from repetitive friction", "B": "Lymphomatous deposit within the periscapular soft tissues and chest wall muscle", "C": "Benign lipomatous tumour carrying MDM2 amplification and atypical stromal cells", "D": "Low-grade sarcoma of fibroblastic origin that infiltrates intercostal muscles"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A reactive pseudotumour with abnormal elastic fibres from repetitive friction): Elastofibroma dorsi is a benign reactive fibroelastic lesion at the inferior scapular angle, often bilateral, in older people with repetitive shoulder movements. Histology shows thick, abnormal elastic fibres. The typical site, muscle-like signal and fatty streaks prevent unnecessary biopsy."}, {"id": 555, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 45-year-old woman has sudden severe shoulder pain with restricted movement. Radiographs show a poorly defined, fluffy calcification beside the supraspinatus insertion, which was dense and sharply marginated on a film two years earlier. Which phase and process does this represent?", "options": {"A": "Chronic degenerative tear with fatty infiltration of the tendon", "B": "Acute CPPD crisis, with rhomboid crystals shedding into the bursa", "C": "Resorptive phase, with macrophage removal of hydroxyapatite deposits", "D": "Formative phase, with fibrocartilage metaplasia depositing calcium crystals"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Resorptive phase, with macrophage removal of hydroxyapatite deposits): Calcific tendinitis is cell-mediated deposition of carbonate apatite in a formative phase, followed by a painful resorptive phase when vascular ingrowth and phagocytosis dissolve the deposit, giving a fluffy, ill-defined appearance. Symptoms are worst in this phase and may include bursal rupture."}, {"id": 556, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 50-year-old recreational runner has two months of posterior heel pain and Achilles tendon thickening. MRI shows an intratendinous focus of increased signal on short echo time sequences without paratendinous fluid. A biopsy taken at surgery would be most likely to show which finding in this degenerative condition?", "options": {"A": "Monosodium urate crystals deposited between the collagen fibres of the tendon and sheath", "B": "Neutrophilic infiltration and fibrinous exudate surrounding the tendon sheath and paratenon", "C": "Disordered collagen, excess ground substance and new vessels with few inflammatory cells", "D": "Palisading granulomas with central fibrinoid necrosis within the substance of the tendon"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Disordered collagen, excess ground substance and new vessels with few inflammatory cells): Tendinosis is a failed healing response: collagen fibres lose their orderly arrangement, mucoid ground substance accumulates, tenocytes proliferate and new vessels grow, with minimal inflammatory cells. This degeneration accounts for intrasubstance signal on short echo time sequences and tendon thickening."}, {"id": 559, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 35-year-old man has a foot drop after a knee dislocation. MRI two weeks later shows diffuse increased T2 signal in the anterior compartment muscles with preserved bulk and normal T1 signal. What pathophysiological change accounts for this appearance on imaging?", "options": {"A": "Coagulative necrosis of fibres from acute compartment ischaemia", "B": "Replacement of muscle by mature adipose tissue after chronic degeneration and fibre loss", "C": "Expanded extracellular water and capillary volume in denervated fibres before atrophy", "D": "Haemorrhage containing methaemoglobin within the belly of the muscle"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Expanded extracellular water and capillary volume in denervated fibres before atrophy): Within days of denervation, muscle shows increased T2 signal from enlarged extracellular and vascular spaces, with normal T1 signal and preserved bulk. Atrophy and fatty replacement develop over months if the nerve does not recover. This timing helps separate denervation from ischaemia or haematoma."}, {"id": 560, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 60-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis has a fluctuant swelling that varies with knee extension in the posteromedial popliteal fossa. Ultrasound shows an anechoic collection with a neck passing between the medial gastrocnemius and semimembranosus tendons. What is the pathogenesis of this lesion?", "options": {"A": "Distension of a communicating bursa, usually secondary to a chronic knee effusion", "B": "A true cystic neoplasm arising from the synovium of the popliteal fossa", "C": "A mucin filled cyst arising from the joint capsule without a synovial lining or communication", "D": "Cystic degeneration of the posterior horn of the medial meniscus unconnected to the joint"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Distension of a communicating bursa, usually secondary to a chronic knee effusion): A Baker cyst is a distended gastrocnemius-semimembranosus bursa that communicates with the joint through a posteromedial capsular gap, commonly in osteoarthritis or inflammatory arthritis. The neck between the tendons is characteristic. Rupture can mimic deep venous thrombosis."}, {"id": 565, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 78-year-old man has a rapidly growing, painless, violaceous nodule on the sun-exposed face. Biopsy shows small round blue cells with salt and pepper chromatin that stain for cytokeratin 20 in a perinuclear dot pattern. Which risk factor is associated with this tumour?", "options": {"A": "Merkel cell polyomavirus integration with immunosuppression in older adults", "B": "Epstein-Barr virus infection with nuclear latency proteins in the tumour cells", "C": "Biallelic loss of PTCH1 with hedgehog pathway activation", "D": "Human papillomavirus type 16 infection of basal keratinocytes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Merkel cell polyomavirus integration with immunosuppression in older adults): Merkel cell carcinoma is a neuroendocrine skin carcinoma arising on sun-damaged skin, linked to clonal integration of Merkel cell polyomavirus and to immunosuppression and ultraviolet exposure. Perinuclear dot CK20 staining supports the diagnosis. It spreads early to regional nodes, so nodal imaging is central."}, {"id": 566, "domain": "musculoskeletal_and_soft_tissue", "question": "A 50-year-old woman has Raynaud phenomenon, tight skin over the fingers and recurrent finger ulcers. Hand radiographs show tuft resorption of several terminal phalanges and small, dense calcifications in the subcutaneous soft tissues. Which pathological process is responsible for the soft tissue changes?", "options": {"A": "Chronic microvascular injury with fibrosis, ischaemia and dystrophic calcification", "B": "Hypertrophic synovitis with direct bone invasion at the bare areas", "C": "Urate crystal granulomas forming tophi around the interphalangeal joints", "D": "Metastatic calcification from raised serum calcium and phosphate levels in the soft tissues of the hand"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chronic microvascular injury with fibrosis, ischaemia and dystrophic calcification): Systemic sclerosis causes endothelial injury, obliterative vasculopathy and excess collagen deposition. Ischaemic or damaged tissue develops dystrophic calcification, giving subcutaneous calcinosis, and distal tufts resorb. Serum calcium is normal. Gout and rheumatoid arthritis lack the skin tightening and acro-osteolysis."}, {"id": 576, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 30-year-old man is struck on the back of the head with a bat. The first CT is normal, but 24 hours later he is drowsy and CT shows haemorrhagic foci in both inferior frontal lobes and temporal poles. What explains this distribution?", "options": {"A": "Venous infarction secondary to occlusion of the superior sagittal sinus after injury", "B": "Shearing of long white matter tracts at the interface of grey and white matter", "C": "Brain movement over rough bony surfaces of the anterior and middle cranial fossae", "D": "Vasospasm of anterior cerebral arteries producing watershed infarction in border zones"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Brain movement over rough bony surfaces of the anterior and middle cranial fossae): Contusions are bruises of the gyral crests that occur where the moving brain meets rough bone, namely the orbitofrontal surfaces and temporal poles, and they often blossom after an initially normal scan. Shear injury is deeper. Sinus thrombosis and vasospasm do not match the history."}, {"id": 578, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 35-year-old man with chronic otitis media has fever, headache and left temporal lobe signs. MRI shows a rim-enhancing lesion with central restricted diffusion, and the enhancing wall is thinner on the medial side facing the ventricle. Which pathological feature explains the thinner medial wall?", "options": {"A": "Pressure from cerebrospinal fluid in the adjacent ventricle erodes the forming capsule", "B": "Poorer blood supply to deep white matter slows fibroblast and granulation tissue formation", "C": "Neutrophil activity is greater near the ventricle, melting encapsulating fibroblasts early", "D": "Glial scarring is more abundant medially because astrocytes cluster near the ependyma"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Poorer blood supply to deep white matter slows fibroblast and granulation tissue formation): A capsule forms as fibroblasts and new vessels wall off the cerebritis. Cortex is better vascularised than deep white matter, so the capsule is thicker laterally and thinner towards the ventricle, which is why abscesses tend to rupture into the ventricle. The other options invoke mechanisms that do not account for the asymmetry."}, {"id": 582, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 29-year-old man from a high-incidence country has three weeks of headache, night sweats and low-grade fever, then develops a right sixth nerve palsy. Contrast MRI shows thick basal meningeal enhancement and mild hydrocephalus. The cerebrospinal fluid results are shown. Which pathological process underlies this pattern?", "options": {"A": "Lymphocytic meningitis with perivascular cuffing around ventricular ependyma and viral inclusions", "B": "Purulent neutrophilic exudate over the cerebral convexities with cortical vein thrombosis", "C": "Non-caseating granulomas of sarcoid pattern infiltrating the basal leptomeninges and cranial nerves with lymphocytic perivascular cuffing", "D": "Thick gelatinous granulomatous exudate around the basal cisterns with caseation and obliterative arteritis"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Thick gelatinous granulomatous exudate around the basal cisterns with caseation and obliterative arteritis): Tuberculous meningitis forms a basal exudate that entraps cranial nerves, obstructs cerebrospinal fluid flow and causes arteritis with infarcts. Pyogenic meningitis favours the convexities. Neurosarcoidosis has non-caseating granulomas. Viral meningitis lacks a gelatinous basal exudate.", "image": "question_images/q_582.png"}, {"id": 583, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 63-year-old woman has rapidly progressive dementia, myoclonus and ataxia over three months, with a normal cerebrospinal fluid cell count. Diffusion MRI shows high signal in the cortical ribbon and basal ganglia. Autopsy is performed. Which feature best accounts for the tissue changes?", "options": {"A": "Alpha synuclein positive Lewy bodies in limbic and neocortical neurons with Lewy neurites", "B": "Hyperphosphorylated tau aggregates forming neurofibrillary tangles across the medial temporal lobes", "C": "Accumulation of protease resistant misfolded prion protein with vacuolation of neuropil and neuronal loss", "D": "Intranuclear viral inclusions with microglial nodules and neuronophagia in cortical grey matter"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Accumulation of protease resistant misfolded prion protein with vacuolation of neuropil and neuronal loss): Sporadic Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease causes spongiform vacuolation, astrogliosis and neuronal loss, with protease resistant prion protein, and cortical ribboning on diffusion MRI. Tau tangles, viral inclusions and Lewy bodies are slower, have different distributions and do not produce spongiform change."}, {"id": 588, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 74-year-old man has fluctuating cognition, well-formed visual hallucinations and parkinsonism arising within a year of dementia onset, and he acts out dreams during sleep. Dopamine transporter imaging is abnormal. What is the characteristic neuropathological finding in his brain at autopsy?", "options": {"A": "Intraneuronal alpha synuclein inclusions in brainstem, limbic and neocortical neurons", "B": "Frontotemporal atrophy with silver positive round intraneuronal Pick bodies of tau", "C": "Spongiform vacuolation with astrogliosis in cortical layers and prion protein deposition", "D": "Extracellular beta amyloid plaques with dystrophic neurites confined to hippocampal subfields"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Intraneuronal alpha synuclein inclusions in brainstem, limbic and neocortical neurons): Dementia with Lewy bodies shows Lewy bodies and Lewy neurites of alpha synuclein in brainstem, limbic and neocortical regions, often with coexisting Alzheimer pathology. Pick bodies, hippocampal plaques and spongiform change belong to other dementias with different clinical syndromes."}, {"id": 592, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has a temporal lobe tumour with IDH1 R132H mutation and ATRX loss on immunohistochemistry, without necrosis or microvascular proliferation and with a low mitotic count. Molecular testing shows homozygous deletion of CDKN2A/B. By WHO CNS5, how is this tumour graded?", "options": {"A": "Glioblastoma, IDH wild type, because the deletion indicates a primary high grade tumour", "B": "Grade 3 astrocytoma, IDH mutant, because the deletion indicates anaplastic features without necrosis", "C": "Grade 2 astrocytoma, IDH mutant, as necrosis and microvascular proliferation are absent", "D": "Grade 4 astrocytoma, IDH mutant, on the basis of the CDKN2A/B deletion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Grade 4 astrocytoma, IDH mutant, on the basis of the CDKN2A/B deletion): In CNS5, IDH mutant diffuse astrocytomas are graded 2 to 4 within one entity, and homozygous CDKN2A/B deletion assigns grade 4 even without necrosis. The tumour keeps its IDH mutant astrocytoma label and is not reclassified as IDH wild type glioblastoma."}, {"id": 594, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 7-year-old child has headache, vomiting and ataxia. MRI shows a well-circumscribed cystic cerebellar mass with an enhancing mural nodule. Histology shows a biphasic pattern with bipolar hairlike cells and eosinophilic corkscrew fibres. Which genetic alteration is most characteristic of this tumour?", "options": {"A": "KIAA1549 and BRAF gene fusion leading to MAPK pathway activation", "B": "Loss of VHL with hypoxia inducible factor stabilisation and stromal cell proliferation", "C": "Amplification of MYC with large cell anaplastic cytology in small round blue cells", "D": "Mutation of IDH1 with associated 2 hydroxyglutarate accumulation in glial cells"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (KIAA1549 and BRAF gene fusion leading to MAPK pathway activation): Pilocytic astrocytoma, a grade 1 tumour, usually carries a KIAA1549-BRAF fusion activating MAPK signalling, and shows Rosenthal fibres and eosinophilic granular bodies. MYC amplification, IDH1 mutation and VHL loss characterise other tumours."}, {"id": 605, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 66-year-old obese diabetic man has thoracic stiffness and back pain. Radiographs show flowing ossification along the right anterolateral vertebral bodies over more than four contiguous levels, with preserved disc height and normal sacroiliac joints. Which underlying pathology best explains this appearance?", "options": {"A": "Ossification of the anterior longitudinal ligament and adjacent entheses with preserved discs", "B": "Osteophyte formation from degenerative disc narrowing with vacuum phenomenon and endplate sclerosis", "C": "Syndesmophytes from inflammatory enthesitis at the outer annulus fibres with sacroiliac fusion", "D": "Paravertebral calcification of ligaments from calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition within the annulus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Ossification of the anterior longitudinal ligament and adjacent entheses with preserved discs): Diffuse idiopathic skeletal hyperostosis involves ossification of the anterior longitudinal ligament and entheses over four or more levels, with preserved disc height and normal sacroiliac joints, and is associated with obesity and diabetes. Ankylosing spondylitis, spondylosis and pyrophosphate disease differ in disc and joint findings."}, {"id": 606, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 27-year-old man sustains a complete transection of the median nerve at the wrist in a glass injury, which is surgically repaired the same day. Distal to the injury, which sequence of events occurs in the following weeks and allows later regeneration?", "options": {"A": "Axon and myelin breakdown with macrophage clearance and Schwann cell proliferation forming guiding bands", "B": "Immediate loss of Schwann cells with endoneurial collapse preventing sprouting of regrowing axons", "C": "Neuronal cell body death in the dorsal root ganglion with permanent distal denervation", "D": "Segmental demyelination with preserved axons and conduction block restoring through remyelination"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Axon and myelin breakdown with macrophage clearance and Schwann cell proliferation forming guiding bands): After transection the distal axon and myelin degenerate, macrophages clear debris, and Schwann cells proliferate and form bands of Bungner that guide regenerating axons at about 1 mm per day. Segmental demyelination is a different pattern of neuropathy."}, {"id": 612, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 52-year-old non-smoking man has a painless 3 cm level II neck mass. CT shows a thin-walled cystic node with an enhancing mural nodule, and MRI shows a small asymmetrical lesion in the palatine tonsil. Which pathology best explains both findings and the strongly positive immunostain expected in the cell blocks?", "options": {"A": "Keratinising squamous carcinoma related to tobacco with TP53 loss and nodal necrosis producing cystic change", "B": "Branchial cleft cyst with lymphoid wall that has undergone late squamous malignant transformation", "C": "Non-keratinising tonsillar crypt carcinoma, with diffuse p16 from high-risk papillomavirus activity", "D": "Lymphoepithelial cyst related to immunodeficiency, with benign squamous lining and florid lymphoid hyperplasia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Non-keratinising tonsillar crypt carcinoma, with diffuse p16 from high-risk papillomavirus activity): Cystic nodal metastases from an occult small tonsil or base of tongue primary are characteristic of human papillomavirus related oropharyngeal carcinoma, which is non-keratinising, arises in reticulated crypt epithelium and overexpresses p16. Tobacco related keratinising tumours usually produce solid or necrotic nodes. A true branchial cyst carcinoma is rare and a benign lymphoepithelial cyst would not show an enhancing mural nodule with a tonsil lesion."}, {"id": 614, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 57-year-old man has a p16-positive tonsil carcinoma measuring 3.5 cm. Imaging shows three enlarged ipsilateral level II and III nodes, the largest 4 cm, none with extranodal extension. There is no distant disease. Using the current TNM staging for human papillomavirus related oropharyngeal cancer, what is the clinical stage and the reason?", "options": {"A": "Stage I, because clinical N1 nodes up to 6 cm are downstaged with p16 positivity", "B": "Stage III, because a primary above 2 cm with nodal disease exceeds stage II", "C": "Stage IVA, because multiple nodes in the neck indicate advanced regional disease in oropharyngeal cancer", "D": "Stage II, because several ipsilateral nodes denote N2 and size above 2 cm raises the group"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stage I, because clinical N1 nodes up to 6 cm are downstaged with p16 positivity): The p16-positive oropharyngeal system uses a separate scheme in which clinical N1 is one or more ipsilateral nodes of 6 cm or less, so a T2 tumour with N1 and no metastases is stage I. The p16-negative scheme counts multiple ipsilateral nodes as N2b and gives a higher stage, which is the trap in the other choices. Advanced stages require contralateral or large nodes or distant disease."}, {"id": 619, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 45-year-old man has anosmia and recurrent epistaxis. CT and MRI show a dumbbell-shaped enhancing mass in the upper nasal cavity at the cribriform plate extending into the anterior cranial fossa, with small cysts at the tumour-brain interface. Which origin and histological features are most likely?", "options": {"A": "Minor salivary myoepithelial cells forming spindle cell sheets in a myxoid matrix with cystic change", "B": "Respiratory seromucinous glands, with basaloid cribriform growth and strong S100 expression in neoplastic cells", "C": "Surface squamous mucosa, with keratinisation, intercellular bridges and positivity for high-risk papillomavirus", "D": "Olfactory neuroepithelium with lobular growth and Homer Wright rosettes, graded by the Hyams system"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Olfactory neuroepithelium with lobular growth and Homer Wright rosettes, graded by the Hyams system): A cribriform plate mass with intracranial dumbbell extension and peritumoral cysts, in a patient with anosmia, points to esthesioneuroblastoma, which arises from olfactory neuroepithelium and shows lobules, neurofibrillary matrix and rosettes. It is graded by Hyams system. Adenoid cystic carcinoma, myoepithelial tumours and squamous carcinoma are sinonasal tumours but lack the neuroendocrine features and the characteristic location."}, {"id": 621, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 24-year-old man with asthma and nasal polyps has chronic nasal congestion. CT shows expansion of several opacified sinuses with central hyperattenuating material and thinning of the bony walls. MRI shows marked T2 signal void centrally. Which pathological finding best accounts for the hyperattenuation and the T2 signal void?", "options": {"A": "Thick eosinophilic mucin containing sparse hyphae and concentrated calcium, iron and manganese salts", "B": "Caseous necrosis inside granulomas of chronic tuberculous sinusitis, with dystrophic calcification of the necrotic debris", "C": "Dense Aspergillus ball with tissue invasion through mucosa and thrombosis of adjacent small vessels", "D": "Inspissated mucous retention within an obstructed ostium with high protein content and no organisms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Thick eosinophilic mucin containing sparse hyphae and concentrated calcium, iron and manganese salts): Allergic fungal rhinosinusitis in an atopic patient produces eosinophilic mucin laden with hyphae, and heavy metal and calcium content explains the high CT attenuation and the T2 signal void. The fungus does not invade tissue. Tuberculosis and invasive fungal disease would behave differently, and retained secretions without organisms may be hyperdense but lack the allergic mucin and polyps."}, {"id": 622, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 62-year-old smoker with hoarseness has a 1.5 cm squamous carcinoma limited to the mid portion of one mobile true vocal cord, without involvement of the anterior commissure. Staging imaging shows no enlarged neck nodes. Which anatomical feature best explains why early tumours at this site rarely present with regional nodal metastasis?", "options": {"A": "Sparse lymphatic network in the vocal fold mucosa, which limits early regional spread", "B": "Dense subglottic lymphatics draining directly to paratracheal nodes and bypassing the neck chain", "C": "The conus elasticus acting as a barrier that blocks lateral tumour spread into the paraglottic space", "D": "Rich lymphatic drainage through the thyrohyoid membrane, which carries tumour to upper jugular nodes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Sparse lymphatic network in the vocal fold mucosa, which limits early regional spread): The true vocal fold has very few lymphatic channels, so small glottic cancers seldom involve nodes and tend to present early because hoarseness appears quickly. Supraglottic tumours drain richly through the thyrohyoid membrane and often present with nodes. Subglottic drainage goes to paratracheal and pretracheal nodes, and the conus elasticus does influence local spread but does not explain nodal absence."}, {"id": 623, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 60-year-old man has an epiglottic carcinoma with extension to the vallecula. CT shows replacement of pre-epiglottic fat by enhancing tumour. The vocal cords are mobile, the outer thyroid cartilage cortex is intact, and there is no extralaryngeal spread. Using the current TNM classification for supraglottic cancer, which T category applies?", "options": {"A": "T2, because extension to adjacent mucosa outside the supraglottis such as the vallecula defines this category", "B": "T4a, because replacement of pre-epiglottic fat indicates extension beyond the larynx into the neck soft tissues", "C": "T3, because invasion of the pre-epiglottic space places the tumour above the limits of T2", "D": "T4b, because tumour in fat spaces anterior to the larynx implies involvement of the prevertebral compartment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (T3, because invasion of the pre-epiglottic space places the tumour above the limits of T2): Vallecular involvement alone is T2, but invasion of the pre-epiglottic or paraglottic space, vocal cord fixation or inner thyroid cartilage cortex makes the tumour T3. The pre-epiglottic space lies within the larynx, so this is not T4a, which needs outer cortex or extralaryngeal invasion. Prevertebral space or carotid involvement defines T4b."}, {"id": 624, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 67-year-old smoker has a breathy voice. Laryngoscopy shows a paramedian left vocal cord that does not abduct. CT of the neck shows a dilated left piriform sinus and laryngeal ventricle. There is no dysphagia, and the palate elevates symmetrically. Which lesion should the radiologist look for next on chest CT?", "options": {"A": "Apical mass invading the right subclavian loop of the recurrent laryngeal nerve", "B": "Superior pole thyroid lesion injuring the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve", "C": "Skull base lesion at the jugular foramen affecting the vagus nerve with palatal weakness", "D": "Mass at the aortopulmonary window compressing the left recurrent laryngeal nerve"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Mass at the aortopulmonary window compressing the left recurrent laryngeal nerve): The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks beneath the aortic arch beside the ligamentum arteriosum, so mediastinal nodes or a mass at the aortopulmonary window can paralyse the left cord. The right nerve loops the subclavian artery and is not the affected side. Superior laryngeal injury causes pitch problems, and a vagal lesion at the skull base would also impair the palate and swallowing."}, {"id": 625, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 29-year-old woman has a firm thyroid nodule. Ultrasound shows a solid hypoechoic nodule with punctate microcalcifications and a cystic level IV lymph node with bright internal foci. Fine needle aspiration of the node suggests metastasis. Which histological features best explain the microcalcifications and the nuclear appearances in the primary?", "options": {"A": "Sheets of polygonal cells in amyloid-rich stroma with calcitonin immunoreactivity and C-cell hyperplasia", "B": "Overlapping clear nuclei with grooves, and psammoma bodies from calcified papillary tips", "C": "Follicular architecture with capsular and vascular invasion in a well-circumscribed thick-walled nodule", "D": "Oxyphilic cells with abundant granular cytoplasm and prominent nucleoli arranged in trabeculae"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Overlapping clear nuclei with grooves, and psammoma bodies from calcified papillary tips): Papillary carcinoma shows optically clear, overlapping nuclei with grooves and pseudoinclusions, and psammoma bodies form from calcification of papillary tips, which produces punctate microcalcifications and often cystic nodal metastases. Follicular carcinoma is diagnosed by invasion, medullary carcinoma has amyloid and calcitonin, and oncocytic tumours have granular cytoplasm without these nuclear features."}, {"id": 630, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 34-year-old woman has weight loss, tremor and a diffuse goitre. Ultrasound shows an enlarged hypoechoic gland with markedly increased flow on colour Doppler, and technetium scintigraphy shows homogeneous high uptake throughout both lobes. Which mechanism best explains the hyperthyroidism and the gland appearance?", "options": {"A": "Stimulating autoantibodies at the TSH receptor, causing diffuse follicular hyperplasia with scalloped colloid", "B": "Viral inflammation with granulomatous destruction of follicles and reduced uptake on scintigraphy", "C": "Antibody-mediated destruction of follicles by peroxidase antibodies, releasing preformed hormone into the circulation", "D": "Autonomous nodule with activating TSH receptor mutation, suppressing uptake in the remaining normal gland"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stimulating autoantibodies at the TSH receptor, causing diffuse follicular hyperplasia with scalloped colloid): Graves disease results from antibodies that stimulate the TSH receptor, producing diffuse hyperplasia, increased vascularity and uniform high tracer uptake. A toxic adenoma gives focal uptake with suppressed background. Destructive processes such as Hashimoto or subacute thyroiditis release hormone from damaged follicles and show low uptake rather than high uptake."}, {"id": 634, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 22-year-old woman has a soft fluctuant swelling at the anterior border of the upper sternocleidomastoid muscle that enlarged after an upper respiratory infection. Ultrasound shows an anechoic cyst, and CT shows a beak extending between the internal and external carotid arteries. What is the origin and typical histology of this lesion?", "options": {"A": "Incomplete obliteration of the cervical sinus, with squamous lining and lymphoid tissue in the wall", "B": "Remnant of the first pharyngeal cleft with a duct opening near the external auditory canal", "C": "Failure of lymphovenous connection producing multiloculated spaces with fluid levels in the posterior triangle", "D": "Persistent thyroglossal tract with ciliated respiratory lining located midline at the hyoid level"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Incomplete obliteration of the cervical sinus, with squamous lining and lymphoid tissue in the wall): Second branchial cleft cysts are the most common branchial anomaly and arise from the cervical sinus. They lie at the anterior sternocleidomastoid border, often with a beak between the carotid arteries, and the wall contains squamous epithelium with lymphoid tissue. First cleft anomalies sit near the parotid and ear canal, and thyroglossal cysts are midline."}, {"id": 635, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 3-month-old infant has a soft compressible posterior triangle neck mass that transilluminates. MRI shows a multiloculated lesion with fluid-fluid levels, T2 hyperintense septa, and enhancement confined to the septa. There is no flow void. Which pathological description is most accurate?", "options": {"A": "Proliferating immature capillaries expressing GLUT1, with growth in infancy and later involution", "B": "Thin-walled epithelial cyst with keratin debris and skin appendage structures in the wall", "C": "Slow-flow venous channels with phleboliths, thrombosis and incomplete smooth muscle in the vessel walls", "D": "Endothelium-lined lymphatic cysts without venous communication, with lymphoid aggregates in some walls"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Endothelium-lined lymphatic cysts without venous communication, with lymphoid aggregates in some walls): Lymphatic malformations are cystic, endothelium-lined, multiloculated lesions with fluid-fluid levels from internal haemorrhage and enhancement limited to the septa. Haemangiomas enhance avidly and are GLUT1 positive, venous malformations show phleboliths, and dermoid cysts contain fat and adnexal structures."}, {"id": 636, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 41-year-old woman has a painless pulsatile neck mass. CT angiography shows a hypervascular mass at the carotid bifurcation that splays the internal and external carotid arteries. MRI shows a salt-and-pepper appearance on T2. Which histological pattern and genetic association best explain this tumour?", "options": {"A": "Nests of chief cells in a vascular network, often linked to succinate dehydrogenase mutations", "B": "Sheets of small lymphocytes with germinal centres and sinus histiocytosis from reactive nodal enlargement", "C": "Spindle cells with palisading nuclei and Verocay bodies arising from the vagus nerve sheath", "D": "Calcitonin-secreting parafollicular cells with amyloid deposition and a germline RET activating mutation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Nests of chief cells in a vascular network, often linked to succinate dehydrogenase mutations): Carotid body tumours are paragangliomas with nests of chief cells, called Zellballen, surrounded by sustentacular cells and a rich capillary network, and many are linked to succinate dehydrogenase gene mutations. This explains the hypervascularity and flow voids. A vagal schwannoma displaces the carotids anteriorly rather than splaying them, and lymph node and thyroid tumours do not fit."}, {"id": 637, "domain": "neuropathology_and_head_and_neck", "question": "A 30-year-old man has chronic foul ear discharge and conductive hearing loss. CT shows a soft tissue mass in the epitympanum with blunting of the scutum and erosion of the ossicles. MRI shows restricted diffusion within the mass and no enhancement centrally. Which pathological process best explains the mass and the bone erosion?", "options": {"A": "Langerhans cell histiocytosis with grooved nuclei and eosinophils infiltrating the petrous bone", "B": "Keratinising squamous epithelium in the middle ear, shedding debris and driving bone resorption", "C": "Glomus tympanicum paraganglioma with vascular nests of chief cells and salt-and-pepper MRI appearance", "D": "Granulation tissue with cholesterol clefts from chronic haemorrhage, which is T1 hyperintense on MRI"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Keratinising squamous epithelium in the middle ear, shedding debris and driving bone resorption): Cholesteatoma is a collection of keratinising squamous epithelium in the middle ear that sheds keratin, and the matrix and inflammatory mediators resorb bone. Restricted diffusion without central enhancement is typical. Cholesterol granuloma is bright on T1, paraganglioma enhances avidly, and histiocytosis is rare and enhances."}, {"id": 647, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 27-year-old woman has drenching night sweats and a 12 percent weight loss over four months. Imaging and biopsy confirm classic Hodgkin lymphoma involving left cervical nodes, bulky mediastinal nodes and the spleen, with no liver or marrow involvement. Which Ann Arbor stage is correct?", "options": {"A": "Stage III with B symptoms, the spleen counted as nodal tissue and given the S suffix", "B": "Stage IV with B symptoms, because splenic involvement is disseminated extranodal organ disease needing separate classification", "C": "Stage III without B symptoms, because weight loss below fifteen percent does not count as systemic", "D": "Stage II with B symptoms, because the nodal regions affected are contiguous and exclude the spleen"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Stage III with B symptoms, the spleen counted as nodal tissue and given the S suffix): Disease above and below the diaphragm makes stage III, and the spleen is classified as lymphatic tissue, so splenic involvement is recorded with the S suffix rather than moving the patient to stage IV. Weight loss over ten percent in six months, with drenching sweats, adds the B designation. Stage IV needs diffuse or disseminated involvement of an extranodal organ such as liver or marrow. The Lugano classification retains this approach."}, {"id": 651, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 48-year-old man with BCR::ABL1 positive chronic myeloid leukaemia, treated for six years, develops fever, bone pain and increasing splenic size. Blood shows 35 percent blasts. MRI shows a new pelvic soft tissue mass and patchy marrow replacement. What is the best explanation for this change?", "options": {"A": "Large cell lymphoma transformation of an underlying small lymphocytic clone producing bulky extranodal disease and symptoms", "B": "Secondary haemophagocytic syndrome driven by cytokine excess, producing fever, splenomegaly and marrow infiltration by activated macrophages", "C": "Blast phase with additional genetic lesions, producing myeloid sarcoma and diffuse marrow replacement by immature cells", "D": "Accelerated phase marked by basophilia and thrombocytosis without excess blasts, with reticulin fibrosis explaining marrow signal"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Blast phase with additional genetic lesions, producing myeloid sarcoma and diffuse marrow replacement by immature cells): Blast counts of 20 percent or more in blood or marrow, or extramedullary blast proliferation such as myeloid sarcoma, define blast phase of chronic myeloid leukaemia. Blast phase is usually myeloid but about a quarter of cases are lymphoid. Accelerated phase is defined by lower blast percentages, basophilia, thrombocytosis and clonal evolution. Richter-type transformation belongs to chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, and haemophagocytic syndrome does not produce 35 percent circulating blasts."}, {"id": 655, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 3-year-old boy has polyuria and unilateral proptosis. Skull radiographs show several sharply defined lucent lesions with bevelled edges and no sclerotic rim. Biopsy of a lesion shows large cells with grooved, folded nuclei in a background of numerous eosinophils. Which finding would best confirm the diagnosis?", "options": {"A": "Touton giant cells with lipid-laden macrophages in a skin and bone lesion, negative for S100", "B": "S100 positivity with emperipolesis and abundant foamy histiocytes, but negative staining for CD1a", "C": "CD1a and langerin expression with Birbeck granules resembling tennis rackets on electron microscopy", "D": "CD163 positive macrophages engulfing blood cells, accompanied by fever, cytopenias and markedly elevated ferritin"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (CD1a and langerin expression with Birbeck granules resembling tennis rackets on electron microscopy): Lytic punched-out skull lesions, diabetes insipidus from pituitary stalk involvement and exophthalmos form the classic triad of multisystem Langerhans cell histiocytosis. The cells express CD1a, langerin and S100 and contain Birbeck granules. Rosai-Dorfman cells are CD1a negative, and juvenile xanthogranuloma cells are also CD1a negative. Haemophagocytic syndrome is a systemic macrophage activation state and does not form discrete bone lesions."}, {"id": 656, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 34-year-old man has an incidental well-circumscribed middle mediastinal mass with intense homogeneous enhancement and no systemic symptoms. Histology of the resected node shows small atrophic germinal centres, concentric lymphocyte layering around them and penetrating hyalinised small vessels. Which diagnosis best fits?", "options": {"A": "Multicentric Castleman disease with plasmacytosis driven by HHV-8 infection and excess interleukin-6 causing systemic symptoms", "B": "Type B thymoma with abundant lymphocytes and sparse epithelial cells forming a lobulated anterior mediastinal mass", "C": "Follicular lymphoma grade one, with packed neoplastic follicles that lose mantle zones and express BCL2", "D": "Unicentric Castleman disease of hyaline vascular type, usually cured by complete surgical removal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Unicentric Castleman disease of hyaline vascular type, usually cured by complete surgical removal): The hyaline vascular variant produces a solitary, avidly enhancing mass with small regressed germinal centres surrounded by concentric mantle layers, called onion skinning, and radial hyalinised vessels. Surgical excision is usually curative. The multicentric plasma cell type is systemic with fever and cytopenias. Follicular lymphoma loses mantle zones, and thymoma lies in the anterior compartment."}, {"id": 659, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 50-year-old man with diffuse large B cell lymphoma has an interim PET-CT after four cycles of chemotherapy. A residual mediastinal node shows uptake with an SUVmax approximately 1.5 times that of the liver. No new lesions are present and uptake has fallen compared with baseline. Which statement correctly applies the Lugano response criteria?", "options": {"A": "Deauville score 2, uptake equal to or below mediastinal blood pool, which indicates complete metabolic response", "B": "Deauville score 3, uptake above mediastinal blood pool but not above liver, indicating complete metabolic response", "C": "Deauville score 4 with reduced uptake from baseline, indicating a partial metabolic response at interim assessment", "D": "Deauville score 5, uptake markedly higher than liver, indicating absence of metabolic response to treatment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Deauville score 4 with reduced uptake from baseline, indicating a partial metabolic response at interim assessment): On the five point Deauville scale, a score of 4 means uptake moderately above liver, and 5 means markedly higher, usually two to three times liver uptake or new lesions. With reduced uptake from baseline at interim, score 4 or 5 is a partial metabolic response, whereas at end of treatment persistent 4 or 5 signals residual disease. Scores 1 to 3 are regarded as a complete metabolic response in the Lugano criteria."}, {"id": 664, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 60-year-old woman with dyspepsia has diffuse gastric wall thickening on CT with no adenopathy. Biopsy shows centrocyte-like cells infiltrating gastric glands to form lymphoepithelial lesions, and Helicobacter pylori is present. After successful eradication therapy the lymphoma persists. Which genetic finding best predicts failure to regress with eradication?", "options": {"A": "The t(14;18) placing BCL2 near the heavy chain locus, favouring survival of follicle centre lymphoid cells", "B": "The t(11;14) placing cyclin D1 near the heavy chain locus, favouring entry into the cell cycle", "C": "The t(8;14) placing MYC near the heavy chain locus, favouring rapid proliferation of high-grade cells", "D": "The t(11;18) producing API2::MALT1 fusion, which activates NF-kB signalling independent of antigen stimulation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (The t(11;18) producing API2::MALT1 fusion, which activates NF-kB signalling independent of antigen stimulation): Early gastric extranodal marginal zone lymphoma depends on antigen driven signalling from H. pylori and often regresses after eradication. Lymphomas carrying API2::MALT1 from t(11;18) have constitutive NF-kB activation, so they do not depend on bacterial stimulation and rarely regress. The other translocations belong to follicular, Burkitt and mantle cell lymphomas."}, {"id": 672, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 66-year-old man has splenomegaly and diffuse polyp-like thickening of the small bowel and colon, with enlarged mesenteric nodes on CT. Biopsy shows monotonous small to medium cells with irregular nuclei that coexpress CD5 and CD20 but not CD23. Which genetic event is the underlying driver of this lymphoma?", "options": {"A": "Activating BRAF V600E mutation in the neoplastic cells, causing constitutive signalling through the MAP kinase pathway", "B": "Deletion at chromosome 13q14 removing microRNA genes, the commonest lesion in chronic lymphocytic leukaemia", "C": "Translocation of CCND1 beside the immunoglobulin heavy chain enhancer, leading to cyclin D1 overexpression", "D": "Translocation of BCL2 beside the immunoglobulin heavy chain enhancer, causing resistance to programmed cell death"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Translocation of CCND1 beside the immunoglobulin heavy chain enhancer, leading to cyclin D1 overexpression): CD5 positive, CD23 negative small B cells with lymphomatous polyposis are typical of mantle cell lymphoma, which carries t(11;14) producing cyclin D1 overexpression. Chronic lymphocytic leukaemia is CD23 positive and often has 13q14 deletion. BCL2 translocation is characteristic of follicular lymphoma, and BRAF V600E marks hairy cell leukaemia."}, {"id": 673, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A male infant born at 29 weeks to a mother with poorly controlled gestational diabetes develops grunting and cyanosis within hours of birth. Chest radiography shows diffuse fine granular opacification with air bronchograms and low lung volumes. Which pathological change would be expected in the lungs at autopsy?", "options": {"A": "Neutrophilic exudate with fibrin and bacteria filling the airspaces in a segmental lobar distribution", "B": "Alveolar septal fibrosis with cystic airspace enlargement and squamous metaplasia of the bronchiolar epithelium", "C": "Meconium and squames within distal airspaces producing a patchy chemical pneumonitis with air trapping", "D": "Eosinophilic hyaline membranes lining collapsed terminal airspaces, with atelectasis and congested alveolar walls"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Eosinophilic hyaline membranes lining collapsed terminal airspaces, with atelectasis and congested alveolar walls): Surfactant deficiency in a preterm infant of a diabetic mother (insulin delays surfactant synthesis) causes diffuse atelectasis. Plasma proteins and necrotic type 2 pneumocyte debris form hyaline membranes on the airspace walls. Bronchopulmonary dysplasia is the later complication of ventilation and oxygen. Meconium aspiration affects term or post-term infants, and bacterial pneumonia would show neutrophils rather than a uniform granular pattern."}, {"id": 676, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 27-week infant has a sudden fall in haematocrit on day 2 of life. Cranial ultrasound shows an echogenic focus at the caudothalamic groove with blood filling a mildly dilated lateral ventricle. Which feature of the neonatal brain best explains the site of this haemorrhage?", "options": {"A": "A vascular subependymal layer with fragile thin-walled vessels and little supporting stroma", "B": "A choroid plexus layer with high capillary permeability that bleeds under raised central venous pressure", "C": "A watershed zone between the anterior and middle cerebral arteries that is vulnerable to systemic hypotension", "D": "A deep white matter region containing immature pre-oligodendrocytes that are sensitive to free radical injury"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (A vascular subependymal layer with fragile thin-walled vessels and little supporting stroma): The germinal matrix is a highly vascular, cellular layer that involutes by about 36 weeks and has thin-walled vessels with little supportive stroma. Fluctuations in cerebral blood flow and venous pressure in the sick premature infant make it bleed, and blood can rupture into the ventricle. Watershed injury and white matter injury are separate lesions."}, {"id": 677, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A former 28-week infant, now aged 2 years, has spastic diplegia with greater leg than arm involvement. Magnetic resonance imaging shows ventricular enlargement with irregular margins and reduced periventricular white matter volume posteriorly. Which earlier neuropathological lesion most likely produced this appearance?", "options": {"A": "Neuronal migration failure with a thickened smooth cortex and reduced gyral folding from impaired cell movement", "B": "Laminar cortical necrosis in the borderzone between the major arterial territories after term perinatal hypotension", "C": "Coagulative necrosis and gliosis of the deep white matter beside the lateral ventricles in neonates", "D": "Intraparenchymal venous infarction secondary to a large germinal matrix haemorrhage with a haemorrhagic cavity forming"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Coagulative necrosis and gliosis of the deep white matter beside the lateral ventricles in neonates): Periventricular leukomalacia follows hypoxia or infection in premature infants and affects the arterial end-zone white matter beside the ventricles, where pre-oligodendrocytes are vulnerable. Necrosis is followed by gliosis and volume loss, and the corticospinal fibres to the legs lie closest to the ventricles, producing spastic diplegia. Venous infarct and term watershed injury have different distributions and cell targets."}, {"id": 681, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 14-month-old boy has progressive abdominal distension over several weeks and is otherwise afebrile. The referral summary is shown. Computed tomography shows a large solitary hypervascular right hepatic lobe mass with scattered calcification, in an otherwise normal liver. Which underlying pathology is most likely?", "options": {"A": "A cavernous vascular tumour of infancy composed of endothelial lined spaces and fibrous septa", "B": "An embryonal liver tumour of fetal and embryonal epithelial cells, sometimes with mesenchymal elements", "C": "A mesenchymal hamartoma with cystic spaces, loose myxoid stroma and disorganised bile duct structures", "D": "A mature hepatocellular carcinoma arising in a cirrhotic liver after perinatal hepatitis B infection"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (An embryonal liver tumour of fetal and embryonal epithelial cells, sometimes with mesenchymal elements): Hepatoblastoma is the commonest primary liver malignancy of early childhood, linked to prematurity, Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome and familial adenomatous polyposis. It has epithelial fetal and embryonal patterns, with or without osteoid and cartilage, and it secretes alpha-fetoprotein. Hepatocellular carcinoma usually needs a diseased liver at this age, and the other options have different features and a lower marker rise.", "image": "question_images/q_681.png"}, {"id": 687, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 6-year-old recently arrived from a high-incidence country has fever, cough and enlarged hilar lymph nodes. Chest radiography shows a small peripheral right mid-zone opacity with ipsilateral hilar lymphadenopathy. What is the pathological name for this combination of a subpleural parenchymal focus and nodal disease?", "options": {"A": "Simon focus, formed by haematogenous seeding of the apex during a prior bacteraemic episode of infection", "B": "Rasmussen aneurysm, a dilated pulmonary artery branch within the wall of a tuberculous cavity", "C": "Assmann focus, a cavitating infraclavicular lesion arising from reactivation of dormant bacilli", "D": "Ghon complex, a subpleural caseating focus plus caseous involvement of draining hilar lymph nodes"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The correct answer is D (Ghon complex, a subpleural caseating focus plus caseous involvement of draining hilar lymph nodes): The Ghon focus is a subpleural caseating granuloma, usually in the lower part of the upper lobe or upper part of the lower lobe, and with caseating lymph node involvement it forms the Ghon complex. Calcification and healing are usual. The Simon focus, Assmann focus and Rasmussen aneurysm relate to later haematogenous, reactivation and cavitary events."}, {"id": 690, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 42-year-old with advanced HIV infection has headache and confusion. The cerebrospinal fluid results are shown. Magnetic resonance imaging shows multiple non-enhancing T2 hyperintense cystic spaces in the basal ganglia along the perivascular spaces. Which pathological explanation is most appropriate for these cystic lesions?", "options": {"A": "Perivascular spaces distended by encapsulated yeasts and mucoid capsule material, with minimal inflammation", "B": "Liquefactive necrosis with a fibrous capsule and ring enhancement around bacterial abscess cavities in the parenchyma", "C": "Parasitic cysts containing a scolex within a vesicular wall and surrounding oedema from degeneration of larvae", "D": "Granulomas with central caseation and a thick enhancing rim around mycobacterial infection"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Perivascular spaces distended by encapsulated yeasts and mucoid capsule material, with minimal inflammation): Cryptococcus neoformans, an encapsulated yeast, spreads through the subarachnoid space into Virchow-Robin spaces, where the mucoid capsule and organisms form gelatinous pseudocysts (soap bubble lesions) with a very weak inflammatory response in immunosuppression. Mucicarmine stains the capsule. Abscess, tuberculoma and cysticercosis each elicit enhancement or contain different organisms.", "image": "question_images/q_690.png"}, {"id": 691, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 33-year-old with HIV infection, not taking prophylaxis, has fever, hemiparesis and seizures. Magnetic resonance imaging shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions at the corticomedullary junction and in the basal ganglia, with surrounding vasogenic oedema. Which pathological feature accounts for the ring enhancement?", "options": {"A": "Hypercellular tumour with central necrosis, pseudopalisading and microvascular proliferation at the margin", "B": "Central liquefaction containing neutrophils and bacteria within a well-formed collagenous capsule and gliotic rim", "C": "Central necrosis with surrounding inflammation containing tachyzoites and a peripheral zone of encysted bradyzoites", "D": "Central caseous necrosis surrounded by epithelioid histiocytes and a rim of fibrous capsule in the parenchyma"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The correct answer is C (Central necrosis with surrounding inflammation containing tachyzoites and a peripheral zone of encysted bradyzoites): Reactivation of latent Toxoplasma gondii in the setting of low CD4 counts causes necrotising encephalitis. Lesions have a central necrotic zone, an inflamed zone with tachyzoites, and an outer zone with encysted bradyzoites. The resulting disruption of the blood-brain barrier produces ring enhancement. Pseudopalisading necrosis defines glioblastoma, and lymphoma is the other common differential diagnosis."}, {"id": 697, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 5-month-old is brought with irritability and limb swelling. A skeletal survey shows a thin, lucent fragment across the distal femoral metaphysis, parallel to the physis, with a subtle corner fragment. The history is of a fall from a sofa. Which pathological mechanism best explains the injury?", "options": {"A": "Defective collagen type I synthesis causing recurrent low energy fractures with osteopenia and blue sclerae throughout", "B": "Shearing forces from limb acceleration and deceleration through the immature primary spongiosa at the physis", "C": "Deficient osteoid mineralisation at the growth plate producing widened physes and a cupped metaphysis in infancy", "D": "Subperiosteal haemorrhage in vitamin C deficiency with thickened sclerotic metaphyseal margins and ground-glass bone"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The correct answer is B (Shearing forces from limb acceleration and deceleration through the immature primary spongiosa at the physis): Classic metaphyseal lesions (corner or bucket handle fractures) are specific for non-accidental injury in infants and arise from torsion and traction when a limb is shaken or yanked. The injury passes through the weakest area, the primary spongiosa, and a short fall from a sofa does not generate these forces. Rickets, scurvy and osteogenesis imperfecta have systemic features and different radiographic patterns."}, {"id": 700, "domain": "haematology_paediatric_and_infection", "question": "A 40-year-old man who grew up in the Nile delta has painless haematuria. Computed tomography shows a thickened bladder wall with curvilinear mural calcification and an irregular mass at the trigone. Which pathological sequence best explains the malignant risk in this setting?", "options": {"A": "Chronic inflammation around deposited eggs, leading to squamous metaplasia and later squamous cell carcinoma", "B": "Chronic inflammation with colonic polyp formation and later adenocarcinoma through the adenoma carcinoma sequence", "C": "Granulomatous periportal fibrosis with portal hypertension and later hepatocellular carcinoma in the cirrhotic liver", "D": "Chemical carcinogen exposure leading to urothelial dysplasia and later papillary urothelial carcinoma of the bladder"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The correct answer is A (Chronic inflammation around deposited eggs, leading to squamous metaplasia and later squamous cell carcinoma): Schistosoma haematobium eggs lodge in the bladder wall, provoking granulomatous inflammation, fibrosis and calcified eggs that line the wall. Chronic injury causes squamous metaplasia and squamous cell carcinoma, which is the usual tumour type in endemic regions. S. mansoni and japonicum affect the liver and bowel, and smoking-related urothelial carcinoma is the common tumour elsewhere."}];
